const TEACHING = {"guides":{"MOL-1":{"overview":"Water determines which molecules dissolve, which surfaces associate, and how biological macromolecules organize. Begin with electron distribution, then follow the consequences through hydrogen bonding, solubility, membranes and the logarithmic pH scale.","objectives":["Explain polarity from electronegativity and shape.","Identify hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors.","Predict how ionic, polar and nonpolar groups behave in water.","Explain amphipathic organization.","Convert changes in hydrogen-ion concentration into pH changes."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-1:guide-1","title":"Water as a biological solvent","body":["Water is the medium in which most cellular reactions occur. It dissolves many ions and polar solutes, participates in chemical reactions and provides the surroundings in which proteins and membranes acquire their organization. Calling water a solvent describes its ability to surround solute particles and disperse them.","Solubility depends on the balance between interactions being broken and formed. A molecule containing oxygen or nitrogen is not automatically highly soluble: the number and accessibility of polar groups must be considered alongside the size of its nonpolar region."],"keyPoints":["Ionic and polar groups can interact favorably with water.","A larger hydrocarbon region can oppose the effect of a single polar group."],"example":"Compare ethanol with a long-chain alcohol: both have one hydroxyl group, but the much larger nonpolar region of the latter makes water solubility lower.","pitfalls":["Do not classify solubility from the presence of just one oxygen atom."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":8}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-2","title":"Why water is polar","body":["Oxygen attracts shared electrons more strongly than hydrogen. Electron density therefore lies closer to oxygen, giving it a partial negative charge and leaving each hydrogen partially positive. The O–H bonds are polar covalent bonds rather than bonds between fully charged ions.","Water has a bent shape. Its bond dipoles do not cancel, so the whole molecule has a net dipole. Both electronegativity difference and geometry matter: a molecule can contain polar bonds but have little or no net dipole if those bond dipoles cancel by symmetry."],"keyPoints":["Oxygen is δ− and the hydrogen atoms are δ+.","Bent geometry permits an overall molecular dipole."],"example":"A question that changes water from bent to a hypothetical linear symmetric molecule is testing dipole cancellation.","pitfalls":["Polarity is not the same thing as a net electrical charge."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-3","title":"Covalent bonds and hydrogen bonds","body":["An O–H covalent bond involves sharing electrons within a water molecule. A hydrogen bond is an attraction between a suitably bonded hydrogen and an electron-rich acceptor in another group. Breaking a hydrogen bond between two water molecules does not split either water molecule into atoms.","Individual hydrogen bonds are weaker than covalent bonds, but many hydrogen bonds together can stabilize a biological structure. They are also directional. Their reversibility allows water molecules to exchange partners and helps biological structures remain dynamic."],"keyPoints":["Distinguish intramolecular covalent structure from intermolecular association.","Many weak interactions can have a substantial combined stabilizing effect."],"example":"DNA strands can separate while each strand retains its covalent sugar-phosphate backbone.","pitfalls":["A hydrogen bond does not create a new ordinary covalent O–H bond between water molecules."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-4","title":"Hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors","body":["A donor supplies a hydrogen covalently attached to an electronegative atom, commonly oxygen or nitrogen in biological examples. An acceptor supplies an available electron pair. A carbonyl oxygen can accept a hydrogen bond even though it has no hydrogen to donate.","A hydroxyl can commonly act as both donor and acceptor. A hydrocarbon C–H group is not treated as a usual strong donor in this course. The chemical state matters: protonation and electron delocalization can change whether a nitrogen has an available pair, so do not assume every N is an acceptor."],"keyPoints":["N–H and O–H are common donor groups.","Carbonyl oxygen is a common acceptor."],"example":"In an amide, the carbonyl oxygen accepts hydrogen bonds and an N–H can donate; the amide nitrogen is not a usual hydrogen-bond acceptor.","pitfalls":["A donor must contain an appropriate hydrogen; oxygen alone is not enough."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":11}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-5","title":"Hydration of ions and polar molecules","body":["Water molecules orient around an ion. Their partially negative oxygen atoms face a cation such as Na+, whereas their partially positive hydrogen atoms face an anion such as Cl−. These ion–dipole interactions help stabilize separated ions in solution.","Uncharged polar molecules dissolve by a different but related route: groups such as carbonyls and hydroxyls form hydrogen bonds with surrounding water. A molecule can therefore be hydrophilic without carrying a full ionic charge."],"keyPoints":["Cations attract the oxygen side of water.","Polar uncharged solutes can be hydrated through hydrogen bonding."],"example":"Urea can interact with water using its carbonyl and N–H groups despite having no overall charge.","pitfalls":["Do not call all water-solute interactions hydrogen bonds; hydration of an ion is usually described as ion–dipole attraction."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-6","title":"The hydrophobic effect","body":["Hydrocarbon-rich surfaces cannot replace water–water hydrogen-bonding interactions with equally favorable polar interactions. Water adjacent to these nonpolar surfaces becomes constrained. When nonpolar groups gather, the area exposed to water decreases and more water molecules can interact with bulk solvent.","This hydrophobic effect is central to membrane assembly and to the burial of many nonpolar side chains inside soluble proteins. It is a solvent-dependent association rather than a special covalent bond between hydrocarbons."],"keyPoints":["Nonpolar association reduces exposed nonpolar surface area.","The hydrophobic effect is important in both membranes and soluble proteins."],"example":"An oil droplet gathers into a larger drop rather than spreading uniformly through water.","pitfalls":["Hydrophobic molecules do not literally repel all water molecules through an electrical charge."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-7","title":"Amphipathic molecules and self-assembly","body":["An amphipathic molecule contains distinct hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions. A phospholipid has a polar head and nonpolar hydrocarbon tails. In water, arrangements that expose the head groups to water and shield tails from water are favored.","Depending on molecular shape and conditions, amphipathic molecules can form micelles or bilayers. A bilayer exposes polar surfaces on both sides while providing a nonpolar interior. This explains why a membrane is a barrier to many ions and polar solutes even though it is made from molecules with polar heads."],"keyPoints":["Amphipathic means possessing both kinds of region.","Bilayers place tails in an internal nonpolar region."],"example":"A phospholipid placed in water does not orient its tails outward simply because it contains a charged head.","pitfalls":["Amphipathic is not a synonym for completely water-soluble."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":14}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-8","title":"Water ionization and the meaning of pH","body":["Water can undergo reversible ionization. Hydrogen ions are often written H+ for convenience, although in water they are hydrated. The pH scale is defined by a negative base-10 logarithm of hydrogen-ion activity; introductory calculations approximate this using molar concentration.","Because of the minus sign, more H+ gives a lower pH. Pure water at 25°C has approximately 10⁻⁷ mol/L H+ and pH 7. Neutral pH means equal H+ and OH− activities, and the numerical pH of neutrality depends on temperature."],"keyPoints":["pH = −log10[H+] in the usual course approximation.","A tenfold increase in H+ lowers pH by one unit."],"example":"[H+] = 10⁻⁵ M corresponds to pH 5, which has one hundred times as much H+ as pH 7.","pitfalls":["Do not assume pH 7 is the neutral value at every temperature."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":15}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-9","title":"Acids, bases and conjugate partners","body":["A Brønsted acid donates a proton and a Brønsted base accepts a proton. After an acid loses H+, its remaining species is its conjugate base. These pairs differ by one proton rather than by an arbitrary change in the molecular structure.","Acid strength describes the tendency to donate H+, whereas acid concentration describes how much acid is present. A concentrated weak acid and a dilute strong acid cannot be compared just from the words strong and weak. Later buffer calculations combine concentration and dissociation behavior."],"keyPoints":["HA and A− form a conjugate pair.","Strength and concentration answer different questions."],"example":"For HA ⇌ H+ + A−, adding H+ tends to drive A− toward HA.","pitfalls":["A strong acid is not necessarily more concentrated than a weak acid."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":16}]},{"id":"MOL-1:guide-10","title":"Solving logarithmic pH questions","body":["Translate a pH difference into a power of ten. If pH falls by two units, hydrogen-ion concentration rises by 10² = 100-fold. If pH rises by three units, hydrogen-ion concentration falls by 10³ = 1000-fold.","Check the direction before calculating: a more acidic solution has more H+ and a lower pH. For two concentrations written a×10⁻ⁿ, pH = n − log10(a). Even without a calculator, concentration orders of magnitude usually identify the intended answer."],"keyPoints":["pH 6 has ten times the H+ of pH 7.","pH differences cannot be interpreted as simple arithmetic concentration differences."],"example":"A change from pH 8 to pH 6 is a hundredfold increase in H+, not a twofold increase.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse a decrease in pH with a decrease in hydrogen ions."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-1","page":21}]}],"summary":["Water has partial charges, not full ionic charges.","Hydrogen bonds are intermolecular attractions; O–H covalent bonds hold each water molecule together.","Hydrophobic association reflects unfavorable exposure of nonpolar surfaces to water.","pH changes are logarithmic."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-1:blank-0","prompt":"Water has a bent geometry and an overall molecular _____.","answers":["dipole"],"explanation":"Its polar O–H bonds do not cancel because the molecule is bent.","concept":"MOL-1:0"},{"id":"MOL-1:blank-1","prompt":"A carbonyl oxygen commonly acts as a hydrogen-bond _____.","answers":["acceptor"],"explanation":"Its available electron pairs can accept a hydrogen bond.","concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-1"},{"id":"MOL-1:blank-2","prompt":"A molecule with distinct hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions is _____.","answers":["amphipathic","amphiphilic"],"explanation":"Both regions are present in molecules such as phospholipids.","concept":"MOL-1:2"},{"id":"MOL-1:blank-3","prompt":"A fall from pH 7 to pH 5 raises [H+] by a factor of _____.","answers":["100","one hundred"],"explanation":"Two pH units correspond to 10 squared.","concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-5"},{"id":"MOL-1:blank-4","prompt":"After HA loses H+, A− is its conjugate _____.","answers":["base"],"explanation":"The conjugate pair differs by one proton.","concept":"MOL-1:slides-2026-4"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-1:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Polar covalent bond","definition":"Unequal sharing of electrons within a bond"},{"term":"Hydrogen bond","definition":"Attraction of a suitable bonded hydrogen to an electron-pair acceptor"},{"term":"Hydrophilic","definition":"Interacts favorably with water"},{"term":"Hydrophobic","definition":"Predominantly nonpolar and poorly water-soluble"},{"term":"Amphipathic","definition":"Has distinct polar and nonpolar regions"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-2":{"overview":"Nucleic acids store and express sequence information. Learn the chemical parts and bonds first, then use strand direction, base pairing and packaging to solve structural and quantitative questions.","objectives":["Distinguish bases, nucleosides and nucleotides.","Identify DNA/RNA sugars and bonds.","Write antiparallel complementary sequences.","Apply Chargaff relationships to double-stranded DNA.","Compare DNA conformations, denaturation and chromatin organization."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-2:guide-1","title":"Information flow and RNA functions","body":["DNA provides a durable sequence store. In gene expression, transcription produces RNA from a DNA template and translation uses the nucleotide sequence of mRNA to specify an amino-acid sequence. Information transfer from RNA to DNA is possible through reverse transcription; the simple diagram is therefore a useful framework rather than a claim that every biological transfer must be DNA→RNA.","RNA also performs structural, catalytic and regulatory roles. mRNA carries coding information, tRNA supplies amino acids to translation, and rRNA contributes to ribosome structure and catalysis."],"keyPoints":["Not every RNA is translated.","Transcription and translation involve different polymers and machinery."],"example":"An rRNA gene is transcribed but does not first need to become an mRNA encoding rRNA.","pitfalls":["The central dogma does not mean all RNA molecules encode proteins."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":3}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-2","title":"Bases, sugars and nucleosides","body":["A nucleotide contains a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar and phosphate. Adenine and guanine are purines with two fused rings. Cytosine, thymine and uracil are pyrimidines with one ring. In the standard comparison, DNA contains A, G, C and T; RNA contains A, G, C and U.","The base attaches to the sugar’s 1′ carbon through an N-glycosidic bond. The linkage involves N9 of a purine or N1 of a pyrimidine. Base plus sugar is a nucleoside; adding phosphate makes a nucleotide."],"keyPoints":["Sugar positions use primes; base positions do not.","Adenosine is a nucleoside; AMP is a nucleotide."],"example":"A molecule containing adenine and ribose but no phosphate is adenosine, not adenine alone and not AMP.","pitfalls":["A nucleotide does not require three phosphates; one is sufficient."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":6}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-3","title":"Ribose, deoxyribose and backbone chemistry","body":["Ribose has a 2′ hydroxyl group; 2′-deoxyribose has hydrogen at that position. Both can supply a 3′ hydroxyl that participates in chain extension. The extra 2′ hydroxyl contributes to RNA’s susceptibility to alkaline hydrolysis and to its capacity for varied folding.","A nucleic-acid chain uses 3′–5′ phosphodiester links: a phosphate connects the 3′ oxygen of one sugar to the 5′ oxygen of the next. This gives the polymer direction and leaves chemically distinct ends."],"keyPoints":["The 2′ group distinguishes the usual DNA and RNA sugars.","The 3′ hydroxyl is the reactive group used for nucleotide-chain extension."],"example":"Removing the 3′ hydroxyl from an incorporated analogue can stop extension even if a base and a 5′ phosphate are present.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse 2′-deoxy with absence of a 3′ hydroxyl."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":7}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-4","title":"Phosphate bond vocabulary","body":["The first phosphate is connected to a sugar through a phosphoester bond. A phosphate bridging two sugar groups makes a phosphodiester linkage. By contrast, adjacent phosphate groups in ATP are joined by phosphoanhydride bonds. These names identify different atoms and groups being connected.","During polymer synthesis, a nucleotide triphosphate supplies a nucleotide unit and pyrophosphate is released. The chain contains nucleoside monophosphate residues, not intact triphosphates at every internal position."],"keyPoints":["Phosphodiester links define the polymer backbone.","Phosphoanhydrides connect phosphate groups to each other."],"example":"ATP has one sugar–phosphate phosphoester linkage and phosphate–phosphate phosphoanhydride linkages.","pitfalls":["Do not call the base–sugar bond phosphodiester; it is N-glycosidic."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-5","title":"Base pairing and antiparallel strands","body":["A pairs with T in ordinary double-stranded DNA, while G pairs with C. The conventional pairs fit the helix geometry by pairing a purine with a pyrimidine. A–T has two hydrogen bonds and G–C has three, although stacking interactions also contribute importantly to duplex stability.","The two strands run in opposite chemical directions. If one is written 5′→3′, the aligned complement is written 3′→5′. To report that complementary strand in the usual 5′→3′ convention, reverse the aligned sequence."],"keyPoints":["Complementarity concerns base identity.","Antiparallel concerns strand direction."],"example":"5′-AGTC-3′ pairs with 3′-TCAG-5′; the latter written conventionally is 5′-GACT-3′.","pitfalls":["A correct list of complementary bases is not enough if the direction is wrong."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":17}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-6","title":"Chargaff calculations","body":["For a complete double-stranded DNA sample using standard base pairing, the number of adenines equals thymines and guanines equals cytosines. Total A+T+G+C is 100%, allowing one measured percentage to determine all four.","The relationship applies to the combined double-stranded sample, not necessarily to an isolated single strand. A single strand can contain very different percentages of A and T; its complementary strand supplies the balancing bases."],"keyPoints":["If G=10%, C=10%, leaving 80% for A+T.","A=T=40% in that example."],"example":"A duplex with 60% G+C contains 30% G, 30% C, 20% A and 20% T.","pitfalls":["Do not apply A=T as an unconditional rule to any single-stranded nucleic acid."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":23}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-7","title":"Helical forms and grooves","body":["B-DNA is the usual reference helix under physiological hydrated conditions. It is right-handed and has major and minor grooves that provide access to base-specific chemical patterns. A-DNA is also right-handed and is favored by different hydration conditions; RNA duplexes commonly have A-like geometry.","Z-DNA is left-handed, with a characteristic zigzag sugar–phosphate backbone. Sequence and conditions influence its occurrence. Questions often ask the handedness distinction rather than asking you to assume every cellular DNA region is permanently one uniform conformation."],"keyPoints":["A and B are right-handed.","Z is left-handed."],"example":"A left-handed helix with a zigzag backbone is identified as Z-DNA.","pitfalls":["Do not infer that RNA cannot form double-helical regions because it is usually described as single-stranded."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":24}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-8","title":"Denaturation, melting and absorbance","body":["DNA denaturation separates strands by disrupting interactions that stabilize the duplex. It does not ordinarily cut the covalent phosphodiester backbone. Heat or altered chemical conditions can favor strand separation, and complementary strands may reanneal under suitable conditions.","Unstacking and separation increase absorbance around 260 nm, called the hyperchromic effect. Tm is the temperature at which half of the DNA is denatured under stated conditions. More GC generally raises Tm when length and other conditions are comparable; salt, mismatches and sequence context also matter."],"keyPoints":["Hyperchromicity is an increase in A260 during denaturation.","Tm comparisons require comparable experimental conditions."],"example":"A mismatch-rich probe-target duplex generally melts at a lower temperature than a fully complementary one under the same conditions.","pitfalls":["Higher GC content does not override every possible difference in length or salt."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":26}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-9","title":"Nucleosomes and chromatin","body":["In eukaryotic chromatin, DNA wraps around a histone octamer containing two copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4. Histones are rich in basic residues that interact with the negatively charged DNA backbone. H1 associates with linker DNA and helps organize chromatin beyond the core nucleosome.","Packaging is regulated rather than purely passive storage. The accessibility of DNA to transcription machinery depends on nucleosome organization and associated chemical modifications. The traditional higher-order diagrams are models of packaging; the key examination distinction is core histones versus linker histone."],"keyPoints":["The octamer contains eight histone molecules.","H1 is not one of the eight core molecules."],"example":"A question asking which histone binds linker DNA is testing H1, not H3.","pitfalls":["A nucleosome core is not an octamer of eight different histone types."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":28}]},{"id":"MOL-2:guide-10","title":"Bacterial DNA and plasmids","body":["Many bacteria have a circular double-stranded chromosome in the nucleoid, although bacterial chromosome organization has exceptions. Plasmids are independently replicating DNA molecules that can carry useful traits such as antibiotic resistance. They are distinct from the main chromosome even when both are circular DNA.","A cloning plasmid can carry a gene of interest, replication machinery recognized by the host and a selectable marker. Human insulin production illustrates how an expressed coding sequence can be carried into bacteria. A eukaryotic genomic gene containing introns generally cannot simply be assumed to be correctly processed by bacteria."],"keyPoints":["Plasmids have their own replication origin.","A selectable marker helps identify cells carrying the construct."],"example":"A cDNA-derived insulin coding sequence avoids dependence on human pre-mRNA splicing in the bacterial host.","pitfalls":["Plasmids are not the same thing as small ribosomes."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-2","page":30}]}],"summary":["Nucleoside = base + sugar; nucleotide adds phosphate.","DNA strands are antiparallel and complementary.","Sequences are written 5′→3′ unless explicitly labeled otherwise.","Histone octamers and H1 have different positions."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-2:blank-0","prompt":"A nucleoside plus phosphate is a _____.","answers":["nucleotide"],"explanation":"A nucleoside contains only base and sugar.","concept":"MOL-2:0"},{"id":"MOL-2:blank-1","prompt":"The base pairs with the sugar at its _____ carbon.","answers":["1′","1 prime","1","1'"],"explanation":"The N-glycosidic link uses sugar C1′.","concept":"MOL-2:0"},{"id":"MOL-2:blank-2","prompt":"The two strands in a DNA duplex run in _____ directions.","answers":["antiparallel","opposite"],"explanation":"One runs 5′→3′ while its paired partner runs 3′→5′.","concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-2"},{"id":"MOL-2:blank-3","prompt":"If a duplex is 20% G, it is _____% A.","answers":["30"],"explanation":"C is also 20%, leaving 60% equally for A and T.","concept":"MOL-2:slides-2026-3"},{"id":"MOL-2:blank-4","prompt":"The linker histone is _____.","answers":["H1","histone H1"],"explanation":"H1 binds linker regions rather than forming part of the core octamer.","concept":"MOL-2:2"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-2:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Nucleoside","definition":"Base joined to sugar without phosphate"},{"term":"Nucleotide","definition":"Base and sugar with one or more phosphates"},{"term":"Phosphodiester","definition":"Backbone linkage joining adjacent sugar units"},{"term":"Histone octamer","definition":"Core with two copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4"},{"term":"H1","definition":"Histone associated with linker DNA"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-3":{"overview":"Replication copies both DNA strands while preserving sequence fidelity. Link every enzyme to its substrate, direction and stage, then compare repair mechanisms by the kind of damage they recognize.","objectives":["Trace semiconservative bidirectional replication.","Explain primer dependence and leading/lagging synthesis.","Assign helicase, topoisomerase, polymerase and ligase roles.","Distinguish proofreading from repair.","Explain telomere maintenance and chain termination."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-3:guide-1","title":"Semiconservative replication and origins","body":["Semiconservative replication produces daughter duplexes containing one parental strand and one new strand. Each parental strand supplies the information needed to construct its complement. Bidirectional replication means forks move away from an origin in opposite directions.","Eukaryotic chromosomes are long and use multiple origins so replication can finish within S phase. An origin is a place where replication begins, not a DNA end or a start codon. The two forks from one origin create two replication directions but every individual strand is still synthesized 5′→3′."],"keyPoints":["Each daughter duplex retains one old strand.","Multiple origins improve the speed of copying a long chromosome."],"example":"Two replication forks can move in opposite directions without requiring a 3′→5′ DNA-synthesizing polymerase.","pitfalls":["Bidirectional replication does not mean a polymerase extends both ends of one strand."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-2","title":"Opening and stabilizing the fork","body":["Helicase separates the parental strands, making template DNA accessible. Single-strand-binding proteins stabilize the exposed single strands and limit reannealing or secondary structure. Opening the helix also creates torsional stress ahead of the fork.","Topoisomerases relieve that stress through transient strand cleavage and resealing. Type I topoisomerases usually make a single-strand break; type II topoisomerases make a double-strand break and pass another DNA segment through. These are controlled enzyme intermediates, not uncontrolled destruction of DNA."],"keyPoints":["Helicase separates paired strands.","Topoisomerase changes DNA topology ahead of or around the fork."],"example":"A drug that prevents topoisomerase resealing can turn a normal transient break into damaging persistent DNA cleavage.","pitfalls":["Ligase is not the enzyme that initially opens the double helix."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":8}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-3","title":"Primer dependence and chain extension","body":["DNA polymerase requires a template and an existing primer with an available 3′ hydroxyl. That hydroxyl attacks the incoming nucleotide triphosphate, extending the chain at its 3′ end. Thus the newly synthesized strand grows 5′→3′ while the template is read 3′→5′.","Primase supplies RNA primers. RNA polymerases can initiate a chain without a pre-existing primer, which explains why primase can provide something that the replicative DNA polymerase cannot make for itself."],"keyPoints":["Incoming substrates are deoxyribonucleotide triphosphates.","The new bond is made at the primer’s 3′ end."],"example":"If a chain ends in a nucleotide lacking 3′-OH, a normal next nucleotide cannot be attached through the usual reaction.","pitfalls":["Template dependence and primer dependence are separate requirements."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-4","title":"Leading and lagging strands","body":["Because DNA strands are antiparallel, the same 5′→3′ synthesis chemistry produces different patterns at a replication fork. The leading strand is synthesized continuously in the direction of fork movement, whereas the lagging strand is synthesized as Okazaki fragments in the opposite overall orientation.","Each lagging fragment needs a primer. A lagging fragment is still extended 5′→3′. The apparent conflict disappears once the direction of the fork and the direction along one fragment are distinguished."],"keyPoints":["Leading strand needs fewer initiation events at one fork.","Lagging strand needs repeated priming."],"example":"On a diagram, label template 3′ and 5′ ends before identifying leading versus lagging.","pitfalls":["Okazaki fragments are not made in the 3′→5′ direction."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-5","title":"Primer removal and ligation","body":["RNA primers must be removed and replaced with DNA. The detailed enzymes differ between bacteria and eukaryotes, but the overall requirement is the same: replace RNA-containing segments and create a continuous DNA backbone.","DNA ligase seals a nick between adjacent DNA fragments by forming the missing phosphodiester linkage. It does not fill a large gap with new nucleotides. Polymerase fills the gap; ligase closes the remaining nick once appropriate ends are adjacent."],"keyPoints":["Gap filling and nick sealing are distinct tasks.","Final DNA should not retain the ordinary replication primers."],"example":"A strand with every nucleotide present but one missing backbone bond is a ligase problem, not a need for helicase.","pitfalls":["Do not interchange primase, polymerase and ligase just because all act near a fork."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":14}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-6","title":"Proofreading and replication fidelity","body":["Many DNA polymerases can inspect the most recently added nucleotide. An incorrectly paired terminal nucleotide can be removed by 3′→5′ exonuclease proofreading, after which synthesis resumes 5′→3′. The proofreading direction describes removal from the growing end, not the direction of synthesis.","Fidelity also depends on initial nucleotide selection and postreplication repair. Proofreading does not find every mistake, so mismatch repair provides a further layer after replication."],"keyPoints":["Synthesis: 5′→3′.","Proofreading removal: 3′→5′."],"example":"If an enzyme loses proofreading while retaining polymerization, copying can continue but mutation frequency rises.","pitfalls":["A proofreading exonuclease is not a separate 3′→5′ synthesis pathway."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":18}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-7","title":"Telomeres and the end-replication problem","body":["Removing a terminal RNA primer leaves an end that ordinary DNA polymerase cannot fully replace when no upstream primer 3′-OH is available. Linear chromosomes therefore face an end-replication problem. Telomeres provide repetitive terminal DNA and associated protective proteins.","Telomerase extends a chromosome end using its own RNA as a template. Its catalytic component is a reverse transcriptase because it synthesizes DNA from RNA information. It creates additional template length that permits complementary-strand synthesis; it does not simply glue two chromosome ends together."],"keyPoints":["Telomerase contains RNA and a protein reverse-transcriptase component.","Telomeres are terminal repeats rather than protein-coding start signals."],"example":"A mutation destroying the telomerase RNA template impairs its ability to add the proper terminal DNA repeats.","pitfalls":["Telomerase does not replace every DNA polymerase at ordinary replication forks."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":22}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-8","title":"Mismatch repair and base excision repair","body":["Mismatch repair corrects mispaired bases that escape the replication machinery. It must identify the newly made strand and remove a segment containing the error, allowing DNA polymerase to copy the correct parental information.","Base excision repair handles smaller chemically altered bases. A DNA glycosylase removes the damaged base, creating an abasic site. Further enzymes cut and process the backbone, polymerase supplies the missing nucleotide and ligase seals the repair."],"keyPoints":["Mismatch repair addresses copying errors.","Base excision starts by removing a damaged base."],"example":"Deamination of cytosine produces uracil in DNA; removal of that inappropriate base is a classic base-excision task.","pitfalls":["A mismatch is not necessarily a bulky helix-distorting lesion."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":30}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-9","title":"Nucleotide excision and double-strand repair","body":["Nucleotide excision repair removes an oligonucleotide segment containing a bulky helix-distorting lesion, such as UV-induced pyrimidine photoproducts. The undamaged strand supplies the template for replacement synthesis. Defects in this protection can cause marked UV sensitivity.","Double-strand breaks require another strategy because both backbones are broken. Homologous recombination can use a related intact DNA sequence as a template; nonhomologous end joining reconnects ends and may introduce sequence changes. The availability of a sister chromatid influences repair options."],"keyPoints":["NER removes a patch around bulky damage.","A double-strand break is not corrected by simply swapping one damaged base."],"example":"A UV lesion and a clean double-strand break should not be assigned the same repair pathway merely because both are DNA damage.","pitfalls":["Do not call nucleotide excision repair the universal repair mechanism."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":33}]},{"id":"MOL-3:guide-10","title":"Replication inhibitors and experimental reasoning","body":["A nucleoside analogue may be activated to a nucleotide and compete with normal substrates. If its incorporation prevents further extension, it acts as a chain terminator. The critical feature in the usual teaching example is loss of an available 3′ hydroxyl.","Other agents target topoisomerases, nucleotide supply or polymerase function. In a mechanism question, first identify whether the drug blocks DNA opening, substrate generation, nucleotide addition or sealing. Avoid reasoning only from the name of a disease treated by the drug."],"keyPoints":["Drug effects can be understood from the blocked molecular step.","An analogue is not automatically a chain terminator; its chemistry and recognition matter."],"example":"A 2′-deoxy sugar still permits ordinary DNA synthesis, while a dideoxy sugar lacks the needed 3′ hydroxyl.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse a normal DNA nucleotide with a chain-terminating dideoxynucleotide."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-3","page":39}]}],"summary":["All new DNA is extended 5′→3′.","Leading and lagging describe continuity relative to the fork.","A primer supplies the initial free 3′ hydroxyl.","Proofreading and mismatch repair occur at different stages."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-3:blank-0","prompt":"DNA polymerase extends the growing strand at its _____ end.","answers":["3′","3 prime","3","3'"],"explanation":"Extension uses the terminal 3′ hydroxyl.","concept":"MOL-3:0"},{"id":"MOL-3:blank-1","prompt":"The enzyme that supplies RNA primers is _____.","answers":["primase"],"explanation":"The replicative polymerase cannot start a new strand without a primer.","concept":"MOL-3:2"},{"id":"MOL-3:blank-2","prompt":"Lagging-strand segments are called _____ fragments.","answers":["Okazaki"],"explanation":"Repeated primers allow discontinuous 5′→3′ synthesis.","concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-3"},{"id":"MOL-3:blank-3","prompt":"Proofreading removes a terminal error using _____ exonuclease activity.","answers":["3′ to 5′","3 to 5","3′→5′","3->5"],"explanation":"Removal starts at the growing 3′ end and differs from synthesis direction.","concept":"MOL-3:1"},{"id":"MOL-3:blank-4","prompt":"The RNA-containing enzyme that extends telomeres is _____.","answers":["telomerase"],"explanation":"Its reverse-transcriptase component copies an internal RNA template.","concept":"MOL-3:slides-2026-4"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-3:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Helicase","definition":"Separates the parental DNA strands"},{"term":"Primase","definition":"Makes starting RNA primers"},{"term":"DNA polymerase","definition":"Extends a primed DNA chain"},{"term":"Ligase","definition":"Seals an adjacent backbone nick"},{"term":"Telomerase","definition":"Extends terminal DNA repeats using an internal RNA template"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-4":{"overview":"Transcription transfers information from one DNA strand into RNA. Focus on RNA chemistry, the different RNA polymerases, promoter recognition and sequence direction before comparing bacterial and eukaryotic transcription.","objectives":["Distinguish major RNA classes and polymerases.","Trace initiation, elongation and termination.","Identify promoter and enhancer functions.","Convert template DNA into a correctly oriented RNA sequence.","Compare bacterial and eukaryotic transcription and inhibitor targets."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-4:guide-1","title":"RNA structure and functional classes","body":["RNA contains ribose and usually uracil rather than thymine. It is commonly single-stranded but can form extensive internal base-paired structures. This permits compact tRNA folds, ribosomal RNA structures and catalytic RNAs.","mRNA supplies the translated codon sequence. tRNA is an adaptor linking an anticodon to an amino acid. rRNA provides ribosomal structure and key catalytic functions. Regulatory small RNAs can influence the abundance or translation of other RNAs, so RNA function extends well beyond carrying a protein recipe."],"keyPoints":["RNA can form stems and loops.","Only a subset of RNAs are translated."],"example":"A molecule with an anticodon and a 3′ amino-acid attachment site is tRNA rather than mRNA.","pitfalls":["Single-stranded does not mean incapable of hydrogen bonding within the molecule."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-2","title":"RNA chemistry and alkaline instability","body":["The 2′ hydroxyl distinguishes ribose from the usual sugar in DNA. Under alkaline conditions it can participate in cleavage of the RNA phosphodiester backbone. DNA lacks that 2′-OH and is more resistant to this particular reaction.","This is a chemical stability comparison rather than a claim that DNA is indestructible. DNA can still be damaged or hydrolyzed by other conditions and enzymes. In an examination question, identify whether the distinguishing structural group is at 2′ or at the chain-extending 3′ position."],"keyPoints":["RNA has a 2′ hydroxyl.","Both RNA and DNA normally use a 3′ hydroxyl for chain extension."],"example":"If RNA and DNA samples are exposed to alkali, RNA is preferentially susceptible to backbone cleavage from its 2′-OH chemistry.","pitfalls":["Do not attribute alkaline RNA instability to uracil instead of the ribose group."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":5}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-3","title":"RNA polymerases and their products","body":["Eukaryotic nuclear RNA polymerase I makes the large rRNA precursor that supplies most ribosomal RNAs. Polymerase II makes protein-coding pre-mRNAs and several other RNA products. Polymerase III makes tRNAs, 5S rRNA and selected small RNAs. The 5S exception is a frequent test of polymerase specificity.","Bacteria use a single main RNA polymerase for their RNA classes. Different promoters and associated factors direct it to different transcription units. Do not confuse the Roman numerals of eukaryotic RNA polymerases with those used for DNA polymerases."],"keyPoints":["Pol I: major rRNA precursor.","Pol II: most protein-coding nuclear transcripts.","Pol III: tRNA and 5S rRNA."],"example":"A defect selectively reducing 5S rRNA transcription points toward polymerase III rather than polymerase I.","pitfalls":["The shorthand “Pol I makes rRNA” has the important 5S exception."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-4","title":"Stages and substrates of transcription","body":["Initiation establishes the transcription complex at the appropriate DNA region. Elongation adds ribonucleotides to the RNA 3′ end while the polymerase reads the template 3′→5′. Termination releases or completes the transcript through mechanisms that differ across polymerases and organisms.","RNA polymerase uses ATP, GTP, CTP and UTP as substrates. It can begin synthesis without an existing primer. The RNA is complementary to only the strand used as template for that gene; nearby genes can use opposite strands as their templates."],"keyPoints":["RNA grows 5′→3′.","A transcription bubble exposes only a local DNA segment."],"example":"A gene transcribed in the opposite genomic direction can use the other DNA strand as its template.","pitfalls":["There is not one chromosome-wide template strand used for every gene."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-5","title":"Promoters and transcription numbering","body":["A promoter is a DNA region where transcription machinery assembles to position initiation. Bases upstream of the first transcribed nucleotide are conventionally assigned negative positions, whereas the first transcribed base is +1. The promoter and the translation start codon therefore refer to different stages.","Core and promoter-proximal sequence elements help recruit factors. A TATA box occurs in some promoters, and CAAT or GC elements can contribute to regulation. These are common examples rather than requirements that every eukaryotic promoter must contain every box."],"keyPoints":["+1 is the transcription start site.","Upstream positions lie before +1 in the transcriptional direction."],"example":"A TATA element near −25 is upstream of the RNA’s first transcribed base; it is not the AUG used by a ribosome.","pitfalls":["Do not treat a consensus sequence as an invariant sequence in all genes."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":16}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-6","title":"General factors and enhancer-driven control","body":["For many polymerase II genes, general transcription factors assemble with polymerase at a core promoter. They help position and activate the machinery that opens DNA and begins RNA synthesis. Gene-specific activators provide an additional layer of regulation.","Enhancers are cis-acting DNA elements bound by regulatory proteins. DNA looping can allow a distant enhancer-bound activator to influence the promoter. Enhancers can act at variable distances and orientations; they are DNA sequences, whereas the activators that bind them are proteins."],"keyPoints":["Promoter recruitment and enhancer regulation cooperate.","An enhancer is DNA; an activator is a trans-acting product."],"example":"A mutation in an enhancer can reduce transcription despite an intact polymerase and protein-coding sequence.","pitfalls":["A distal enhancer is not itself an enzyme that travels to every chromosome."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":21}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-7","title":"Template versus coding strand","body":["The template strand is read by RNA polymerase. The RNA sequence is complementary and antiparallel to it. The coding strand has the same sequence as the RNA when both are written 5′→3′, except DNA uses T and RNA uses U.","Always copy the orientation labels before solving a sequence problem. A template written 5′→3′ requires reverse-complementing to report the RNA 5′→3′. If the template is already written 3′→5′, simply place the RNA complement below it in the opposite direction."],"keyPoints":["Coding strand matches RNA with T→U.","Template strand is complementary to RNA."],"example":"Coding 5′-ATGCGTAC-3′ gives RNA 5′-AUGCGUAC-3′. Template 5′-TAGC-3′ gives RNA 5′-GCUA-3′.","pitfalls":["An unlabeled TAGC sequence has ambiguous orientation; the question must specify the strand and direction."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":19}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-8","title":"Bacterial RNA polymerase and sigma factor","body":["Bacterial RNA polymerase core enzyme performs RNA synthesis. A sigma factor associates with the core to help recognize promoter sequences and initiate transcription at appropriate genes. The complete initiation form is often called the holoenzyme.","After initiation, the polymerase can elongate while promoter-recognition requirements change. Distinguish the factor that chooses the start from the catalytic machinery that adds ribonucleotides. The course’s bacterial promoter examples and eukaryotic promoter examples belong to different recognition systems."],"keyPoints":["Sigma helps bacterial promoter recognition.","The core enzyme performs RNA polymerization."],"example":"Removing sigma impairs efficient promoter-specific initiation even if the core retains catalytic activity.","pitfalls":["Bacterial sigma factor is not the same as a eukaryotic histone."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":27}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-9","title":"Polycistronic messages and coupled expression","body":["Many bacterial transcripts are polycistronic: one mRNA contains multiple coding regions that can yield separate proteins. This supports coordinated expression of functionally related genes. Most ordinary eukaryotic nuclear mRNAs are described as monocistronic in the course framework.","Because bacteria lack a nuclear envelope separating DNA from ribosomes, translation can begin on a nascent mRNA while transcription is still proceeding. Eukaryotic nuclear transcripts generally undergo processing and export before cytoplasmic translation."],"keyPoints":["One polycistronic RNA can produce several different proteins.","Coupling is possible because transcription and translation share the bacterial compartment."],"example":"A ribosome translating the first coding region while RNA polymerase makes downstream RNA is a bacterial-style coupled arrangement.","pitfalls":["Polycistronic does not mean one long fusion protein must be made."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":25}]},{"id":"MOL-4:guide-10","title":"Transcription inhibitors and source clarification","body":["Rifampin inhibits bacterial RNA polymerase and is used in tuberculosis treatment. Alpha-amanitin strongly inhibits eukaryotic RNA polymerase II; sensitivities of other eukaryotic polymerases differ. Actinomycin D binds DNA and interferes with transcription, so its mechanism is not promoter recognition by sigma factor.","Drug questions usually test the molecular target. Match organism and polymerase before deciding which RNA products are most affected. These teaching examples explain mechanisms; they are not instructions for medication use."],"keyPoints":["Rifampin: bacterial RNA polymerase.","Alpha-amanitin: strongest classic effect on Pol II."],"example":"A reduction of most protein-coding nuclear transcripts after a mushroom toxin fits polymerase II inhibition.","pitfalls":["Do not assign rifampin’s target to the eukaryotic ribosome."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-4","page":29}]}],"summary":["RNA is synthesized 5′→3′ without a pre-existing primer.","RNA matches the coding strand except U replaces T.","Polymerase II makes most protein-coding nuclear pre-mRNAs.","Bacterial sigma factor assists promoter recognition."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-4:blank-0","prompt":"Most protein-coding nuclear pre-mRNAs are made by RNA polymerase _____.","answers":["II","2"],"explanation":"Pol II is the major protein-coding nuclear transcription enzyme.","concept":"MOL-4:0"},{"id":"MOL-4:blank-1","prompt":"RNA polymerase reads template DNA in the _____ direction.","answers":["3′ to 5′","3 to 5","3′→5′","3->5"],"explanation":"The RNA itself grows 5′→3′.","concept":"MOL-4:2"},{"id":"MOL-4:blank-2","prompt":"The RNA matches the _____ DNA strand except that U replaces T.","answers":["coding","sense","non-template"],"explanation":"Both are compared in the 5′→3′ direction.","concept":"MOL-4:2"},{"id":"MOL-4:blank-3","prompt":"Bacterial promoter recognition is assisted by a _____ factor.","answers":["sigma","σ"],"explanation":"Sigma joins core polymerase to support promoter-specific initiation.","concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-2"},{"id":"MOL-4:blank-4","prompt":"Rifampin inhibits bacterial RNA _____.","answers":["polymerase"],"explanation":"Its defining transcription target is bacterial RNA polymerase.","concept":"MOL-4:slides-2026-5"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-4:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"RNA polymerase I","definition":"Makes the precursor of most large ribosomal RNAs"},{"term":"RNA polymerase II","definition":"Makes most protein-coding nuclear pre-mRNAs"},{"term":"RNA polymerase III","definition":"Makes tRNAs and 5S rRNA"},{"term":"Sigma factor","definition":"Assists promoter recognition in bacteria"},{"term":"Coding strand","definition":"Matches the RNA sequence except T replaces U"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-5":{"overview":"Eukaryotic pre-mRNA becomes a mature message through end processing, intron removal and quality control. Alternative processing and RNA editing create diversity, while processing errors can change proteins even when a mutation looks silent in the coding sequence.","objectives":["Explain capping, cleavage/polyadenylation and splicing.","Trace the two splicing reactions and lariat formation.","Distinguish alternative splicing from RNA editing.","Relate processing defects to β-thalassemia and progeria.","Compare mammalian editing with kinetoplastid guide-RNA editing."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-5:guide-1","title":"Pre-mRNA, hnRNA and mature mRNA","body":["The initial product of a protein-coding nuclear gene is pre-mRNA containing exons, introns and untranslated regions. Heterogeneous nuclear RNA is a broader historical term for diverse nuclear transcripts and processing intermediates. Mature mRNA must have appropriate ends and exon connections before efficient export and translation.","Exon means a sequence retained in a processed RNA. It is not an exact synonym for protein-coding sequence because 5′ and 3′ untranslated regions can also be exonic. Introns are removed from that transcript during splicing."],"keyPoints":["Both introns and exons are initially transcribed.","Untranslated regions can remain in mature mRNA."],"example":"A mutation in an exonic 5′ UTR can affect translation without changing any encoded amino acid.","pitfalls":["Do not state that every exon is entirely translated into protein."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":3}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-2","title":"The 5′ cap and its unusual linkage","body":["The cap is a modified guanosine, usually 7-methylguanosine, joined to the RNA through an unusual 5′–5′ triphosphate linkage. It is added early while transcription is still in progress. Its structure distinguishes a mature cellular message from an exposed ordinary 5′ end.","The cap helps protect against degradation and participates in nuclear export and translation initiation. Cap recognition is therefore connected to both RNA stability and protein production. It does not supply the first amino acid or determine the entire reading frame by itself."],"keyPoints":["Cap: 7-methylguanosine at the RNA 5′ end.","The linkage is 5′–5′, unlike the ordinary backbone."],"example":"A defect in cap-binding initiation factors can reduce translation despite an intact coding region.","pitfalls":["The cap is not a short poly-A sequence added to the 5′ end."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":8}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-3","title":"Cleavage and polyadenylation","body":["The RNA is cleaved downstream of a polyadenylation signal, after which poly-A polymerase adds adenosine residues to the new 3′ end. The tail is not simply copied as a long run of template thymidines during transcription. Signal recognition, cleavage and tail addition are related but distinct steps.","Poly-A-binding proteins help organize the tail and influence stability and translation. Most typical protein-coding mRNAs are polyadenylated, but replication-dependent histone mRNAs provide an important exception with specialized 3′-end processing."],"keyPoints":["Cleavage precedes tail addition.","Poly-A polymerase adds the tail without copying a conventional DNA template for each A."],"example":"A mutation in a polyadenylation signal can change 3′-end processing and mRNA abundance.","pitfalls":["Do not describe every human mRNA as obligatorily polyadenylated."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-4","title":"Splice sites and spliceosome assembly","body":["The spliceosome is a large RNA–protein complex assembled from small nuclear ribonucleoproteins and other factors. In the major spliceosome pathway, U1 helps recognize the 5′ splice site and U2 associates with the branch-point region. The common intron pattern begins GU and ends AG in the RNA.","Splice-site recognition depends on several sequence cues and proteins. A base change can destroy a normal splice site or create an abnormal one. Such a mutation can alter exon boundaries, retain intronic sequence or remove required exonic sequence."],"keyPoints":["U1: recognition of the 5′ splice region.","U2: branch-point region."],"example":"A mutation making a cryptic splice site more attractive can change the mature mRNA even when the gene is still transcribed normally.","pitfalls":["A GU–AG mnemonic does not imply those four letters alone specify every splice event."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-5","title":"The two transesterification reactions","body":["The branch-point adenosine uses its 2′ hydroxyl to attack the 5′ splice-site phosphate. The intron’s 5′ end becomes linked to this adenosine through a 2′–5′ bond, generating a lariat intermediate and a free upstream exon 3′ hydroxyl.","That free exon 3′ hydroxyl then attacks the 3′ splice site. The exons are joined and the intron lariat is released for subsequent degradation. ATP-dependent remodeling helps assemble and rearrange the spliceosome, while the bond exchanges themselves are transesterification reactions."],"keyPoints":["The branch-point nucleotide is adenosine.","The lariat contains an unusual 2′–5′ linkage."],"example":"Ask which hydroxyl performs the first attack: branch-point A uses 2′-OH, whereas the joining exon uses its 3′-OH in the second reaction.","pitfalls":["Splicing does not attach the intron permanently to the mature mRNA."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-6","title":"Alternative splicing and protein diversity","body":["One pre-mRNA can be spliced in different patterns to generate different mature RNAs. Inclusion or exclusion of selected exons changes the combinations of sequence retained. Tissue-specific factors allow the same gene to produce isoforms suited to different cellular functions.","Alternative splicing changes processing of a transcript rather than changing the inherited DNA sequence. Not every splice variant must encode a stable functional protein; some affect untranslated regions or generate RNAs destined for surveillance. The core lesson is that gene number and protein-isoform number are not identical."],"keyPoints":["One gene can produce several RNA isoforms.","Splicing choice can depend on cell type."],"example":"Different tropomyosin isoforms can be produced by selecting different exons from the same gene transcript.","pitfalls":["Alternative splicing is not the same as rearranging the chromosome in every cell."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":14}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-7","title":"Disease from abnormal splice-site choice","body":["A splice-site mutation in the β-globin gene can reduce correctly processed β-globin mRNA, producing β-thalassemia. The effect can occur through use of an abnormal splice site rather than a direct amino-acid replacement at the altered base.","The progeria example shows why synonymous does not guarantee harmless. A nucleotide change can leave the directly encoded amino acid unchanged while activating abnormal splicing, leading to an altered lamin A product. Always consider the RNA-processing context of a DNA variant."],"keyPoints":["A coding-silent change may change splice recognition.","Reduced functional RNA can cause disease even when transcription begins normally."],"example":"A sequence variant that activates a cryptic splice site may delete a segment from the final protein despite being synonymous at its original codon.","pitfalls":["Do not classify a variant as biologically neutral solely because its codon is synonymous."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":19}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-8","title":"RNA editing versus splicing","body":["RNA editing changes selected nucleotides or nucleotide content in an RNA after it has been transcribed. This differs from splicing, which removes introns and joins exon regions. Editing can change a codon, a regulatory feature or RNA behavior without requiring the same change in DNA.","In mammals, common teaching examples are C-to-U deamination and A-to-I deamination. Inosine can be interpreted by cellular machinery similarly to guanosine in relevant contexts. Editing is selective and regulated rather than an arbitrary rewriting of every transcript."],"keyPoints":["Splicing changes segment connections.","Editing changes selected RNA residues or sequence content."],"example":"An RNA codon converted into a stop codon by C-to-U editing is an editing event, not removal of an intron.","pitfalls":["Do not call ordinary 5′ capping RNA editing in this specific mechanism comparison."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":23}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-9","title":"Tissue-specific apolipoprotein B editing","body":["APOBEC-1-mediated C-to-U editing of apolipoprotein B mRNA in the intestine creates a premature stop codon. The intestinal product, apoB-48, is shorter than apoB-100 produced from unedited RNA in the liver. The same gene can therefore yield different proteins through RNA editing.","The DNA sequence need not acquire a stop mutation in intestinal cells. The difference is generated at the RNA level. This example connects tissue-specific processing to lipoprotein function while distinguishing editing from alternative splice choices."],"keyPoints":["APOBEC-1 is associated with C-to-U editing.","ApoB-48 is a shortened product of edited RNA."],"example":"If the edited RNA acquires UAA where the unedited RNA had a sense codon, translation terminates earlier.","pitfalls":["Do not explain apoB-48 solely by saying an intron was removed differently."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":27}]},{"id":"MOL-5:guide-10","title":"Species-specific editing and bacterial processing","body":["Kinetoplastid mitochondrial RNAs can undergo guide-RNA-directed uridine insertion or deletion. This is an organism-specific mechanism; it should not be used as the default explanation of human apoB or ADAR editing. Mammalian ADAR enzymes deaminate adenosine to inosine.","Bacteria also process RNAs, especially rRNAs and tRNAs. The comparison with eukaryotes is that typical bacterial mRNA does not undergo the same extensive cap/poly-A/spliceosome maturation pathway, not that every bacterial RNA is completely unprocessed."],"keyPoints":["Guide-RNA uridine insertion/deletion belongs to the kinetoplastid example.","RNA processing exists in bacteria as well as eukaryotes."],"example":"A question naming a human neuronal receptor and A-to-I editing points to ADAR rather than a kinetoplastid uridine-insertion enzyme.","pitfalls":["Avoid the absolute statement “prokaryotes never process RNA.”"],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-5","page":25}]}],"summary":["The cap is added at 5′ and the poly-A tail at 3′.","Exons are retained in mature RNA; not every exonic base is translated.","Alternative splicing changes exon combinations.","Editing changes selected RNA bases or residues after transcription."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-5:blank-0","prompt":"The 5′ mRNA cap contains 7-methyl_____.","answers":["guanosine"],"explanation":"The modified guanosine is joined through a 5′–5′ linkage.","concept":"MOL-5:0"},{"id":"MOL-5:blank-1","prompt":"The branch-point nucleotide forming the lariat is _____.","answers":["adenosine","adenine","A"],"explanation":"Its 2′ OH initiates the first splicing reaction.","concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-2"},{"id":"MOL-5:blank-2","prompt":"The intron lariat contains an unusual _____ phosphodiester link.","answers":["2′–5′","2′ to 5′","2 to 5","2-5"],"explanation":"The branch-point 2′ OH links to the intron 5′ phosphate.","concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-2"},{"id":"MOL-5:blank-3","prompt":"Different exon combinations from one transcript arise through alternative _____.","answers":["splicing"],"explanation":"Exon selection can generate isoforms without changing the DNA.","concept":"MOL-5:1"},{"id":"MOL-5:blank-4","prompt":"ADAR-mediated editing converts adenosine to _____.","answers":["inosine"],"explanation":"A-to-I editing differs from APOBEC C-to-U editing.","concept":"MOL-5:slides-2026-4"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-5:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Capping","definition":"Adds modified guanosine at the RNA 5′ end"},{"term":"Polyadenylation","definition":"Adds adenosines after cleavage at the RNA 3′ end"},{"term":"Splicing","definition":"Removes introns and joins exon regions"},{"term":"Alternative splicing","definition":"Selects different exon combinations from one transcript"},{"term":"RNA editing","definition":"Changes selected RNA residues after transcription"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-6":{"overview":"Translation converts an mRNA reading frame into a polypeptide. Build the mechanism from code recognition, tRNA charging and ribosomal movement, then connect different mutation types and protein modifications to their consequences.","objectives":["Interpret start and stop codons and the reading frame.","Predict missense, nonsense, synonymous and frameshift effects.","Explain tRNA charging and wobble.","Trace initiation, elongation and termination.","Distinguish post-translational modifications."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-6:guide-1","title":"The genetic code and reading frame","body":["Three mRNA bases form one codon. The code is degenerate because several codons can specify the same amino acid, but a given ordinary codon is not freely ambiguous between several amino acids. AUG specifies methionine and commonly establishes the initiation frame.","UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons in the standard code. They are recognized by release factors rather than ordinary aminoacyl-tRNAs. The code is nearly universal, with known exceptions such as selected mitochondrial assignments; the standard table is the basis of course sequence exercises."],"keyPoints":["64 codons include 61 ordinary sense codons and 3 stops.","Degenerate and ambiguous mean different things."],"example":"Two codons can both encode leucine without any single one of them randomly encoding leucine or lysine.","pitfalls":["A stop codon does not encode a special “stop amino acid.”"],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":3}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-2","title":"Point mutations and their consequences","body":["A synonymous change retains the encoded amino acid in the relevant reading frame, a missense change substitutes another amino acid, and a nonsense change creates a premature termination codon. The biological effect depends on location, chemistry and RNA processing as well as on the mutation label.","A replacement of a charged residue by a nonpolar one may change solubility or interactions. A premature stop may cause truncation or trigger mRNA surveillance. A synonymous variant can still affect splicing, stability or translation efficiency."],"keyPoints":["Classify the sequence change before predicting the phenotype.","An amino-acid substitution is not automatically a frameshift."],"example":"The sickle β-globin Glu→Val substitution is missense; it changes surface chemistry without shifting the whole reading frame.","pitfalls":["Do not assume all missense variants have the same severity."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":5}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-3","title":"Insertions, deletions and repeat expansions","body":["A coding insertion or deletion shifts the reading frame when its length is not a multiple of three. Codons downstream are regrouped, often creating a premature stop. A deletion of three bases preserves the frame but removes one amino acid.","CFTR F508del illustrates a severe in-frame deletion: loss of phenylalanine promotes misfolding and defective trafficking. Trinucleotide-repeat expansion is another distinct mutation class. In Huntington disease, expansion of a CAG repeat lengthens a polyglutamine tract rather than simply causing an ordinary one-base frameshift."],"keyPoints":["Multiples of three preserve the downstream frame.","In-frame does not mean benign."],"example":"Compare deletion of 1 base with deletion of 3 bases: only the first necessarily shifts the reading frame.","pitfalls":["Do not classify F508del as a frameshift solely because it is a deletion."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":6}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-4","title":"tRNA as an adaptor","body":["tRNA carries an anticodon that pairs antiparallel with an mRNA codon and an amino-acid attachment site at its 3′ end. It links nucleotide recognition to amino-acid delivery. The anticodon loop and attachment end are physically separated in the folded molecule.","The amino acid is attached to the terminal adenosine of the 3′ CCA end. The ribosome checks codon–anticodon recognition, but the accuracy of the attached amino acid largely depends on prior charging by the appropriate synthetase."],"keyPoints":["Anticodon pairs with mRNA.","Amino acid attaches at the tRNA 3′ end."],"example":"A tRNA with the correct anticodon but an incorrectly attached amino acid can deliver the wrong residue despite normal base pairing.","pitfalls":["The amino acid is not attached directly to the anticodon bases."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":15}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-5","title":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases and energy","body":["Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases recognize an amino acid and the appropriate tRNA identity features. They activate the amino acid using ATP and attach it to the tRNA. Many also have editing mechanisms that improve charging fidelity.","Charging converts ATP to AMP plus pyrophosphate, corresponding to expenditure of two high-energy phosphate equivalents after pyrophosphate hydrolysis. Additional GTP is used during several translation steps. Amino-acid activation and ribosomal translocation therefore have different immediate energy substrates."],"keyPoints":["Charging requires ATP.","A charged tRNA carries an activated amino acid."],"example":"If a synthetase fails, the relevant tRNA may remain uncharged even when the ribosome and mRNA are normal.","pitfalls":["Do not assign every energy-consuming translation event to ATP alone."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":18}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-6","title":"Wobble and codon recognition","body":["The third codon position can permit selected nonstandard base pairings with the first anticodon position. This wobble allows one tRNA species to recognize more than one synonymous codon and reduces the number of different tRNAs needed.","Wobble is constrained base recognition, not unrestricted mismatching at any codon position. Antiparallel orientation remains important: the 5′ anticodon base pairs with the 3′ codon base. Follow orientation carefully when converting between written anticodons and codons."],"keyPoints":["Wobble occurs at codon position 3/anticodon position 1.","It supports recognition of synonymous codons."],"example":"A change at the third base of a codon is often synonymous, but the code table must still be checked.","pitfalls":["Wobble does not mean every third-base substitution is silent."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":17}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-7","title":"Ribosome organization and initiation","body":["The small ribosomal subunit binds mRNA and supports decoding. The large subunit contains the peptidyl-transferase center, whose catalytic activity is associated with rRNA. Bacterial ribosomes are 70S, comprising 30S and 50S subunits; eukaryotic cytosolic ribosomes are 80S, comprising 40S and 60S. S values are sedimentation properties and are not added arithmetically.","Bacterial Shine–Dalgarno pairing positions initiation, while many eukaryotic messages recruit the small subunit near the 5′ cap and scan to a suitable AUG. The initiator tRNA begins in the P site rather than following the usual elongation entry route through A."],"keyPoints":["Initiator tRNA occupies P.","Shine–Dalgarno is a bacterial initiation mechanism."],"example":"A mature eukaryotic capped mRNA ordinarily does not require a bacterial Shine–Dalgarno sequence to initiate translation.","pitfalls":["Do not conclude that 30S+50S must make 80S."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":20}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-8","title":"Elongation through A, P and E sites","body":["The A site accepts incoming aminoacyl-tRNA during elongation. The P site holds the tRNA carrying the growing peptide. Peptidyl transfer moves that peptide to the amino acid in A, creating a new peptide bond and lengthening the chain at its carboxyl terminus.","Translocation advances the ribosome along the mRNA. The peptidyl-tRNA moves toward P and the uncharged tRNA exits through E. Repeated cycles read mRNA 5′→3′ while protein grows from N terminus to C terminus."],"keyPoints":["A: arrival; P: peptide; E: exit.","Peptide growth adds to the C terminus."],"example":"After peptidyl transfer, the growing chain is attached to the tRNA that was in A, not to the now uncharged former P-site tRNA.","pitfalls":["The initiator’s P-site entry is an exception to the usual A-site arrival pattern."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":22}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-9","title":"Termination and polysomes","body":["When a stop codon reaches the A site, a release factor promotes hydrolysis of the bond between the completed chain and the tRNA. The polypeptide is released and the translation complex is recycled. No ordinary tRNA bearing a “stop” amino acid is needed.","Multiple ribosomes can translate one mRNA simultaneously, forming a polysome. They occupy different positions and produce multiple copies of the encoded chain. Polysomes increase output from a message; they do not imply that the message is polycistronic."],"keyPoints":["Release factors recognize stop signals.","Polysomes can occur on a monocistronic mRNA."],"example":"Several ribosomes on one insulin mRNA can all produce insulin precursors rather than several unrelated proteins.","pitfalls":["Polysome and polycistronic describe different features."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":26}]},{"id":"MOL-6:guide-10","title":"Covalent modification and proteolytic processing","body":["After or during synthesis, proteins may be modified at particular chemical groups. Phosphorylation commonly targets Ser, Thr or Tyr hydroxyls. N-linked glycosylation uses Asn; O-linked glycosylation commonly uses Ser or Thr. Hydroxylation of selected Pro and Lys residues is important in collagen maturation.","Proteolytic trimming can remove signal peptides or activate precursors. These modifications alter function, targeting or stability without being additional codons inserted into the original mRNA. Always connect the modification to the relevant residue and cellular location."],"keyPoints":["Phosphorylation and glycosylation are different chemical modifications.","Proteolysis can be a normal activation step."],"example":"A precursor secreted after removal of a signal peptide has undergone processing; the ribosome did not skip those amino acids during translation.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse an Asn glycosylation site with a phosphate attached to tyrosine."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-6","page":30}]}],"summary":["mRNA is read 5′→3′ and the polypeptide grows N→C.","The initiator tRNA enters the P site.","Release factors recognize stop codons.","A three-base deletion is in frame but can still cause severe disease."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-6:blank-0","prompt":"During elongation, incoming aminoacyl-tRNA usually enters the _____ site.","answers":["A"],"explanation":"The initiator tRNA is the important P-site exception.","concept":"MOL-6:0"},{"id":"MOL-6:blank-1","prompt":"The ribosome reads mRNA from _____ to _____.","answers":["5′ to 3′","5 to 3","5′→3′","5->3"],"explanation":"Translation moves toward the mRNA 3′ end.","concept":"MOL-6:3"},{"id":"MOL-6:blank-2","prompt":"The protein chain grows from its _____ terminus toward its carboxyl terminus.","answers":["amino","N","N terminus"],"explanation":"New amino acids are added to the chain C terminus.","concept":"MOL-6:3"},{"id":"MOL-6:blank-3","prompt":"A premature stop-producing base substitution is a _____ mutation.","answers":["nonsense"],"explanation":"A missense substitution instead changes a sense codon to another amino acid.","concept":"MOL-6:2"},{"id":"MOL-6:blank-4","prompt":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases charge tRNA using energy from _____.","answers":["ATP"],"explanation":"Amino-acid activation converts ATP to AMP and pyrophosphate.","concept":"MOL-6:slides-2026-1"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-6:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"A site","definition":"Accepts incoming aminoacyl-tRNA during ordinary elongation"},{"term":"P site","definition":"Holds the tRNA carrying the growing peptide"},{"term":"E site","definition":"Provides a route for uncharged tRNA exit"},{"term":"Synthetase","definition":"Charges a tRNA with its amino acid"},{"term":"Release factor","definition":"Promotes termination at a stop codon"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-7":{"overview":"Cells with essentially the same genome can express very different genes. This lesson connects chromatin accessibility and DNA regulatory elements to signaling, RNA interference, translation control and laboratory measurements.","objectives":["Compare euchromatin and heterochromatin.","Explain methylation, acetylation and epigenetic states.","Distinguish cis DNA elements from trans products.","Trace glucagon and cortisol effects on gene expression.","Explain miRNA, ferritin regulation and expression assays."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-7:guide-1","title":"Differential gene expression","body":["A neuron and a hepatocyte share essentially the same genomic information but express different subsets of genes and different amounts of their products. Differential expression establishes cell identity and allows responses to nutrients, hormones and stress.","Regulation can occur at chromatin, transcription, RNA processing, mRNA turnover, translation or protein degradation. A change in one level need not produce an immediate identical change at every later level. Protein half-life, for example, can delay a response after mRNA decreases."],"keyPoints":["Gene presence is distinct from gene expression.","Expression can be regulated at several stages."],"example":"A long-lived protein may remain abundant after its mRNA has already fallen.","pitfalls":["Different cell functions do not usually require each cell type to delete all unused genes."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-2","title":"Euchromatin and heterochromatin","body":["Euchromatin is generally less compact and more accessible to transcription machinery, whereas heterochromatin is more compact and often less transcriptionally active. These are broad structural and functional associations, not claims that every gene in a region has an identical activity state at every moment.","Constitutive heterochromatin is relatively persistent, such as repeat-rich centromeric regions. Facultative heterochromatin can be established in a regulated context, exemplified by an inactive X chromosome. Accessibility is therefore both a packaging property and a mechanism of regulation."],"keyPoints":["Euchromatin generally permits greater access.","Facultative heterochromatin depends on developmental or cellular context."],"example":"A gene can become less accessible when its region adopts a more compact chromatin state.","pitfalls":["The supplied sample question with unclear euchromatin options should be interpreted from these principles, not memorized as an unreliable key."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":6}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-3","title":"DNA methylation and epigenetic information","body":["Methylation of cytosines at CpG sites can influence gene expression, particularly when present at certain promoter regions. DNA methyltransferases establish or maintain marks, while other pathways can modify and remove methylation. Promoter CpG methylation is commonly associated with transcriptional repression.","Epigenetic inheritance concerns persistence of an expression-related state without requiring a change in base sequence. Genomic imprinting is an important example in which parental origin influences allele expression. Not every methylated cytosine has the same effect in every genomic context."],"keyPoints":["An epigenetic change need not alter DNA letters.","Imprinting can distinguish maternal and paternal allele expression."],"example":"Two alleles with the same coding sequence can have different expression because of parent-specific regulatory marks.","pitfalls":["DNA methylation is not equivalent to changing C into T in the genome."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-4","title":"Histone modifications and accessibility","body":["Histone acetyltransferases add acetyl groups to lysine side chains. This reduces the positive charge associated with the lysine group and often supports a more transcriptionally permissive chromatin environment. Histone deacetylases remove these groups and are often associated with repression.","Histone methylation has a more context-dependent meaning. The effect depends on which residue is modified and the pattern of methylation. “All histone methylation silences genes” is therefore an incorrect extension of a simple mnemonic."],"keyPoints":["HAT adds acetyl groups; HDAC removes them.","Methylation effects depend on the histone residue and mark."],"example":"An increase in promoter-associated histone acetylation commonly accompanies increased accessibility.","pitfalls":["Do not apply the DNA-promoter methylation rule indiscriminately to every histone methyl mark."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":11}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-5","title":"Cis elements and trans factors","body":["Promoters, enhancers, silencers and insulators are DNA control elements. They act in cis because their regulatory effect is linked to the DNA molecule on which they reside. Activators and repressors are usually diffusible proteins that can bind appropriate sites and act in trans.","A mutation in a cis element can affect its linked gene even when a normal transcription factor is present. A defective trans factor can affect many target genes with corresponding binding sites. This distinction helps predict the pattern of consequences of a mutation."],"keyPoints":["Enhancer: cis DNA.","Activator: trans-acting protein."],"example":"A missing activator can reduce expression from several normal enhancer-containing genes.","pitfalls":["A distal enhancer remains cis even though it is far from its promoter."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-6","title":"Activators, domains and DNA looping","body":["A transcriptional activator recognizes a DNA element and recruits or influences machinery that promotes transcription. Distinct functional parts can bind DNA, interact with other proteins or respond to a signal. A short sequence motif is not equivalent to a full folded domain.","DNA looping allows a distal regulatory element and a promoter to approach in three-dimensional space. Consequently, a long distance measured along the DNA sequence does not prevent a physical interaction through protein complexes."],"keyPoints":["Regulatory proteins can have separable functional domains.","Distance in the linear DNA sequence differs from three-dimensional proximity."],"example":"A factor retaining its DNA-binding domain but losing an activation domain may bind the enhancer without efficiently stimulating transcription.","pitfalls":["Binding DNA is not alone proof that a protein activates transcription."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":15}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-7","title":"Glucagon, cAMP and CREB","body":["Glucagon signals through a cell-surface receptor and a cAMP-dependent pathway. Activation of protein kinase A can lead to phosphorylation of CREB, which regulates genes through cAMP response elements. The lecture uses increased PEPCK expression to connect signaling to gluconeogenesis.","The response element is DNA; the hormone, receptor, kinase and transcription factor are different participants. Keep the order of events clear instead of treating a hormone as if it directly entered the promoter sequence."],"keyPoints":["Glucagon can signal through cAMP→PKA→CREB.","CRE is the DNA element; CREB is the binding protein."],"example":"A mutation preventing CREB activation can blunt a transcriptional response even when glucagon receptor signaling initially raises cAMP.","pitfalls":["CREB is not the enzyme that synthesizes cAMP."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":20}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-8","title":"Cortisol and intracellular receptors","body":["Cortisol is a steroid hormone that can cross the membrane and bind an intracellular glucocorticoid receptor. The activated receptor complex influences transcription through glucocorticoid response elements and associated regulatory machinery.","This differs from the classic glucagon surface-receptor/cAMP pathway. Both can regulate expression of gluconeogenic genes, but they do so through distinct receptor and signaling mechanisms. Compare the location of the receptor and the DNA-binding regulatory product."],"keyPoints":["GRE is a cis response element.","The glucocorticoid receptor is a trans-acting regulatory protein."],"example":"A cortisol response can depend on receptor DNA-binding ability even if circulating cortisol concentration is normal.","pitfalls":["Do not require cortisol to activate a membrane adenylyl cyclase as its defining mechanism."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":21}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-9","title":"RNA interference and mRNA regulation","body":["Small RNAs guide silencing complexes to target RNAs through base pairing. miRNAs often reduce translation and promote degradation of partially complementary targets in animal cells; siRNA pathways commonly guide cleavage when complementarity is extensive. Both provide sequence-dependent post-transcriptional control.","Dicer participates in processing selected precursor RNAs, while the RNA-induced silencing complex uses a guide strand to recognize a target. The target is usually mRNA in these teaching examples, not the amino-acid sequence of a protein."],"keyPoints":["Guide RNA supplies sequence specificity.","Silencing can lower protein output without initially changing transcription."],"example":"A reporter retaining its promoter but acquiring a miRNA target site can produce less protein after the miRNA is expressed.","pitfalls":["miRNA does not attach to a tRNA anticodon to change the genetic code."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":23}]},{"id":"MOL-7:guide-10","title":"Ferritin control and expression assays","body":["When iron is low, an iron-regulatory protein can bind an iron-responsive element in the 5′ UTR of ferritin mRNA and hinder translation initiation. When iron is abundant, this repression is relieved, allowing more iron-storage protein to be made. The position of an IRE matters; a 5′ translation-control example should not be generalized to every IRE-bearing transcript.","Northern blot measures an RNA signal; Western blot measures protein. Quantitative reverse-transcription PCR assesses selected RNA-derived targets, and RNA sequencing examines transcript populations. A qPCR assay of RNA expression requires conversion of RNA to cDNA before amplification."],"keyPoints":["Low iron restrains ferritin translation.","Western measures protein; Northern measures RNA."],"example":"Unchanged ferritin mRNA with increased ferritin protein after iron rises is consistent with translational control.","pitfalls":["Ordinary DNA qPCR by itself does not directly measure the quantity of mRNA."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-7","page":25}]}],"summary":["Histone acetylation commonly favors access.","Histone methylation depends on the residue and context.","Cis refers to linked DNA sequence; trans refers to a diffusible product.","mRNA abundance and protein abundance measure different levels of expression."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-7:blank-0","prompt":"An enhancer is a _____-acting regulatory DNA element.","answers":["cis"],"explanation":"It is linked on the same DNA molecule rather than being a diffusible protein.","concept":"MOL-7:1"},{"id":"MOL-7:blank-1","prompt":"Histone _____ commonly increases transcriptional accessibility.","answers":["acetylation"],"explanation":"Acetylation of lysines commonly favors permissive chromatin.","concept":"MOL-7:0"},{"id":"MOL-7:blank-2","prompt":"A glucagon response can signal through cAMP, PKA and _____.","answers":["CREB"],"explanation":"Activated CREB regulates transcription at cAMP-response elements.","concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-3"},{"id":"MOL-7:blank-3","prompt":"Low iron permits an IRP to inhibit _____ mRNA translation.","answers":["ferritin"],"explanation":"Binding at the 5′ UTR restrains production of the iron-storage protein.","concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-4"},{"id":"MOL-7:blank-4","prompt":"A _____ blot detects a protein signal.","answers":["Western","western"],"explanation":"Northern detects RNA, while Western detects protein.","concept":"MOL-7:slides-2026-5"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-7:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Enhancer","definition":"Cis-acting DNA sequence bound by regulatory proteins"},{"term":"Activator","definition":"Trans-acting protein that promotes expression"},{"term":"HAT","definition":"Enzyme that adds acetyl groups to histone lysines"},{"term":"HDAC","definition":"Enzyme that removes histone acetyl groups"},{"term":"Western blot","definition":"Assay detecting a protein signal"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-8":{"overview":"Amino-acid side chains explain how proteins interact, fold and acquire modifications. Learn the shared backbone and then organize the 20 residues by charge, polarity and special chemistry rather than memorizing unrelated names.","objectives":["Recognize the shared alpha-amino-acid framework.","Classify side chains at physiological pH.","Identify chirality exceptions and special residues.","Connect hydroxyl, thiol and amide groups to modifications.","Infer peptide properties from sequence."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-8:guide-1","title":"The alpha-amino-acid framework","body":["The alpha carbon connects an amino group, a carboxyl group, hydrogen and a variable side chain, R. The side chain determines many distinctive properties. Amino acids in proteins are connected through peptide bonds, leaving the side chains available for interactions and selected chemical modifications.","A structural drawing must be interpreted at its stated pH. Writing NH2 and COOH is a convenient neutral formula, but free amino acids near physiological pH commonly have protonated amino and deprotonated carboxyl groups."],"keyPoints":["The R group varies; the general backbone is shared.","Ionization state depends on pH."],"example":"Changing only the R group from hydrogen to methyl changes glycine into alanine without changing the general backbone pattern.","pitfalls":["Do not treat a neutral drawing as proof that the predominant solution form is uncharged at every group."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":3}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-2","title":"Zwitterions and net charge","body":["A zwitterion carries both positive and negative groups within the same molecule. A free amino acid with NH3+ and COO− can have zero net charge while still containing charged sites that interact strongly with water. Zero net charge is therefore different from absence of charge.","Additional ionizable side chains alter the overall balance. Near physiological pH, a free acidic amino acid usually has an extra negative charge from its side-chain carboxylate, whereas a basic residue can contribute an extra positive group."],"keyPoints":["Add the charges of every relevant ionizable group.","A net-zero zwitterion still has polar charged sites."],"example":"Glycine near its isoelectric region can have one positive and one negative group, summing to zero.","pitfalls":["“Zwitterion” does not mean a molecule alternates between being entirely positive and entirely negative."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-3","title":"Chirality and the glycine exception","body":["Most alpha amino acids have four different groups attached to the alpha carbon and are chiral. Ribosomal protein synthesis uses the L series in the standard course framework. Glycine has hydrogen as its side chain, so the alpha carbon has two hydrogens and is not chiral.","D/L naming describes configuration relative to a reference and is not identical to the direction in which a compound rotates plane-polarized light. Isoleucine and threonine contain an additional stereocenter in their side chains, a useful reminder that the alpha carbon is not the only possible source of chirality."],"keyPoints":["Glycine is achiral.","L does not itself mean levorotatory."],"example":"Replacing the glycine side-chain hydrogen with methyl produces alanine and introduces alpha-carbon chirality.","pitfalls":["Do not assign glycine an L or D configuration at an achiral alpha carbon."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":5}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-4","title":"Nonpolar and branched-chain residues","body":["Nonpolar side chains interact less favorably with water and commonly pack within soluble proteins. Alanine, valine, leucine, isoleucine and methionine are common examples. Valine, leucine and isoleucine are the branched-chain amino acids emphasized in the lecture.","The tendency toward a hydrophobic core depends on the protein environment. A membrane-spanning region may expose nonpolar residues to membrane lipids rather than burying them away from every surroundings. Classification predicts interactions, not a rigid location for each residue."],"keyPoints":["Val, Leu and Ile are branched-chain amino acids.","Hydrophobic side chains often contact other nonpolar surfaces."],"example":"A membrane helix can be enriched in leucine because its side chains face the lipid interior.","pitfalls":["Do not conclude all nonpolar amino acids must lie inside a soluble protein in every context."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-5","title":"Polar uncharged side chains","body":["Serine and threonine contain hydroxyl groups, while asparagine and glutamine contain amides. These groups support hydrogen bonding and often occur on water-exposed protein surfaces or in specific internal interaction networks. Cysteine has a thiol with distinctive redox and covalent chemistry.","Polar uncharged refers to the usual side-chain state near the physiological range, not to the entire free amino acid being devoid of charged backbone groups. Asparagine and glutamine are amides and must be distinguished from acidic aspartate and glutamate."],"keyPoints":["Asn/Gln have amide side chains.","Asp/Glu have carboxylate side chains."],"example":"Changing an aspartate side-chain carboxylate into an amide creates asparagine and removes that ordinary side-chain negative charge.","pitfalls":["The similar names do not make asparagine an acidic amino acid."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-6","title":"Acidic and basic side chains","body":["Aspartate and glutamate generally have deprotonated, negatively charged side chains near physiological pH. Lysine and arginine generally retain positive side-chain charge. Histidine has an imidazole group whose protonation is especially sensitive near biologically relevant pH ranges and is useful in catalysis and buffering.","Charge is a pH-dependent property. Raising pH tends to remove protons; lowering pH tends to add them. The identity of the ionizable group and its local environment determine the relevant pKa."],"keyPoints":["Asp/Glu: usual acidic side chains.","Lys/Arg: usually positive; His is condition-sensitive."],"example":"A histidine near an enzyme active site can participate in proton transfer because its protonation can change.","pitfalls":["Do not label histidine obligatorily +1 under every physiological condition."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":11}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-7","title":"Glycine, proline and backbone behavior","body":["Glycine’s small side chain permits conformations that bulkier residues cannot easily adopt, making it useful in tight turns. Its flexibility can also make a regular helix less stable in some settings. Proline’s side chain joins back to the backbone nitrogen and restricts backbone geometry.","A proline within a regular alpha helix can introduce a kink and lacks the usual backbone N–H donor at that residue. Its effect depends on position and protein architecture; “helix breaker” is a useful tendency rather than a rule that proline can never occur in any helix."],"keyPoints":["Glycine is unusually flexible.","Proline constrains backbone conformation."],"example":"A tight turn may favor glycine, whereas inserting proline into the middle of a long regular helix may disturb its hydrogen-bond pattern.","pitfalls":["Small side chain and rigid cyclic side chain produce different effects."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-8","title":"Aromatic and sulfur-containing residues","body":["Phenylalanine, tyrosine and tryptophan contain aromatic rings. Tyrosine also has a hydroxyl and can make polar interactions and undergo phosphorylation. Tryptophan and tyrosine are important contributors to protein ultraviolet absorbance. Aromatic does not therefore mean chemically identical or uniformly nonpolar.","Cysteine contains a thiol and can be oxidized with another cysteine to form cystine, a disulfide-linked pair. Methionine contains a thioether; it cannot form a cysteine-like disulfide using an –SH because it does not have one."],"keyPoints":["Tyr combines an aromatic ring with an –OH.","Cys can form disulfides; Met is a thioether."],"example":"A reagent that reduces disulfide bridges targets cystine connections rather than a bond between two methionines.","pitfalls":["Do not identify every sulfur-containing side chain as a thiol."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":14}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-9","title":"Residue-specific modifications","body":["Serine, threonine and tyrosine are common sites of phosphorylation through their hydroxyl groups. Asparagine supports N-linked glycosylation, while serine and threonine commonly support O-linked glycosylation. The names describe the atom joining the sugar to the protein.","A modification can change charge, interactions, targeting or activity without changing the original amino-acid sequence. A residue can belong to more than one useful category: tyrosine is aromatic and can be phosphorylated, while serine is polar and can receive selected sugars."],"keyPoints":["N-linked glycosylation: Asn.","O-linked glycosylation: commonly Ser/Thr."],"example":"An extracellular protein carrying sugar on the amide nitrogen of Asn has N-linked rather than O-linked glycosylation.","pitfalls":["The N in N-linked does not refer to the protein N terminus."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":16}]},{"id":"MOL-8:guide-10","title":"Reading a peptide as a chemical object","body":["For a peptide sequence, first identify the free N and C termini, then the side chains with charges or special groups. In Gly–Leu–Asp–Ser–Arg–Tyr–Met, Asp supplies an acidic side chain, Arg a basic side chain, Ser and Tyr hydroxyls, and Met sulfur in a thioether.","Internal alpha-amino and alpha-carboxyl groups are incorporated into peptide bonds and are not counted as separate free ionizable termini. Exact net charge requires pH and pKa information; a sequence alone does not justify counting every amino acid as a separate zwitterion."],"keyPoints":["Only the chain ends are free ordinary termini.","Side chains retain their distinctive chemistry within a peptide."],"example":"The sequence has possible common phosphorylation sites at Ser and Tyr, but no cysteine for a disulfide bridge.","pitfalls":["Do not count methionine as a disulfide-forming cysteine."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-8","page":19}]}],"summary":["Most protein amino acids are L; glycine is achiral.","Asp/Glu are acidic, while Lys/Arg are usually positive near physiological pH.","Cysteine can make disulfides; methionine contains a thioether.","Ser/Thr/Tyr are common phosphorylation sites."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-8:blank-0","prompt":"The only common protein amino acid with an achiral alpha carbon is _____.","answers":["glycine","Gly"],"explanation":"Its R group is H, leaving two identical hydrogens at Cα.","concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-2"},{"id":"MOL-8:blank-1","prompt":"Aspartate and glutamate usually have _____ side-chain charge near pH7.4.","answers":["negative","a negative"],"explanation":"Their side-chain carboxyl groups are mostly deprotonated.","concept":"MOL-8:0"},{"id":"MOL-8:blank-2","prompt":"Two _____ residues can form a disulfide bond.","answers":["cysteine","Cys"],"explanation":"Their thiols oxidize to a disulfide-linked cystine pair.","concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-3"},{"id":"MOL-8:blank-3","prompt":"N-linked glycosylation commonly attaches sugar to _____.","answers":["asparagine","Asn"],"explanation":"The link uses the side-chain amide nitrogen.","concept":"MOL-8:1"},{"id":"MOL-8:blank-4","prompt":"Valine, leucine and _____ are branched-chain amino acids.","answers":["isoleucine","Ile"],"explanation":"All three have branched nonpolar side chains.","concept":"MOL-8:slides-2026-4"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-8:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Glycine","definition":"Small amino acid with an achiral alpha carbon"},{"term":"Proline","definition":"Residue whose cyclic side chain constrains the backbone"},{"term":"Cysteine","definition":"Thiol-containing residue that can form disulfides"},{"term":"Methionine","definition":"Sulfur-containing residue with a thioether side chain"},{"term":"Tyrosine","definition":"Aromatic residue containing a hydroxyl group"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-9":{"overview":"Buffers connect chemical equilibrium to physiological pH control. Work from protonation and pKa through Henderson–Hasselbalch calculations, titration curves and peptide charge, then connect metabolic acids to respiratory and renal handling.","objectives":["Use pH and pKa to predict protonation.","Apply Henderson–Hasselbalch correctly.","Explain capacity and optimal buffer range.","Calculate amino-acid and peptide charge and pI.","Distinguish volatile acid, fixed acid and physiological buffering."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-9:guide-1","title":"pH, Ka and pKa describe different quantities","body":["pH describes hydrogen-ion activity in a solution, commonly approximated with concentration for course calculations. Ka describes the equilibrium tendency of a particular acid to dissociate, and pKa is −log10 Ka. A lower pKa corresponds to a stronger acid within the comparison being made.","The pH belongs to the solution and can change when acid or base is added. A stated pKa belongs to a particular ionizable group under stated conditions. Confusing these quantities makes it difficult to predict protonation or choose a buffer."],"keyPoints":["pH measures the solution’s acidity.","pKa describes an acid–base equilibrium."],"example":"Two weak acids placed in the same solution share the solution pH but can have different pKas and different protonation fractions.","pitfalls":["pH and pKa are not always equal."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-2","title":"Deriving the useful buffer relationship","body":["For HA ⇌ H+ + A−, the dissociation equilibrium relates H+, HA and A−. Rearranging the logarithms gives pH = pKa + log10([A−]/[HA]) in the usual concentration approximation. The numerator is conjugate base; the denominator is acid.","If base and acid are equal, the logarithm is zero and pH=pKa. A ratio of 10 gives pH one unit above pKa; a ratio of 0.1 gives pH one unit below pKa. The direction of the ratio is the most common practical trap."],"keyPoints":["Base/acid ratio 10:1 means pH=pKa+1.","Acid/base 10:1 means pH=pKa−1."],"example":"For pKa 6 and [A−]:[HA]=100:1, pH=8.","pitfalls":["Do not reverse HA and A− in the logarithm."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":5}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-3","title":"Protonation from pH versus pKa","body":["At pH below pKa, the protonated form predominates. At pH above pKa, the deprotonated form predominates. The charge change depends on the group: COOH becomes COO−, while NH3+ becomes NH2. Deprotonated therefore does not always mean negatively charged.","At pH=pKa, half the population is in each state for that simple group. These are population proportions, not instructions that one molecule must always carry a fractional physical proton. A local protein environment can shift the pKa from a free amino-acid value."],"keyPoints":["Low pH favors protonation.","High pH favors deprotonation."],"example":"At pH 9, a carboxyl group with pKa 4 is overwhelmingly deprotonated, but an amino group with pKa 10 remains mostly protonated.","pitfalls":["Do not assign charge only from the word acid without considering pH."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":7}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-4","title":"How a buffer resists added acid or base","body":["A buffer contains a weak acid and its conjugate base in useful amounts. Added H+ is consumed largely by the conjugate base; added OH− is neutralized largely through the acid form. The interconversion shifts the base/acid ratio and limits the pH change.","Capacity is finite. Once one component is nearly exhausted, the solution no longer buffers effectively. Higher total buffer concentration generally improves capacity at the same ratio, while the ratio controls the pH through Henderson–Hasselbalch."],"keyPoints":["Added acid consumes A−.","Added base consumes HA."],"example":"A 0.1 M buffer and 0.001 M buffer can have the same pH but very different resistance to a given added amount of acid.","pitfalls":["A buffer does not hold pH perfectly unchanged after unlimited acid addition."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":8}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-5","title":"Choosing a buffer and reading titration curves","body":["A useful buffering region usually lies within about one pH unit of pKa, where both acid and conjugate base are appreciably present. Capacity is greatest near equal amounts for a simple monoprotic buffer. A titration curve is relatively flat in that region because added titrant mainly changes the acid/base balance.","At the midpoint of a simple dissociation step, pH=pKa. Molecules with several ionizable groups show several regions rather than one universal plateau. The pI of an amino acid is a different point from every individual pKa."],"keyPoints":["Choose a pKa near the desired pH.","Each ionizable group can produce a separate titration transition."],"example":"A buffer with pKa 7.2 is more suitable near pH 7.4 than one with pKa 3.0 when comparable concentrations are used.","pitfalls":["A steep region is not the area of strongest buffering."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":7}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-6","title":"Glycine titration and isoelectric point","body":["At very low pH, glycine’s amino and carboxyl groups are protonated and the molecule has net +1. Removing the carboxyl proton gives the zwitterion with net zero. At sufficiently high pH, the ammonium group also loses a proton and net charge becomes −1.","For an amino acid without an ionizable side chain, the pI is the average of the two pKas surrounding the net-zero species. At pI, average net charge is zero, but the zwitterionic molecule still has internal charged groups and can interact with water."],"keyPoints":["Glycine progresses +1→0→−1 as pH rises.","pI is an average of the relevant bracketing pKas."],"example":"With illustrative pKas 2.3 and 9.6, glycine pI≈5.95.","pitfalls":["Do not describe the pI form as NH2–CH2–COOH being the predominant ordinary neutral molecule."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-7","title":"Ionizable side chains and peptide charge","body":["A peptide has one ordinary free N terminus and one ordinary free C terminus. Internal alpha-amino and carboxyl groups are tied up in amide bonds. Asp, Glu, His, Cys, Tyr, Lys and Arg can contribute side-chain ionization depending on pH and environment.","Determine the protonation of each group from its pKa, assign its charge and add the values. For pI, identify the two dissociations surrounding the net-zero form rather than averaging every pKa in the molecule. Acidic amino acids and basic amino acids therefore use different pairs."],"keyPoints":["Count termini plus ionizable side chains.","Choose pKas bracketing net zero for pI."],"example":"A peptide containing two Asp residues can have more negative charge than a peptide of the same length lacking acidic side chains.","pitfalls":["Never give each internal residue a separate free amino and carboxyl charge."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":11}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-8","title":"Solubility and the glargine example","body":["A protein’s net charge influences interactions and solubility, and its isoelectric region can favor reduced electrostatic repulsion between molecules. Insulin glargine uses sequence changes that alter its physicochemical behavior. The acidic formulation remains soluble; after neutralization in subcutaneous tissue, microprecipitates form and release insulin gradually.","The supplied slide contains inconsistent “more soluble” wording at tissue pH. Learn the coherent mechanism of precipitation after neutralization rather than memorizing that inconsistent phrase. This is a formulation example, not a reason to alter prescribed insulin use."],"keyPoints":["Sequence changes can shift pI and solubility behavior.","Glargine forms microprecipitates after neutralization."],"example":"The slow release is explained by a depot formed under tissue conditions rather than immediate high solubility at neutral pH.","pitfalls":["Do not grade the contradictory source wording as if both directions were correct."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-9","title":"Metabolic acid and physiological buffer systems","body":["Metabolism continually supplies acid equivalents. CO2 participates in the bicarbonate system through hydration and carbonic-acid equilibrium and can be removed by the lungs. Fixed acid generation, including products related to sulfur-containing amino acids and incomplete oxidation, also requires buffering and renal handling.","Bicarbonate is a major extracellular buffer system, while proteins, hemoglobin and phosphate contribute in relevant compartments. Chemical buffers act immediately by binding or releasing H+; ventilation and renal processes change the body’s acid/base content over time."],"keyPoints":["Volatile acid handling is linked to CO2 excretion.","Buffers redistribute H+ but do not themselves eliminate all acid from the body."],"example":"Hypoventilation can retain CO2 and lower pH through the bicarbonate equilibrium.","pitfalls":["Do not equate a buffer reaction with permanent acid excretion."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-9:guide-10","title":"Hyperventilation and ionized calcium","body":["Excessive ventilation lowers arterial CO2 and can increase blood pH. At a higher pH, albumin binds more calcium, reducing the ionized fraction even when total calcium has not necessarily fallen. Ionized calcium is the biologically active fraction relevant to neuromuscular excitability.","This explains tingling or muscle spasm in the lecture’s hyperventilation example. Follow the chain from ventilation to CO2, then pH, then calcium binding; do not jump directly from rapid breathing to loss of all total calcium."],"keyPoints":["Hyperventilation can cause respiratory alkalosis.","Alkalosis can lower ionized calcium through increased protein binding."],"example":"Total calcium may be unchanged while the free ionized fraction decreases.","pitfalls":["A fall in ionized calcium does not require an equal fall in the measured total calcium concentration."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-9","page":20}]}],"summary":["pH=pKa gives equal acid and conjugate-base amounts.","At pH above pKa, deprotonated form predominates.","Buffers resist change but have finite capacity.","pI requires the pKas bracketing the net-zero form."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-9:blank-0","prompt":"When pH=pKa, [A−]/[HA] equals _____.","answers":["1","one"],"explanation":"The log term is zero at an equal ratio.","concept":"MOL-9:0"},{"id":"MOL-9:blank-1","prompt":"When pH exceeds pKa, the _____ form predominates.","answers":["deprotonated"],"explanation":"A higher pH favors loss of H+.","concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-0"},{"id":"MOL-9:blank-2","prompt":"A buffer works best when its pKa is _____ the desired pH.","answers":["near","close to","approximately equal to"],"explanation":"Both acid and base forms then remain appreciably available.","concept":"MOL-9:1"},{"id":"MOL-9:blank-3","prompt":"The pH of zero average net charge is the _____.","answers":["pI","isoelectric point"],"explanation":"Internal charges may still be present at net zero.","concept":"MOL-9:2"},{"id":"MOL-9:blank-4","prompt":"Hyperventilation generally _____ arterial CO2.","answers":["lowers","decreases","reduces"],"explanation":"CO2 loss can drive respiratory alkalosis and alter ionized calcium.","concept":"MOL-9:slides-2026-5"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-9:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"pH","definition":"Measure of hydrogen-ion activity in a solution"},{"term":"pKa","definition":"Logarithmic measure of acid dissociation equilibrium"},{"term":"pI","definition":"pH at which average molecular net charge is zero"},{"term":"Buffer capacity","definition":"Ability to resist a given amount of added acid or base"},{"term":"Conjugate base","definition":"Species remaining after an acid donates a proton"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}]},"MOL-10":{"overview":"Protein structure connects amino-acid sequence to a functional three-dimensional shape. This guide follows the structure and folding topics in the shared MOL 10/11 deck; disease mechanisms continue in MOL 11. The shared file has no explicit official lecture divider.","objectives":["Distinguish primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure.","Explain peptide-bond constraints.","Compare alpha helices and beta sheets.","Identify forces that stabilize folding.","Explain chaperones, denaturation and thermal stability."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-10:guide-1","title":"Amino-acid sequence and protein function","body":["Primary structure is the ordered amino-acid sequence of a polypeptide, conventionally written N terminus to C terminus. It is determined by the coding sequence and influences the conformations and interactions available to the chain. Two proteins with the same overall amino-acid composition can behave differently if the order differs.","A mutation can alter a local interaction, a catalytic residue, a targeting signal or a surface patch. It need not unfold the entire protein to cause disease. Sequence, folding, location and interaction partners all contribute to function."],"keyPoints":["Sequence order matters, not only composition.","A single substitution can alter a specific protein behavior."],"example":"Replacing a charged glutamate with valine changes local surface chemistry even if much of the fold is preserved.","pitfalls":["Primary structure does not mean simply the total percentage of each amino acid."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":7}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-2","title":"Peptide bonds and the repeating backbone","body":["A peptide bond joins the carboxyl carbon of one amino acid to the amino nitrogen of the next. The repeating backbone follows N–Cα–C, with side chains projecting from the alpha carbons. The chain has distinct amino and carboxyl ends.","The peptide bond has partial double-bond character because of resonance, making the peptide unit relatively planar and limiting free rotation around that C–N bond. Much of backbone conformational flexibility instead comes from rotation around bonds adjacent to the alpha carbon, subject to steric constraints."],"keyPoints":["Peptide bonds are covalent amide links.","The peptide unit is not a freely rotating single bond."],"example":"An agent that unfolds a protein without hydrolyzing peptide bonds changes conformation while retaining the amino-acid sequence.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse restricted peptide-bond rotation with a completely rigid entire protein chain."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-3","title":"The four levels as distinct questions","body":["Primary asks which amino acids occur and in what order. Secondary asks how local backbone segments form regular arrangements such as alpha helices and beta sheets. Tertiary asks how one complete polypeptide folds in three dimensions. Quaternary asks how multiple polypeptide subunits associate.","A monomeric protein can have primary, secondary and tertiary structure without quaternary structure. A multimeric protein contains subunits with their own folds plus an additional assembly relationship between them. The categories overlap physically but answer different structural questions."],"keyPoints":["One chain’s complete fold is tertiary.","Two or more polypeptide subunits are needed for quaternary structure."],"example":"Myoglobin is a one-chain example; hemoglobin’s several globin chains add quaternary organization.","pitfalls":["Several helices within one chain do not by themselves create quaternary structure."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":5}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-4","title":"Alpha helices: backbone hydrogen bonding","body":["The common protein alpha helix is right-handed and has approximately 3.6 residues per turn. A backbone carbonyl of residue i hydrogen-bonds to a backbone N–H of residue i+4. These repeated local interactions stabilize a coiled backbone arrangement.","Side chains project outward from the helix. They affect whether a helix is favorable and what surface it presents, but the defining repeated hydrogen bonds are between backbone groups. A side chain that clashes sterically or disrupts the donor pattern can destabilize a local helix."],"keyPoints":["Hydrogen bonds use backbone C=O and N–H.","The i→i+4 relationship is a characteristic alpha-helix pattern."],"example":"A proline inserted into the middle of a regular helix can interrupt geometry and remove one ordinary backbone donor.","pitfalls":["The main stabilizing pattern is not a hydrogen bond between every pair of neighboring R groups."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-5","title":"Beta sheets: extended strands and direction","body":["Beta sheets form when extended beta strands lie alongside one another and hydrogen-bond through their backbone groups. Adjacent strands may run in parallel or antiparallel N→C directions. Side chains alternate above and below the sheet.","The strands can come from different parts of one polypeptide or, in some assemblies, from separate chains. A beta sheet is therefore a secondary-structure description and does not automatically mean a quaternary assembly. Later, pathological amyloid examples reuse beta-rich architecture in abnormal intermolecular aggregates."],"keyPoints":["Parallel and antiparallel describe relative strand orientation.","Alternating side-chain positions create two sheet faces."],"example":"A long protein can fold back on itself to form an antiparallel sheet using two regions of the same chain.","pitfalls":["Do not call every beta sheet an amyloid deposit."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-6","title":"Tertiary structure and the hydrophobic core","body":["Tertiary structure is the overall conformation of one chain. In many soluble globular proteins, nonpolar side chains tend to pack away from water while polar and charged groups are often exposed or paired in specific internal interactions. The hydrophobic effect is a major driving force in this organization.","Hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions, van der Waals contacts and sometimes disulfide bonds stabilize a particular fold. No single force explains every residue position. Membrane proteins require a different environmental interpretation because many nonpolar surfaces contact membrane lipids."],"keyPoints":["Tertiary folding organizes distant sequence regions together.","The surrounding solvent helps determine favorable side-chain placement."],"example":"Two residues far apart in primary sequence may contact each other at an enzyme active site after folding.","pitfalls":["Do not infer that every polar residue must be outside or every nonpolar residue must be buried."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":11}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-7","title":"Disulfides and noncovalent forces","body":["A disulfide bond is a covalent connection between two cysteine side chains formed by oxidation of their thiols. It can connect regions within one chain or connect different chains. Its presence must be distinguished from the many weaker interactions that usually stabilize a fold.","Hydrogen bonds, ionic contacts and van der Waals interactions are noncovalent. A reagent reducing disulfides directly changes those covalent crosslinks, whereas a denaturant affecting hydrophobic interactions need not cleave disulfides or peptide bonds. Identify which chemical force the question states is altered."],"keyPoints":["Cysteine thiols form disulfides.","Methionine cannot substitute for cysteine in this reaction."],"example":"Reducing an interchain disulfide can separate covalently linked subunits without necessarily hydrolyzing either chain.","pitfalls":["A disulfide is not a peptide bond between alpha-amino and alpha-carboxyl groups."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":11}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-8","title":"Quaternary assembly and hemoglobin","body":["Quaternary structure describes the association of separate polypeptide chains into a functional complex. Each subunit already has its own primary and tertiary structure. The assembly can use the same broad interaction classes seen within a folded chain, and some complexes also have interchain disulfides.","Hemoglobin contains multiple globin chains and illustrates why subunit interfaces matter. A change at a surface can alter interactions between molecules or subunits without eliminating every local helix. Structural questions should identify whether the affected interaction is within one chain, between subunits or between separate complete protein molecules."],"keyPoints":["Quaternary structure requires multiple chains.","Subunit interaction and abnormal intermolecular polymerization are related but distinct descriptions."],"example":"Separating a tetramer into folded monomers removes quaternary organization while the monomers may retain much of their tertiary structure.","pitfalls":["Loss of quaternary structure does not necessarily imply peptide-bond hydrolysis."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-9","title":"Folding pathways and chaperones","body":["A sequence contains information that favors its native conformation under suitable conditions, but the cell must avoid harmful nonproductive interactions while folding occurs. Local secondary structures form and pack, and exposed hydrophobic regions can otherwise associate with the wrong partners.","Chaperones bind vulnerable unfolded or partly folded regions, reduce aggregation and help productive folding. Many heat-shock proteins are chaperones, and some are expressed during ordinary growth as well as stress. They assist the process rather than supply a new amino-acid sequence or become permanent subunits of every client protein."],"keyPoints":["Chaperones limit inappropriate aggregation.","Stress can increase demand for protein-folding assistance."],"example":"A partly unfolded protein exposing hydrophobic surfaces may be protected by chaperone binding until productive folding can occur.","pitfalls":["Chaperones do not determine a protein’s amino-acid sequence."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-10:guide-10","title":"Denaturation, renaturation and Tm","body":["Denaturation is loss of a protein’s native higher-order conformation, often with loss of function. Heat, extreme pH, detergents and organic solvents can disrupt stabilizing interactions. Ordinary denaturation does not itself mean hydrolysis of the peptide backbone, although severe conditions can cause additional chemical damage.","Protein Tm is the temperature at which half the population is unfolded under the stated conditions. A higher Tm generally indicates greater thermal stability in a comparable experiment. Some proteins can refold after conditions are restored, while aggregation or chemical damage can make recovery incomplete."],"keyPoints":["Sequence can remain intact while function is lost.","Tm refers to fractional unfolding, not a protein turning into liquid."],"example":"A heat-stable polymerase has a higher useful thermal stability than many ordinary human enzymes, which supports its use in temperature-cycling experiments.","pitfalls":["Denaturation is not always fully reversible; conditions and aggregation matter."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":15}]}],"summary":["Primary structure is sequence; peptide bonds are usually retained during denaturation.","Secondary structure uses backbone hydrogen bonds.","Tertiary describes one chain, quaternary describes subunit assembly.","The native fold is stabilized by many interactions, with hydrophobic burial particularly important in soluble proteins."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-10:blank-0","prompt":"The linear amino-acid sequence is the _____ structure.","answers":["primary"],"explanation":"Sequence order is the first structural level.","concept":"MOL-10:expanded-0"},{"id":"MOL-10:blank-1","prompt":"An alpha helix is stabilized by hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O(i) and N–H(i+_____).","answers":["4","four"],"explanation":"The characteristic spacing is i to i+4.","concept":"MOL-10:expanded-3"},{"id":"MOL-10:blank-2","prompt":"The overall fold of one polypeptide is _____ structure.","answers":["tertiary"],"explanation":"Quaternary refers to association of multiple chains.","concept":"MOL-10:expanded-5"},{"id":"MOL-10:blank-3","prompt":"Assembly of separate polypeptide subunits creates _____ structure.","answers":["quaternary"],"explanation":"A monomer does not require this structural level.","concept":"MOL-10:expanded-7"},{"id":"MOL-10:blank-4","prompt":"Protein Tm is the temperature at which _____% is unfolded.","answers":["50","fifty"],"explanation":"It describes a population midpoint under stated conditions.","concept":"MOL-10:expanded-9"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-10:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Primary","definition":"Linear order of the amino acids"},{"term":"Secondary","definition":"Local arrangement of backbone helices or sheets"},{"term":"Tertiary","definition":"Overall three-dimensional conformation of one chain"},{"term":"Quaternary","definition":"Association of two or more polypeptide subunits"},{"term":"Tm","definition":"Temperature at which half the protein population is unfolded"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}],"sourceNote":"MOL 10 and MOL 11 share one 28-slide presentation. Topics are organized here by the corresponding transcript discussions; the presentation has no explicit official divider."},"MOL-11":{"overview":"Protein disease can arise from an altered sequence, an altered fold or abnormal interactions between proteins. Compare quality-control mechanisms and each named aggregate rather than treating all proteinopathies as the same disease.","objectives":["Distinguish abnormal interaction from failed folding.","Trace chaperone, ubiquitin–proteasome and autophagy responses.","Explain oligomer and amyloid formation.","Compare sickle hemoglobin, CFTR, Aβ/tau, alpha-synuclein and TTR.","Explain how a prion propagates a conformation."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-11:guide-1","title":"Three routes from sequence to disease","body":["A sequence change may preserve much of a native fold but create an abnormal surface interaction, as in sickle hemoglobin. A sequence change may instead impair folding and trafficking, as with common CFTR F508del. A protein with a normal sequence can also adopt a harmful conformation and aggregate.","These categories explain why mutation, denaturation and misfolding are not interchangeable terms. Identify the changed sequence, conformation, localization and interaction separately before predicting the loss or gain of function."],"keyPoints":["Mutation is not required for every aggregation disorder.","A protein can retain a fold yet acquire abnormal intermolecular behavior."],"example":"Sickle hemoglobin polymerization and CFTR ER retention illustrate different downstream effects of altered sequence.","pitfalls":["Do not claim every disease protein is completely unfolded."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":17}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-2","title":"Sickle hemoglobin: altered surface interaction","body":["The β-globin Glu→Val substitution replaces a charged hydrophilic side chain with a nonpolar one. Under deoxygenating conditions, this creates an interaction that favors assembly of hemoglobin S into long polymers. The abnormal fibers distort red-cell shape and contribute to impaired flow and cell damage.","This example does not require the conclusion that the whole hemoglobin molecule has lost every normal structural feature. A specific altered interaction can be enough to cause severe pathology. The oxygenation dependence helps distinguish this mechanism from generic heat denaturation."],"keyPoints":["Glu→Val is a missense substitution.","Deoxygenation favors HbS polymerization."],"example":"A surface hydrophobic patch can interact with another protein molecule rather than with water, driving abnormal assembly.","pitfalls":["Do not classify sickle mutation as a deletion or a frameshift."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":17}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-3","title":"CFTR: misfolding and failed delivery","body":["F508del removes a phenylalanine from CFTR without shifting the downstream reading frame. The altered protein is recognized by cellular quality-control machinery and is often retained and degraded rather than delivered efficiently to the apical membrane. A trafficking defect therefore reduces functional chloride-channel abundance.","The mutation illustrates why an in-frame deletion can be severe and why gene transcription alone does not guarantee a functional surface protein. Compare RNA production, folding, degradation and membrane delivery as separate steps."],"keyPoints":["F508del is an in-frame three-base deletion.","ER quality control can reduce surface channel abundance."],"example":"Normal-looking CFTR mRNA abundance can coexist with low membrane CFTR because the block is after translation.","pitfalls":["Do not attribute the common F508del mechanism to deletion of an entire chromosome."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":17}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-4","title":"Refold, degrade or remove aggregates","body":["Chaperones provide a first opportunity for an unfolded or misfolded protein to regain a useful state. Proteins that cannot be rescued may be selected for degradation through ubiquitin-dependent pathways and the proteasome. Larger aggregates and damaged material can be handled through autophagy and lysosomal degradation.","These systems have finite capacity and can become less effective or overwhelmed. Accumulation can therefore occur despite several layers of protection. Long-lived neurons are especially vulnerable to persistent damage because replacing lost cells is limited."],"keyPoints":["Chaperones assist folding.","Proteasomes and lysosomes remove different classes of material through connected pathways."],"example":"Inhibiting the proteasome can cause accumulation of selected ubiquitinated proteins rather than directly increasing their transcription.","pitfalls":["Ubiquitin is a degradation-targeting signal in this example, not the enzyme that cleaves every peptide bond."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":18}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-5","title":"From soluble oligomers to amyloid fibrils","body":["Misfolding can expose normally buried interaction surfaces. Molecules may associate into soluble oligomers, then larger intermediates and fibrils. Some oligomers are particularly toxic, and disease severity cannot be inferred merely by counting the largest visible deposits.","Amyloid fibrils have a characteristic cross-beta organization. Beta strands lie roughly across the fibril axis, while hydrogen-bonded beta-sheet organization extends along it. This architecture is shared by deposits made from different precursor proteins; it does not mean the proteins all share one amino-acid sequence."],"keyPoints":["Amyloid describes a structural aggregate class.","Different diseases can have different amyloid precursor proteins."],"example":"Aβ and TTR can both form amyloid even though their normal functions and sequences differ.","pitfalls":["Not every beta sheet in a normal protein is pathological amyloid."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":19}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-6","title":"Alzheimer disease: two different aggregates","body":["Amyloid precursor protein can be processed to produce Aβ peptides. Aggregation of Aβ can generate oligomers and extracellular plaques. Tau normally associates with neuronal microtubules; abnormal tau modification and aggregation produce intracellular neurofibrillary tangles.","These are distinct proteins in different cellular locations. Both are relevant to the disease process, and their presence should not be collapsed into the single label “amyloid inside neurons.” The molecular lesson is altered processing, interaction and aggregation, rather than a need to memorize a treatment regimen."],"keyPoints":["Aβ plaques: extracellular.","Tau tangles: intracellular."],"example":"A histology-style question naming a microtubule-associated protein points toward tau rather than APP-derived Aβ.","pitfalls":["Do not swap the plaque and tangle locations."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":21}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-7","title":"Parkinson disease and alpha-synuclein","body":["Alpha-synuclein is a neuronal protein involved in synaptic biology. Misfolding and self-association can produce oligomers and aggregates, and alpha-synuclein is a major component of Lewy bodies. This is a different precursor from tau and Aβ.","The lecture connects the process to loss of vulnerable neuronal populations and familiar motor features. For the molecular examination, link the named protein to the aggregate and the idea of impaired protein homeostasis; do not infer that every tremor or every neurodegenerative disease has the same aggregate."],"keyPoints":["Lewy bodies contain aggregated alpha-synuclein.","Protein identity distinguishes this example from Alzheimer pathology."],"example":"A question contrasting tau tangles with Lewy bodies is testing tau versus alpha-synuclein.","pitfalls":["Do not call alpha-synuclein the peptide produced by cleavage of APP."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":23}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-8","title":"Transthyretin amyloidosis","body":["Transthyretin is produced mainly by the liver and normally circulates as a tetramer that transports thyroxine and retinol-binding protein. Tetramer destabilization and dissociation can allow monomers to misfold and form amyloid. This shows how loss of a stable assembly can precede harmful aggregation.","Variant ATTR involves a pathogenic TTR sequence variant. Wild-type ATTR can occur without such a variant, especially later in life. The molecular distinction is altered sequence versus normal sequence with age-associated instability; neither can be identified simply by assuming every case is inherited."],"keyPoints":["TTR normally forms a tetramer.","Both variant and wild-type TTR can be associated with amyloid disease."],"example":"A patient can develop wild-type ATTR despite no pathogenic TTR coding variant being found.","pitfalls":["TTR carries retinol-binding protein; it is not the same protein as retinol-binding protein."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":24}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-9","title":"Prions and templated conformational conversion","body":["The normal cellular prion protein and its disease-associated counterpart differ in conformation. A misfolded conformer can promote conversion of other prion protein molecules into a similar abnormal state, generating a self-propagating cycle and aggregates. The disease-associated state is relatively beta-rich compared with the normal alpha-rich description.","A prion is an infectious protein-conformation phenomenon rather than a conventional organism containing its own nucleic-acid genome. Human prion diseases can be sporadic, inherited or acquired, so transmission from contaminated material is not the explanation of every case."],"keyPoints":["Prion propagation is templated misfolding.","An amino-acid sequence change is not required in every acquired or sporadic case."],"example":"An abnormal protein seed converting otherwise normal protein explains amplification without replication of a viral DNA genome.","pitfalls":["Human prion diseases are not all identical to bovine spongiform encephalopathy."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":25}]},{"id":"MOL-11:guide-10","title":"Comparing mechanisms without mixing names","body":["Use a mechanism table mentally: HbS involves abnormal interaction after Glu→Val; common CFTR F508del involves folding and trafficking; Alzheimer disease features Aβ and tau; Parkinson disease features alpha-synuclein; ATTR features TTR destabilization; prion disease features self-propagating conformational conversion.","For an unfamiliar vignette, identify the protein, whether the sequence changed, the relevant compartment and the quality-control failure. The shared theme is sequence/structure/function, but the causal route differs. This approach is more reliable than memorizing a disease name next to the vague word “misfolding.”"],"keyPoints":["Name the specific protein before naming the aggregate.","Distinguish loss of normal function from toxicity of accumulated material."],"example":"An aggregate made from a tetrameric plasma transport protein suggests TTR, whereas a cell-surface neural protein that templates misfolding suggests prion protein.","pitfalls":["Do not treat the presence of aggregates as proof that a primary-sequence mutation is present."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-10-11","page":27}]}],"summary":["Misfolding can occur with a normal or altered sequence.","Soluble oligomers can be toxic; visible fibrils are not the only relevant species.","Aβ plaques are extracellular, tau tangles intracellular.","Prion propagation transmits a conformation, not a new DNA genome."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-11:blank-0","prompt":"The sickle β-globin substitution changes glutamate to _____.","answers":["valine","Val"],"explanation":"The new nonpolar surface group promotes abnormal interactions.","concept":"MOL-11:expanded-1"},{"id":"MOL-11:blank-1","prompt":"Proteins selected for proteasomal degradation are commonly tagged with _____.","answers":["ubiquitin"],"explanation":"The tag helps select the substrate for degradation.","concept":"MOL-11:expanded-3"},{"id":"MOL-11:blank-2","prompt":"Alzheimer tau tangles occur _____ neurons.","answers":["inside","within","intracellularly"],"explanation":"Aβ plaques are the classic extracellular counterpart.","concept":"MOL-11:expanded-5"},{"id":"MOL-11:blank-3","prompt":"Lewy bodies contain aggregated alpha-_____.","answers":["synuclein","α-synuclein"],"explanation":"This differs from tau and APP-derived Aβ.","concept":"MOL-11:expanded-6"},{"id":"MOL-11:blank-4","prompt":"Normal plasma transthyretin is a _____.","answers":["tetramer"],"explanation":"Dissociation can permit monomer misfolding and amyloid formation.","concept":"MOL-11:expanded-7"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-11:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"HbS","definition":"Hemoglobin with abnormal deoxygenation-dependent polymerization"},{"term":"CFTR F508del","definition":"A chloride-channel variant with misfolding and reduced surface delivery"},{"term":"Aβ","definition":"APP-derived peptide associated with extracellular plaques"},{"term":"Tau","definition":"Microtubule-associated protein forming intracellular tangles"},{"term":"Alpha-synuclein","definition":"Protein that is a major component of Lewy bodies"},{"term":"Prion","definition":"Protein conformer that templates further misfolding"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}],"sourceNote":"MOL 10 and MOL 11 share one 28-slide presentation. Topics are organized here by the corresponding transcript discussions; the presentation has no explicit official divider."},"MOL-12":{"overview":"Protein localization requires encoded signals and recognition machinery. Follow cargo from free ribosomes or ER entry to its destination, and use trafficking diseases to understand what fails when a signal or quality-control step is disrupted.","objectives":["Assign signals and machinery to ER, nucleus, mitochondria and peroxisomes.","Trace ER→Golgi→destination traffic.","Compare N- and O-linked glycosylation.","Explain M6P and I-cell disease.","Distinguish constitutive and regulated secretion and major trafficking disease examples."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-12:guide-1","title":"Free ribosomes and ER-bound ribosomes","body":["Ribosomes are redirected according to the signal encoded by the protein they are making. Translation begins in the cytosol. Cytosolic proteins and many proteins destined for the nucleus, mitochondria or peroxisomes are synthesized on free ribosomes and imported afterward.","Many proteins entering the endomembrane system or being secreted are redirected to the rough ER while synthesis continues. Free and bound ribosomes are not permanently different genetic species of ribosome; their association changes with the cargo being translated."],"keyPoints":["Destination information is in the cargo sequence.","Free ribosomes can synthesize proteins for several destinations."],"example":"A nuclear transcription factor can be fully synthesized in the cytosol before being imported through a nuclear pore.","pitfalls":["Do not assume every protein made on a free ribosome must stay in the cytosol."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-2","title":"SRP and cotranslational ER entry","body":["An emerging hydrophobic ER signal sequence is recognized by signal recognition particle. SRP temporarily pauses translation and helps direct the ribosome–nascent-chain complex to the SRP receptor at the ER. The chain is then transferred to a translocon and translation continues with entry into or insertion into the ER.","For a typical soluble secreted cargo with a cleavable N-terminal signal peptide, signal peptidase removes the signal. Membrane proteins can use additional signal-anchor and stop-transfer sequences, so the simple soluble-cargo diagram should not be treated as the only topology possible."],"keyPoints":["SRP recognizes the nascent signal.","Translocon provides the protein-conducting channel."],"example":"Deleting a necessary ER signal can cause a normally secreted soluble protein to remain in the cytosol.","pitfalls":["SRP is not the protease that removes the signal peptide."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":7}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-3","title":"Folding, disulfides and ER quality control","body":["The ER lumen provides a setting for secretory-protein folding, chaperone action and disulfide formation. BiP and related quality-control machinery help assess whether cargo has reached a suitable conformation. Misfolded proteins are retained rather than indiscriminately sent onward.","ER-associated degradation moves selected defective cargo back toward the cytosol, where ubiquitin/proteasome machinery degrades it. This explains how a post-translational quality-control process can reduce surface protein abundance despite a gene still being expressed."],"keyPoints":["Correct folding helps cargo progress.","ERAD connects ER quality control with cytosolic degradation."],"example":"F508del CFTR can be retained and degraded rather than reaching the membrane efficiently.","pitfalls":["The ER does not simply package every translated chain for immediate secretion."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-4","title":"N-linked and O-linked glycosylation","body":["N-linked glycosylation begins in the ER with attachment of an oligosaccharide to selected asparagine residues in an appropriate sequence context, commonly Asn-X-Ser/Thr with X not proline. The N refers to the nitrogen that links the sugar to the Asn side chain.","O-linked glycosylation commonly adds sugars to serine or threonine hydroxyls in the Golgi. Glycans can affect folding, stability, recognition and targeting. These modifications are enzymatic and must be distinguished from nonenzymatic glycation such as HbA1c formation."],"keyPoints":["N-linked: Asn and ER initiation.","O-linked: commonly Ser/Thr and Golgi processing."],"example":"A glycan attached to an Asn amide nitrogen is N-linked even if the residue is far from the protein N terminus.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse glycosylation with spontaneous glucose attachment to hemoglobin."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-5","title":"Golgi traffic and ER retrieval","body":["Cargo leaves the ER in transport carriers, reaches the cis-Golgi and progresses through processing toward the trans-Golgi network. The TGN sorts cargo into routes for secretion, plasma membrane or endosomal/lysosomal delivery.","Soluble ER-resident proteins with a C-terminal KDEL signal can be retrieved after escape to the Golgi. Recognition of the retention/retrieval signal and retrograde traffic return them to their working compartment. COPI-mediated retrieval and forward secretory transport therefore serve different directional roles."],"keyPoints":["Cis is the receiving Golgi side; trans/TGN sorts onward cargo.","KDEL helps retrieve soluble ER-resident proteins."],"example":"An ER chaperone missing its retrieval signal may escape its normal compartment more readily.","pitfalls":["Do not call KDEL a lysosomal M6P tag."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-6","title":"Mannose-6-phosphate and lysosomal hydrolases","body":["Soluble lysosomal enzymes first enter the secretory pathway and acquire a mannose-6-phosphate targeting modification during Golgi processing. M6P receptors recognize the tag and help package enzymes for delivery toward endosomes and lysosomes.","The signal is a modified carbohydrate on a protein rather than a short amino-acid nuclear localization sequence. The same enzyme protein can be synthesized normally yet delivered incorrectly if the tag or its recognition is missing. Location is therefore part of enzyme function in a cell."],"keyPoints":["M6P sorts many soluble lysosomal hydrolases.","Tagging and catalysis are separate properties of the enzyme cargo."],"example":"A hydrolase secreted into blood cannot efficiently digest its intended substrate inside a lysosome.","pitfalls":["Do not say all lysosomal proteins use M6P; the teaching pathway specifically concerns soluble hydrolases."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":10}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-7","title":"I-cell disease as a sorting defect","body":["Mucolipidosis II, or I-cell disease, involves a defect in the phosphotransferase machinery required to generate the M6P recognition mark. Many lysosomal enzymes therefore miss their destination and are secreted, leaving lysosomes deficient in multiple hydrolases.","Undegraded material accumulates in intracellular inclusions, while activities of selected lysosomal enzymes can be elevated outside the cell. This differs from a single enzyme’s catalytic deficiency in a mucopolysaccharidosis. Ask whether the problem is one missing activity or misdelivery of many otherwise active enzymes."],"keyPoints":["Missing M6P tagging causes broad hydrolase misrouting.","Multiple lysosomal substrates can accumulate."],"example":"High extracellular lysosomal enzyme activity with intracellular storage supports a targeting problem rather than absence of every enzyme’s synthesis.","pitfalls":["Do not identify I-cell disease as a defect of the nuclear pore."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":12}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-8","title":"Nuclear localization and Ran","body":["Many nuclear proteins contain a basic nuclear localization signal enriched in residues such as lysine and arginine. Import receptors recognize the signal and move cargo through nuclear pores. The NLS is generally retained rather than cleaved like a typical ER signal peptide.","Ran nucleotide-state organization helps make transport directional. In the nucleus, Ran-GTP promotes release of import cargo from the receptor, and the receptor is recycled. Nuclear pores can transport folded cargo, distinguishing this route from the usual mitochondrial matrix-import example."],"keyPoints":["NLS: basic amino-acid sequence.","Importin recognizes cargo; Ran helps control transport direction."],"example":"Adding an appropriate NLS can redirect an otherwise cytosolic reporter toward the nucleus.","pitfalls":["Do not require every nuclear protein to unfold and lose its NLS before entry."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-9","title":"Mitochondrial protein import","body":["Most mitochondrial proteins are encoded by nuclear genes and synthesized in the cytosol. Many matrix-directed precursors carry an N-terminal amphipathic helix. Chaperones help keep precursors import-competent, and translocases in outer and inner membranes participate in delivery.","TOM and TIM complexes are central to the lecture’s import pathway. ATP and the inner-membrane potential support import; matrix-targeting sequences are often removed after entry. Different mitochondrial destinations can require additional signals and machinery, so not every protein follows one identical path to the matrix."],"keyPoints":["TOM: outer-membrane import machinery.","TIM: inner-membrane translocation routes."],"example":"A precursor unable to remain unfolded can fail efficient import even when its targeting sequence is intact.","pitfalls":["An amphipathic mitochondrial signal is not identical to a highly hydrophobic ER signal peptide."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":14}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-10","title":"Peroxisomal targeting and Zellweger syndrome","body":["A common peroxisomal targeting signal is a C-terminal tripeptide exemplified by Ser-Lys-Leu, recognized by a receptor such as PEX5. This is a post-translational route and differs from cotranslational entry into ER. Peroxisomes can import selected folded cargo.","Defects in peroxisome biogenesis machinery can impair delivery of many proteins, as in Zellweger spectrum disorders. The metabolic consequences include disturbed very-long-chain fatty-acid handling and impaired plasmalogen synthesis. Distinguish failure of an organelle’s assembly/import system from a single cargo lacking its signal."],"keyPoints":["PTS1 is commonly C-terminal.","Peroxisomal import is distinct from TOM/TIM mitochondrial import."],"example":"Changing a necessary C-terminal SKL signal can disrupt peroxisomal localization of that cargo.","pitfalls":["Do not describe PTS1 as an N-terminal cleavable ER signal peptide."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":16}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-11","title":"Constitutive versus regulated secretion","body":["Constitutive secretion sends cargo outward continuously without requiring storage in stimulus-responsive granules. Regulated secretion first stores concentrated cargo and then releases it after an appropriate signal, often involving calcium or cyclic-nucleotide pathways. Both depend on membrane traffic and fusion.","Insulin and digestive-enzyme precursors are common regulated examples; collagen and albumin are conventional constitutive examples. Proteins lacking organelle-targeting information do not all default to secretion: many remain cytosolic. The secretory pathway generally requires prior ER entry."],"keyPoints":["Regulated cargo is stored before signal-triggered release.","Cytosolic residence is not the same as constitutive secretion."],"example":"A beta cell can release stored insulin rapidly after stimulation without needing to synthesize every released molecule at that moment.","pitfalls":["The source’s broad “all non-organelle proteins are secreted” statement needs this cytosolic-protein exception."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":18}]},{"id":"MOL-12:guide-12","title":"Trafficking diseases and source corrections","body":["Different trafficking defects connect the same pathway to different tissues. Some LDL-receptor variants impair folding or cell-surface delivery and contribute to poor LDL clearance. Misfolded alpha-1 antitrypsin can accumulate in hepatocyte ER while reduced secretion deprives the lung of antiprotease protection.","The lecture’s HFE example concerns association with beta-2 microglobulin, not “macroglobulin.” A common C282Y variant disrupts normal processing and surface expression, contributing to impaired iron-regulatory signaling. These examples illustrate that intracellular accumulation and deficiency at the destination can coexist."],"keyPoints":["A retained protein can cause toxicity at its source and deficiency at its destination.","HFE’s partner is beta-2 microglobulin."],"example":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin retention helps explain both liver accumulation and reduced circulating protection of the lung.","pitfalls":["Not every LDLR variant has the same trafficking defect; some alter other receptor functions."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":19}],"furtherReading":[{"title":"HFE and beta-2 microglobulin · original research","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9356458/"}]}],"summary":["All cytosolic translation starts on free ribosomes; ER-targeted chains are redirected by their signal.","ER entry, nuclear entry, mitochondrial import and peroxisomal import use different signals.","M6P sorts lysosomal hydrolases.","Constitutive secretion is continuous; regulated secretion uses stored cargo and a trigger."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-12:blank-0","prompt":"The nascent ER signal peptide is recognized by _____.","answers":["SRP","signal recognition particle"],"explanation":"SRP directs the complex toward the ER receptor.","concept":"MOL-12:expanded-1"},{"id":"MOL-12:blank-1","prompt":"A soluble ER-resident protein can carry a retrieval signal called _____.","answers":["KDEL"],"explanation":"It helps retrieve escaped cargo from Golgi.","concept":"MOL-12:expanded-4"},{"id":"MOL-12:blank-2","prompt":"The lysosomal hydrolase targeting carbohydrate tag is _____.","answers":["M6P","mannose-6-phosphate","mannose 6 phosphate"],"explanation":"The receptor recognizes this modification for sorting.","concept":"MOL-12:expanded-5"},{"id":"MOL-12:blank-3","prompt":"A basic NLS is recognized by the nuclear import receptor _____.","answers":["importin","nuclear import receptor"],"explanation":"Ran helps regulate directional transport and cargo release.","concept":"MOL-12:expanded-7"},{"id":"MOL-12:blank-4","prompt":"A common C-terminal peroxisomal PTS1 sequence is Ser-Lys-_____.","answers":["Leu","leucine"],"explanation":"SKL is the common teaching example.","concept":"MOL-12:expanded-9"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-12:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"ER signal peptide","definition":"Nascent signal recognized by SRP for ER entry"},{"term":"NLS","definition":"Basic sequence used in nuclear import"},{"term":"M6P","definition":"Carbohydrate tag on many soluble lysosomal hydrolases"},{"term":"PTS1","definition":"Common C-terminal peroxisomal targeting signal"},{"term":"KDEL","definition":"Retrieval signal on soluble ER-resident proteins"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}],"corrections":[{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":22,"note":"The HFE partner is beta-2 microglobulin, correcting the slide’s “macroglobulin” wording.","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC24956/"},{"source":"mol-slides-12","page":18,"note":"Proteins lacking organelle-targeting signals do not all default to secretion; many remain cytosolic."}]},"MOL-13":{"overview":"Carbohydrates serve as fuels, storage polymers, recognition structures and hydrated extracellular materials. Learn stereochemistry and glycosidic links before comparing storage polysaccharides, glycoproteins, proteoglycans and GAG degradation disorders.","objectives":["Classify monosaccharides and isomers.","Explain anomeric carbon and reducing sugars.","Distinguish glycation from glycosylation.","Compare amylose, amylopectin and glycogen.","Identify GAG properties, examples and degradation disorders."],"sections":[{"id":"MOL-13:guide-1","title":"Functions and classification of carbohydrates","body":["Monosaccharides are single sugar units, oligosaccharides contain short linked chains and polysaccharides contain longer chains. Carbohydrates supply energy, store fuel, contribute to structure and participate in cell-surface recognition. Their carbonyl and hydroxyl groups contribute to polarity and chemical reactivity.","Sugars can be classified by carbon number and carbonyl type. An aldose has an aldehyde-derived carbonyl in its open-chain form; a ketose has a ketone-derived carbonyl. Glucose and fructose can have the same molecular formula yet differ in connectivity and chemistry."],"keyPoints":["Carbon number and aldose/ketose classification are independent features.","Carbohydrate functions extend beyond ATP production."],"example":"Glucose is an aldohexose and fructose is a ketohexose.","pitfalls":["The empirical (CH2O)n pattern is useful but not a universal formula for every modified carbohydrate."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":2}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-2","title":"Ring formation and the anomeric carbon","body":["An open-chain sugar can cyclize when a hydroxyl reacts with its carbonyl group, forming a hemiacetal or hemiketal. The former carbonyl carbon becomes the anomeric carbon and acquires a new stereochemical distinction. In glucose the anomeric carbon is C1; in fructose it is C2.","Alpha and beta anomers differ at that anomeric center. For a standard Haworth drawing of D-glucose, the relative orientation of the anomeric OH and the reference CH2OH group distinguishes alpha from beta. Free anomers can interconvert through the open-chain form."],"keyPoints":["Anomeric carbon derives from the carbonyl carbon.","Alpha/beta is distinct from D/L."],"example":"Alpha-D-glucose and beta-D-glucose are anomers, not mirror-image enantiomers.","pitfalls":["Do not label every sugar’s anomeric carbon as C1."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":4}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-3","title":"Isomers, epimers and enantiomers","body":["Structural isomers differ in connectivity. Stereoisomers have the same connectivity but differ in spatial arrangement. Epimers differ at one stereogenic center; glucose and galactose are C4 epimers, while glucose and mannose are C2 epimers.","Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images. D/L nomenclature concerns configuration at the reference chiral center and is separate from alpha/beta configuration at the anomeric center. Enzymes can distinguish these small geometric differences, making stereochemistry biologically consequential."],"keyPoints":["Anomers are a special stereochemical relationship at the anomeric center.","Epimers differ at just one specified stereocenter."],"example":"D-glucose and D-galactose differ at C4 yet are both D sugars.","pitfalls":["Do not treat D/L and alpha/beta as interchangeable labels."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":5}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-4","title":"Reducing sugars and glycosidic bonds","body":["A sugar with an available anomeric center can interconvert with an open-chain form and show reducing behavior in appropriate chemical tests. Many monosaccharides and several disaccharides are reducing sugars. A glycosidic bond that locks both relevant anomeric centers can prevent this behavior.","Sucrose is nonreducing because its glucose and fructose anomeric centers participate in the linkage. Lactose and maltose retain a free reducing end. Benedict testing detects reducing substances and is not a uniquely glucose-specific clinical measurement."],"keyPoints":["Free anomeric center supports reducing behavior.","Sucrose is the classic nonreducing disaccharide example."],"example":"A disaccharide with one anomeric carbon still available can still be reducing despite containing a glycosidic bond.","pitfalls":["Do not state that every disaccharide is nonreducing."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":7}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-5","title":"HbA1c and nonenzymatic glycation","body":["Glucose can react nonenzymatically with hemoglobin to form glycated hemoglobin. HbA1c therefore reflects glycemic exposure over the preceding months rather than a single meal or a momentary blood-glucose value. Formation depends on exposure and red-cell biology.","Glycation differs from enzyme-directed glycosylation that builds defined glycans on proteins. The slide’s mixed “mM/dL” expression and single HbA1c target are not a reliable universal clinical target. For this lesson, learn the chemical reaction and why the measurement integrates time; individual clinical targets require context."],"keyPoints":["HbA1c formation is nonenzymatic.","A single glucose value and HbA1c describe different time windows."],"example":"A transient meal-related glucose rise does not mean the entire HbA1c result immediately changes to match that concentration.","pitfalls":["Conditions changing red-cell survival can affect interpretation of HbA1c."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":7}],"furtherReading":[{"title":"HbA1c and individualized targets · NIDDK","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diagnostic-tests/a1c-test"}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-6","title":"Oligosaccharides and cell recognition","body":["Glycoproteins and glycolipids expose carbohydrate structures at the cell surface. Lectins recognize particular glycan patterns, supporting adhesion, recognition and targeting. Sugar order, linkage and branching can create information distinct from the amino-acid sequence of the carrier.","The same recognition principle appears in selectin-mediated interactions, pathogen attachment and M6P-receptor binding to lysosomal enzymes. A glycan can therefore be a targeting signal rather than merely an energy reserve."],"keyPoints":["Lectins bind selected carbohydrate structures.","Glycans can function as recognition labels."],"example":"The M6P receptor recognizes a modified glycan on lysosomal-enzyme cargo.","pitfalls":["Not every extracellular sugar chain is a dietary storage polysaccharide."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":3}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-7","title":"Amylose, amylopectin and glycogen","body":["Amylose is mainly a linear glucose polymer with alpha(1→4) links. Amylopectin has alpha(1→4) chains with alpha(1→6) branch points. Glycogen uses the same broad linkage types but has more frequent branching than amylopectin. Liver and muscle glycogen serve different physiological fuel roles.","Branching changes polymer organization and the number of accessible chain ends. Food digestion also depends on processing, particle structure and other meal components, so a simple amylose/amylopectin comparison does not establish a universal health outcome for every food."],"keyPoints":["Alpha(1→6) marks a branch point.","Glycogen is more densely branched than amylopectin."],"example":"A glucose polymer containing no alpha(1→6) branch links fits the basic amylose model.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse alpha(1→4) storage polymers with beta-linked cellulose."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":9}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-8","title":"Dietary carbohydrate structure and digestion","body":["Digestive enzymes recognize particular glycosidic linkages and substrate structures. Differences in accessibility, branching and physical organization can change the rate at which digestion yields absorbable products. Soluble fiber can also affect viscosity and nutrient delivery.","These principles explain why equal carbohydrate mass need not produce identical absorption patterns. They do not justify claiming that one named ingredient always causes obesity or that a structural label alone determines the glucose response of an entire mixed meal. Separate the molecular mechanism from an overgeneralized dietary claim."],"keyPoints":["Enzyme specificity depends on linkage and substrate access.","Food context affects physiological response."],"example":"Two starch-containing meals can differ in digestion rate because one has a different physical matrix even at a similar total carbohydrate amount.","pitfalls":["Do not infer a diagnosis or a universal dietary prescription from a single starch diagram."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":8}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-9","title":"GAG composition and negative charge","body":["Glycosaminoglycans are long, generally unbranched polysaccharides with repeating disaccharide units. The repeat commonly contains an amino sugar and a uronic acid. Carboxylate and sulfate groups make many GAGs strongly negative at physiological pH.","There are important exceptions: keratan sulfate uses galactose rather than a uronic acid, and hyaluronan is unsulfated. These differences do not remove them from the GAG family. The negative charge promotes hydration and interactions with proteins and ions."],"keyPoints":["Most GAGs contain amino sugar plus uronic acid.","Keratan sulfate and hyaluronan are important exceptions to common patterns."],"example":"A sulfated GAG containing galactose and GlcNAc rather than uronic acid is consistent with keratan sulfate.","pitfalls":["Do not define GAGs so rigidly that keratan sulfate or hyaluronan is excluded."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":13}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-10","title":"Hydration, resilience and extracellular matrix","body":["Negative groups attract counterions and water, creating a highly hydrated matrix. GAG-containing structures can resist compression: fluid is displaced under load and re-enters as the load is removed, while charge-related repulsion contributes to expansion.","This supports lubrication, tissue hydration and mechanical resilience in locations such as joints, cartilage and the vitreous. The functional link is charge→hydration→material behavior. GAGs are not hard mineral rods; their water-rich organization helps explain their unusual mechanical properties."],"keyPoints":["Water is central to GAG mechanical function.","Negative charge supports a hydrated, expanded structure."],"example":"Cartilage proteoglycans help resist compressive loading because their GAG chains retain a large hydrated volume.","pitfalls":["High viscosity and compressive resilience are not explained by a hydrophobic GAG core."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":14}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-11","title":"Named GAGs and distinguishing features","body":["Hyaluronan is a large unsulfated GAG important in hydrated extracellular matrices and joint lubrication. Chondroitin sulfate is abundant in cartilage-associated proteoglycans. Dermatan sulfate and keratan sulfate occur in selected tissues, with keratan’s composition providing a useful distinction.","Heparin has a highly sulfated structure and anticoagulant activity through antithrombin-related interactions. Heparan sulfate is structurally related but has different sulfation patterns and broad cell-surface/ECM signaling roles. Similar names do not make all GAGs interchangeable in function."],"keyPoints":["Hyaluronan: unsulfated hydration/lubrication example.","Heparin: anticoagulant example."],"example":"A question emphasizing a noncovalently associated long matrix backbone and absence of sulfate points toward hyaluronan.","pitfalls":["Do not assign heparin’s anticoagulant role to every GAG."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":16}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-12","title":"Proteoglycans, glycoproteins and assembly","body":["A proteoglycan consists of a core protein with covalently attached GAG chains. Many proteoglycans have a large carbohydrate fraction and help organize extracellular matrices and signaling. Glycoproteins commonly carry shorter and often branched oligosaccharides; glycolipids carry sugar on lipid rather than protein.","Hyaluronan is not covalently attached to a core protein like an ordinary proteoglycan GAG chain. It can instead form large noncovalent assemblies with proteoglycans and linking components. Synthesis of many sulfated GAGs occurs during secretory-pathway processing, whereas hyaluronan is synthesized at the plasma membrane."],"keyPoints":["Proteoglycan means core protein plus GAG chains.","Hyaluronan can support noncovalent proteoglycan aggregates."],"example":"A membrane lipid carrying an external oligosaccharide is a glycolipid, not a protein-core proteoglycan.","pitfalls":["Do not say every GAG is synthesized and attached to a protein in exactly the same way."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":19}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-13","title":"Lysosomal degradation and storage","body":["Extracellular matrix components are turned over and degraded through pathways that include lysosomal enzymes. GAG breakdown requires sequential actions of specific hydrolases and sulfatases. Loss of a needed step can cause partially degraded material to accumulate.","Mucopolysaccharidoses are disorders involving deficient GAG degradation. Their biochemical logic differs from I-cell disease, where many hydrolases are misdirected because of defective tagging. Both can cause storage, but the location of the defect differs."],"keyPoints":["A missing degradation step causes substrate accumulation.","Catalytic enzyme deficiency differs from broad targeting failure."],"example":"A single GAG-degrading enzyme defect need not cause high blood levels of multiple hydrolases through secretion.","pitfalls":["The word storage does not identify one universal biochemical lesion."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":21}]},{"id":"MOL-13:guide-14","title":"Hurler, Scheie and Hunter comparisons","body":["MPS I results from alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency, with Hurler and Scheie representing different severities on a clinical spectrum. Accumulated dermatan and heparan sulfate contribute to manifestations. MPS II, Hunter syndrome, involves iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency and is X-linked.","The classic course comparison includes corneal clouding in Hurler and its usual absence in Hunter. These features support the comparison but should be combined with enzyme identity and inheritance rather than treated as a complete standalone diagnosis."],"keyPoints":["MPS I: alpha-L-iduronidase.","MPS II: iduronate-2-sulfatase, X-linked."],"example":"A vignette specifying iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency points to Hunter even if it does not list every classic clinical feature.","pitfalls":["Hurler and Hunter are similar names with different enzyme defects."],"references":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":24}]}],"summary":["Glucose/galactose are C4 epimers.","A free anomeric center permits reducing behavior.","HbA1c reflects nonenzymatic glycation.","GAG negative charge attracts water and supports resistance to compression.","Hyaluronan is unsulfated; keratan sulfate lacks a uronic acid in its repeat."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"MOL-13:blank-0","prompt":"Glucose and galactose are _____ epimers.","answers":["C4","carbon 4","4"],"explanation":"Their configuration differs at carbon 4.","concept":"MOL-13:expanded-2"},{"id":"MOL-13:blank-1","prompt":"The former carbonyl carbon becomes the _____ carbon after ring formation.","answers":["anomeric"],"explanation":"This center defines alpha/beta anomers.","concept":"MOL-13:expanded-1"},{"id":"MOL-13:blank-2","prompt":"HbA1c is produced by nonenzymatic _____.","answers":["glycation"],"explanation":"This differs from enzymatic glycosylation.","concept":"MOL-13:expanded-4"},{"id":"MOL-13:blank-3","prompt":"The unsulfated GAG emphasized in synovial fluid is _____.","answers":["hyaluronan","hyaluronic acid"],"explanation":"Its negative carboxylates still support hydration.","concept":"MOL-13:expanded-10"},{"id":"MOL-13:blank-4","prompt":"Hunter syndrome involves deficiency of iduronate-2-_____.","answers":["sulfatase"],"explanation":"MPS II differs from MPS I alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency.","concept":"MOL-13:expanded-13"}],"matching":[{"id":"MOL-13:matching-0","title":"Match the mechanism to the term","pairs":[{"term":"Amylose","definition":"Mainly linear glucose polymer with alpha(1→4) links"},{"term":"Amylopectin","definition":"Plant glucose polymer with alpha(1→4) chains and alpha(1→6) branches"},{"term":"Glycogen","definition":"Animal glucose polymer with more frequent branches than amylopectin"},{"term":"Hyaluronan","definition":"Unsulfated GAG forming hydrated extracellular matrices"},{"term":"Heparin","definition":"Highly sulfated GAG with anticoagulant activity"},{"term":"Proteoglycan","definition":"Core protein bearing covalently attached GAG chains"}],"explanation":"Use the defining chemical role or location of each term. Read the relevant guide section again for any pair you mixed up."}],"corrections":[{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":7,"note":"The mixed mM/dL expression and single HbA1c target are not used as universal clinical targets. HbA1c integrates glycemic exposure over preceding months.","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diagnostic-tests/a1c-test/"},{"source":"mol-slides-13","page":15,"note":"GAG repeat-unit rules have exceptions: keratan sulfate lacks uronic acid, and hyaluronan is unsulfated.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK579925/"}]},"CVP-P1":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain how the impulse reaches the ventricles.","Explain automaticity: the nodal action potential.","Explain working ventricular cells: phases 0–4.","Explain why cardiac muscle cannot normally be tetanized.","Explain conduction delay, escape rhythms and hierarchy.","Explain from cellular action potentials to the surface ECG."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-P1:week1-2","concept":"CVP-P1:2","title":"How the impulse reaches the ventricles","body":["Most cardiomyocytes generate force; a small specialized population initiates or conducts excitation. The SA node normally starts the impulse. It spreads through atrial muscle and reaches the AV node, where conduction slows. The AV bundle crosses the insulating fibrous skeleton, divides into right and left branches, and distributes excitation through Purkinje fibers to ventricular muscle.","The delay gives atria time to activate before the ventricles. Rapid ventricular distribution then coordinates pumping. Gap junctions connect the cytoplasm of neighboring cells electrically; the cells remain individual cells rather than one fused cell."],"keyPoints":["SA → atria → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje network.","AV nodal delay and rapid Purkinje conduction serve different purposes."],"example":"A delay before ventricular activation is useful; slow, patchy spread throughout the ventricles is not the same useful delay.","pitfalls":["An autonomic nerve modifies the pacemaker; it does not normally initiate each heartbeat."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":8},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":10},{"source":"cvp-t-p1","page":29}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-P1-focus-2","kind":"lecturer","label":"Sequence and approximate activation timing","evidence":"These numbers are not for you to memorize ... memorize 0.16 sec ... Purkinje fibers ... fastest velocity.","teaching":"Retain the approximately0.16s SA-to-early-ventricular teaching model; do not equate all of it with AV nodal delay.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p1","page":29}}]},{"id":"CVP-P1:week1-1","concept":"CVP-P1:1","title":"Automaticity: the nodal action potential","body":["SA nodal cells have no stable phase-4 resting potential. After repolarization, hyperpolarization-activated funny channels contribute net inward current, while declining outward potassium current and calcium entry help the membrane approach threshold. L-type calcium channels produce the main nodal phase-0 upstroke. Potassium efflux then repolarizes the cell.","The maximum diastolic potential is less negative than in a working ventricular myocyte. Many fast sodium channels are consequently unavailable. The slope of diastolic depolarization helps determine when threshold is reached and therefore the firing rate."],"keyPoints":["SA nodal phase 4 is spontaneous, not flat.","Nodal phase 0 is mainly calcium-mediated; ventricular phase 0 is mainly fast sodium-mediated."],"example":"If a nodal cell reaches threshold sooner after each repolarization, its cycle is shorter and the heart rate rises.","pitfalls":["The funny current is a mixed cation current with net inward flow at diastolic voltages, not simply a calcium plateau."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":20},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":21}]},{"id":"CVP-P1:week1-0","concept":"CVP-P1:0","title":"Working ventricular cells: phases 0–4","body":["A ventricular myocyte rests near −85 to −90 mV. Phase 0 is rapid sodium entry, phase 1 is initial repolarization, phase 2 is the plateau, phase 3 is final repolarization, and phase 4 is the stable resting interval. During the plateau, inward L-type calcium current offsets outward potassium current. When calcium current declines and potassium efflux predominates, repolarization proceeds.","The plateau is a balance of currents, not a moment when no ions move. Calcium entry is also the trigger linking the electrical signal to calcium release and contraction in the next lesson."],"keyPoints":["Fast sodium → upstroke; calcium balanced by potassium → plateau.","Predominant outward potassium current → phase-3 repolarization."],"example":"A flat-looking voltage trace during phase 2 can coexist with substantial inward and outward currents of similar size.","pitfalls":["Do not transfer nodal phase numbers and ion mechanisms uncritically to working ventricular cells."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":23},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":24},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":25}]},{"id":"CVP-P1:week1-3","concept":"CVP-P1:3","title":"Why cardiac muscle cannot normally be tetanized","body":["Excitation opens and then inactivates voltage-gated channels. During much of the cardiac action potential, these channels have not recovered sufficiently to support another propagated response: this is the effective refractory period. Its long duration overlaps most of the twitch, limiting temporal summation and sustained tetanic contraction.","A heart must alternate contraction and relaxation to fill and eject. A sustained contraction would impair filling. Late in repolarization a stronger stimulus may evoke a response during relative refractoriness, so the entire electrical cycle is not absolutely inexcitable."],"keyPoints":["Long action potential → long refractory period overlapping contraction.","Relaxation between beats permits filling."],"example":"Skeletal muscle can be stimulated repeatedly during a twitch; a ventricular cell remains refractory for much of its twitch.","pitfalls":["Refractoriness does not mean the heart never responds to premature impulses."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":26}]},{"id":"CVP-P1:week1-4","concept":"CVP-P1:4","title":"Conduction delay, escape rhythms and hierarchy","body":["The fastest normal pacemaker suppresses slower latent pacemakers. If atrial impulses fail to reach the ventricles, a subsidiary focus can eventually generate an escape rhythm. The His–Purkinje system has a slower intrinsic rate than the SA node; a prolonged pause before escape can temporarily reduce cerebral perfusion.","Distinguish failure to generate an impulse, failure to conduct an impulse, and takeover by an ectopic focus. All can change rhythm, but a visible atrial rhythm with an independent slow ventricular rhythm points to an atrioventricular conduction problem."],"keyPoints":["SA activity normally outruns subsidiary pacemakers.","An escape beat follows a missed impulse; a premature beat arrives early."],"example":"Continued P waves with a much slower unrelated QRS rhythm suggest that atria and ventricles have separate electrical clocks.","pitfalls":["An escape rhythm does not mean that the normal AV conduction pathway has recovered."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":27},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":28}]},{"id":"CVP-P1:week1-5","concept":"CVP-P1:5","title":"From cellular action potentials to the surface ECG","body":["The surface ECG records changing extracellular potential differences generated by many cardiac cells. It is not a recording of one cell’s transmembrane action potential and is not a direct pressure or contraction trace. P is atrial depolarization; QRS is ventricular depolarization; T is ventricular repolarization. Atrial repolarization is usually hidden within QRS.","A flat segment need not mean electrical inactivity: uniformly depolarized tissue can produce little net voltage difference at the body surface. Mechanical contraction follows electrical activation, rather than being identical to a wave on the ECG."],"keyPoints":["P, QRS and T describe electrical events.","Atrial repolarization is normally obscured by QRS."],"example":"The ST segment may lie near baseline while ventricular myocytes remain in their depolarized plateau phase.","pitfalls":["A positive T wave is repolarization, not a second ventricular depolarization."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":29},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":30},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":31},{"source":"cvp-slides-p1","page":32}]}],"summary":["SA → atria → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje network.","SA nodal phase 4 is spontaneous, not flat.","Fast sodium → upstroke; calcium balanced by potassium → plateau.","Long action potential → long refractory period overlapping contraction.","SA activity normally outruns subsidiary pacemakers.","P, QRS and T describe electrical events."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-P1-w1-blank-0","prompt":"The main inward current of the SA nodal phase-0 upstroke is carried by ___.","answers":["calcium","Ca2+","Ca²⁺"],"explanation":"Nodal phase 0 is primarily L-type calcium entry."},{"id":"CVP-P1-w1-blank-1","prompt":"The normal AV conducting bridge across the fibrous skeleton is the bundle of ___.","answers":["His"],"explanation":"The AV bundle crosses the insulating framework."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-P1-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"How the impulse reaches the ventricles","definition":"SA → atria → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje network."},{"term":"Automaticity: the nodal action potential","definition":"SA nodal phase 4 is spontaneous, not flat."},{"term":"Working ventricular cells: phases 0–4","definition":"Fast sodium → upstroke; calcium balanced by potassium → plateau."},{"term":"Why cardiac muscle cannot normally be tetanized","definition":"Long action potential → long refractory period overlapping contraction."},{"term":"Conduction delay, escape rhythms and hierarchy","definition":"SA activity normally outruns subsidiary pacemakers."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}],"scopeExclusions":[{"text":"The SA-node HCN molecular-mapping figure details are explicitly not asked in the exam.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p1","page":7}}]},"CVP-P2":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain cardiac muscle architecture and coupling.","Explain calcium-induced calcium release.","Explain troponin, cross-bridges and the sliding filament.","Explain relaxation requires active calcium removal.","Explain refractory period and the mechanical twitch.","Explain length–tension and the Frank–Starling response.","Explain afterload and force–velocity: when can the muscle shorten?."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-3","concept":"CVP-P2:3","title":"Cardiac muscle architecture and coupling","body":["Cardiac muscle consists of striated, branching cells joined end to end at intercalated discs. Mechanical junctions keep cells attached while they pull; gap junctions provide low-resistance electrical coupling. This arrangement permits a wave of excitation and coordinated force without merging the cells into one anatomical cell.","Actin and myosin are organized into sarcomeres. Electrical coupling synchronizes activation, while the sarcomere converts calcium signaling and ATP use into force. These are complementary functions, not interchangeable descriptions of a junction."],"keyPoints":["Desmosomes/adherens junctions provide mechanical attachment.","Gap junctions provide electrical communication."],"example":"If the cells stay attached but electrical coupling is reduced, strong structure alone cannot guarantee synchronized activation.","pitfalls":["An intercalated disc is not a motor end plate."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":3},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":8},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":9}]},{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-0","concept":"CVP-P2:0","title":"Calcium-induced calcium release","body":["An action potential travels along the sarcolemma and into T-tubules. L-type calcium channels admit a relatively small trigger calcium current. This activates ryanodine receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, releasing a larger calcium store into the cytosol: calcium-induced calcium release.","Cardiac muscle depends on extracellular calcium as well as SR calcium. Compared with skeletal muscle, its SR is less extensive and the extracellular trigger has a more direct role. The calcium arriving through the surface membrane is therefore not merely an unrelated accompaniment to contraction."],"keyPoints":["L-type entry triggers SR release through ryanodine receptors.","Cardiac activation uses extracellular and stored calcium."],"example":"Reducing trigger calcium entry can reduce SR calcium release and the ensuing twitch, even if actin and myosin remain intact.","pitfalls":["The L-type channel and ryanodine receptor are different channels in different membranes."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":16}]},{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-4","concept":"CVP-P2:4","title":"Troponin, cross-bridges and the sliding filament","body":["Cytosolic calcium binds troponin C, shifting the troponin–tropomyosin complex and exposing actin binding sites. Energized myosin heads bind actin and undergo force-generating cycling. ATP binding detaches myosin; ATP hydrolysis prepares the head for another cycle. Calcium regulates access to the sites rather than providing the mechanical energy.","Thin and thick filaments slide past one another. Their lengths do not shrink. Sarcomeres shorten as Z lines approach and overlap increases; the A band remains approximately constant while I band and H zone narrow."],"keyPoints":["Calcium binds troponin C; ATP supplies cross-bridge cycling.","Sarcomere shortening reflects sliding, not shorter filaments."],"example":"When calcium falls, tropomyosin can cover actin sites again even though ATP and the filaments are still present.","pitfalls":["Cardiac muscle uses troponin regulation; smooth muscle primarily uses calmodulin–MLCK."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":20}]},{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-1","concept":"CVP-P2:1","title":"Relaxation requires active calcium removal","body":["Relaxation begins when cytosolic calcium falls. SERCA uses ATP to pump calcium back into the SR. The sodium–calcium exchanger moves calcium out across the sarcolemma using the sodium gradient; the sodium–potassium ATPase indirectly maintains that gradient. Smaller additional pathways also remove calcium.","As calcium dissociates from troponin C, tropomyosin blocks further cross-bridge recruitment. Relaxation therefore requires energy and ion gradients. Stopping membrane excitation alone does not instantly remove calcium already present around the contractile proteins."],"keyPoints":["SERCA returns calcium to SR; NCX extrudes calcium from the cell.","Falling cytosolic calcium turns off troponin-mediated activation."],"example":"A cell can repolarize before every calcium ion has been cleared; the mechanical twitch follows calcium handling, not just membrane voltage.","pitfalls":["SERCA is a pump in the SR membrane, not the surface L-type channel."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":17}]},{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-5","concept":"CVP-P2:5","title":"Refractory period and the mechanical twitch","body":["The long ventricular action potential and refractory period overlap much of the contraction. This prevents the rapid repetitive stimulation needed for tetanus. A single action potential can therefore support a prolonged calcium transient and a twitch, followed by a filling interval.","Conduction velocity describes how quickly excitation travels between regions, whereas refractory period describes how long recently excited tissue resists re-excitation. A fast Purkinje network and a long ventricular refractory period can exist together."],"keyPoints":["Velocity and refractoriness describe different properties.","No normal cardiac tetanus means relaxation remains possible."],"example":"Increasing propagation speed does not necessarily shorten the time that a given myocyte stays refractory.","pitfalls":["A long refractory period does not mean slow propagation through Purkinje fibers."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":23}]},{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-2","concept":"CVP-P2:2","title":"Length–tension and the Frank–Starling response","body":["Passive tension is the resistance of unstimulated muscle to stretch. Active tension is the extra force generated by stimulation at a given initial length. Total tension is their sum. Over the physiological working range, greater initial fiber length increases active force through length-dependent activation, including increased calcium sensitivity and more favorable filament interactions.","In the intact heart, greater end-diastolic filling stretches ventricular fibers and can increase stroke volume: the Frank–Starling response. This intrinsic response is different from increasing contractility at a fixed preload. It has limits and is not a claim that unlimited stretching always improves pumping."],"keyPoints":["Total tension = passive tension + active tension.","More physiological preload can increase force without a new nerve signal."],"example":"If total tension is 12 units and passive tension is 4 units at the same length, active tension is 8 units.","pitfalls":["Preload, afterload and contractility are different variables."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":24},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":25},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":26},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":27},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":28},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":29},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":30},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":31},{"source":"cvp-t-p2","page":3}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-P2-focus-2","kind":"lecturer","label":"Frank–Starling and preload","evidence":"The very most important things ... Frank-Starling law, the pre-load, and the after-load.","teaching":"Separate length-dependent activation from a change in contractile state.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p2","page":3}}]},{"id":"CVP-P2:week1-6","concept":"CVP-P2:6","title":"Afterload and force–velocity: when can the muscle shorten?","body":["Afterload is the load the ventricle must overcome to eject, often approximated by the pressure opposing ventricular outflow. At a fixed activation and initial length, increasing load slows shortening. At a sufficiently high load, the muscle develops force without shortening; this is isometric contraction. The theoretical zero-load shortening velocity is an intercept, not normal ejection against arterial pressure.","Within its physiological range, increasing preload improves length-dependent force generation and can shift the force–velocity relationship so that shortening is faster at a given load. Keep the initial stretch, opposing load and intrinsic contractile state distinct when interpreting a curve."],"keyPoints":["Higher afterload → slower shortening at fixed preload/activation.","Isometric force is generated without shortening."],"example":"A heavier load can make a muscle shorten more slowly even though it is generating greater force.","pitfalls":["Preload is the starting stretch; afterload is the opposing ejection load."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":29},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":30},{"source":"cvp-slides-p2","page":31},{"source":"cvp-t-p2","page":3}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-P2-focus-6","kind":"lecturer","label":"Afterload and shortening velocity","evidence":"The very most important things ... Frank-Starling law, the pre-load, and the after-load.","teaching":"At fixed preload/activation, greater opposing load reduces shortening velocity.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p2","page":3}}]}],"summary":["Desmosomes/adherens junctions provide mechanical attachment.","L-type entry triggers SR release through ryanodine receptors.","Calcium binds troponin C; ATP supplies cross-bridge cycling.","SERCA returns calcium to SR; NCX extrudes calcium from the cell.","Velocity and refractoriness describe different properties.","Total tension = passive tension + active tension.","Higher afterload → slower shortening at fixed preload/activation."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-P2-w1-blank-0","prompt":"The ATP-dependent pump returning calcium to SR is ___.","answers":["SERCA"],"explanation":"SERCA sequesters calcium to promote relaxation."},{"id":"CVP-P2-w1-blank-1","prompt":"Total tension minus passive tension gives ___ tension.","answers":["active"],"explanation":"Active tension is the stimulated force component at the same length."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-P2-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Cardiac muscle architecture and coupling","definition":"Desmosomes/adherens junctions provide mechanical attachment."},{"term":"Calcium-induced calcium release","definition":"L-type entry triggers SR release through ryanodine receptors."},{"term":"Troponin, cross-bridges and the sliding filament","definition":"Calcium binds troponin C; ATP supplies cross-bridge cycling."},{"term":"Relaxation requires active calcium removal","definition":"SERCA returns calcium to SR; NCX extrudes calcium from the cell."},{"term":"Refractory period and the mechanical twitch","definition":"Velocity and refractoriness describe different properties."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}]},"CVP-P3":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain read the waves before naming a rhythm.","Explain intervals, segments and paper calibration.","Explain heart rate from regular and irregular traces.","Explain limb leads and Einthoven’s law.","Explain precordial leads and different viewpoints.","Explain vectors explain polarity, axis and the T wave.","Explain a practical mean-QRS-axis check."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-0","concept":"CVP-P3:0","title":"Read the waves before naming a rhythm","body":["P represents atrial depolarization, QRS ventricular depolarization and T ventricular repolarization. Within a QRS complex, Q is an initial negative deflection before any positive deflection, R is a positive deflection, and S is a negative deflection after R. A complex need not contain all three letters.","Electrical activity precedes mechanical contraction. An ECG is the changing projection of the heart’s electrical activity onto a lead, not a graph of chamber pressure. Atrial repolarization is usually hidden by the much larger QRS signal."],"keyPoints":["Name Q, R and S by direction and sequence.","A QS complex can lack an R wave."],"example":"An upward deflection followed by a downward deflection is an RS complex; the downward part is S, not Q.","pitfalls":["Not every negative deflection is a Q wave."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":8},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":12}]},{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-2","concept":"CVP-P3:2","title":"Intervals, segments and paper calibration","body":["At 25 mm/s, one small 1-mm square is 0.04 s and one large 5-mm square is 0.20 s. Standard voltage calibration is 10 mm/mV; confirm the actual trace settings before measuring. The PR interval runs from P onset to QRS onset and includes atrial depolarization plus AV conduction. The PR segment excludes the P wave.","The QT interval runs from QRS onset to T end and encompasses ventricular depolarization and repolarization. It varies with heart rate and should not be equated precisely with the duration of mechanical contraction. A typical adult PR interval is 0.12–0.20 s; QRS is normally narrower than 0.12 s."],"keyPoints":["PR is an interval, not just the flat PR segment.","At 25 mm/s: small square 0.04 s; large square 0.20 s."],"example":"A PR interval spanning six small squares lasts 6 × 0.04 = 0.24 s, longer than 0.20 s.","pitfalls":["Always check speed; numbers calculated for 25 mm/s are wrong at 50 mm/s."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":16}]},{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-3","concept":"CVP-P3:3","title":"Heart rate from regular and irregular traces","body":["For a regular rhythm at 25 mm/s, heart rate equals 300 divided by the number of large squares between successive R waves, or 1500 divided by small squares. Alternatively use 60 divided by RR in seconds. These formulas express the same conversion from cycle length to cycles per minute.","For an irregular rhythm, one RR interval is not representative. Count QRS complexes in a known time window and scale to a minute; a six-second count multiplied by ten gives an approximate average. Report regularity separately from rate."],"keyPoints":["Regular rate: 300 / large squares between R waves.","An irregular trace requires a representative time window."],"example":"Four large squares between R waves corresponds to 75/min; five corresponds to 60/min.","pitfalls":["Do not use the P-wave rate as the ventricular rate when there is AV dissociation."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":15}]},{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-1","concept":"CVP-P3:1","title":"Limb leads and Einthoven’s law","body":["A lead is an electrical viewpoint, not the same thing as a physical electrode. Lead I compares left arm positive with right arm negative; II compares left leg positive with right arm negative; III compares left leg positive with left arm negative. At the same instant, signed voltages satisfy II = I + III.","The augmented limb leads aVR, aVL and aVF point toward the right arm, left arm and foot respectively. The six limb leads view the frontal plane. A standard twelve-lead ECG uses ten electrodes: twelve leads are twelve derived viewpoints."],"keyPoints":["Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, with signs retained.","I: RA−/LA+; II: RA−/LL+; III: LA−/LL+."],"example":"If I is +0.6 mV and III is −0.2 mV at the same instant, II is +0.4 mV.","pitfalls":["Use simultaneous signed voltages, not unrelated peak amplitudes from different times."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":20},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":27},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":28}]},{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-4","concept":"CVP-P3:4","title":"Precordial leads and different viewpoints","body":["V1–V6 view the heart in the horizontal plane using chest electrodes referenced to a combined limb reference. V1 is placed at the fourth right intercostal space beside the sternum and V2 at the fourth left; V4 is at the fifth left intercostal space on the midclavicular line; V3 lies between V2 and V4. V5 and V6 share V4’s horizontal level at the anterior and midaxillary lines.","A normal ventricular resultant generally moves away from right anterior leads and toward left lateral leads. Consequently V1 often has a predominantly negative QRS while lateral R waves become larger. Interpret the pattern across leads, not the sign in isolation."],"keyPoints":["Limb leads view the frontal plane; chest leads view the horizontal plane.","A predominantly negative QRS in V1 can be normal."],"example":"The same electrical event can be positive in one lead and negative in another because their positive axes differ.","pitfalls":["“Negative QRS” does not by itself establish a diseased or electrically silent ventricle."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":23}]},{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-5","concept":"CVP-P3:5","title":"Vectors explain polarity, axis and the T wave","body":["A lead records the projection of an instantaneous electrical vector onto its positive axis. A depolarization vector toward the positive electrode yields a positive deflection; away yields a negative one. A nearly perpendicular vector produces a small or biphasic signal. Vector length represents magnitude and direction represents the net orientation, not the travel path of one cell.","The mean QRS axis summarizes ventricular depolarization in the frontal plane. Initial septal activation can point away from a left-facing lead and create a small Q wave. Ventricular repolarization generally proceeds in a different spatial order from depolarization; reversal of both electrical polarity and propagation direction helps make T concordant with the main QRS in many normal leads."],"keyPoints":["Toward positive → positive depolarization deflection; perpendicular → small net projection.","A positive T wave can accompany a positive QRS even though T is repolarization."],"example":"A vector directed along Lead I has a strong Lead-I projection; rotating it toward perpendicular reduces the recorded amplitude.","pitfalls":["An electrical vector is not universally an arrow showing physical movement of repolarizing cells."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":24},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":25},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":26},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":28},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":29},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":30},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":31},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":32},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":33},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":34},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":35},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":36},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":37},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":38},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":39},{"source":"cvp-t-p3","page":24}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-P3-focus-5","kind":"lecturer","label":"Vector direction and magnitude","evidence":"If you remember these two characteristics, you know how to read an ECG.","teaching":"Direction sets lead polarity; vector length represents magnitude, and the lead measures a projection.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p3","page":24}}]},{"id":"CVP-P3:week1-6","concept":"CVP-P3:6","title":"A practical mean-QRS-axis check","body":["Use the net QRS deflection in limb leads: add positive and negative components with their signs. Lead I points toward 0 degrees and aVF toward +90 degrees. Positive in both places the mean axis in the lower-left quadrant of the patient. Negative I with positive aVF points rightward; negative in both points to an extreme superior/rightward quadrant.","If I is positive and aVF negative, use Lead II to distinguish a mildly superior axis from a more leftward axis: a positive II is consistent with the usual adult range down to about minus 30 degrees, while negative II supports a more leftward axis. These are screening steps; lead quality, morphology and the clinical setting still matter."],"keyPoints":["Use signed net QRS, not just the tallest R wave.","I positive/aVF positive suggests a mean frontal axis between 0 and +90 degrees."],"example":"If I is +6 mm net and aVF +4 mm net, both positive projections place the resultant in the 0 to +90 degree quadrant.","pitfalls":["Left and right refer to the patient, not the viewer facing the tracing."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":27},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":28},{"source":"cvp-slides-p3","page":37}]}],"summary":["Name Q, R and S by direction and sequence.","PR is an interval, not just the flat PR segment.","Regular rate: 300 / large squares between R waves.","Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, with signs retained.","Limb leads view the frontal plane; chest leads view the horizontal plane.","Toward positive → positive depolarization deflection; perpendicular → small net projection.","Use signed net QRS, not just the tallest R wave."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-P3-w1-blank-0","prompt":"At 25 mm/s, one small square is ___ seconds.","answers":["0.04",".04"],"explanation":"1 mm divided by 25 mm/s equals 0.04 s."},{"id":"CVP-P3-w1-blank-1","prompt":"Einthoven’s law: Lead II = Lead I + Lead ___.","answers":["III","3"],"explanation":"Use simultaneous signed potentials."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-P3-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Read the waves before naming a rhythm","definition":"Name Q, R and S by direction and sequence."},{"term":"Intervals, segments and paper calibration","definition":"PR is an interval, not just the flat PR segment."},{"term":"Heart rate from regular and irregular traces","definition":"Regular rate: 300 / large squares between R waves."},{"term":"Limb leads and Einthoven’s law","definition":"Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, with signs retained."},{"term":"Precordial leads and different viewpoints","definition":"Limb leads view the frontal plane; chest leads view the horizontal plane."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}]},"CVP-P4":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain a systematic rhythm check and sinus variation.","Explain first-degree AV block: every impulse conducts late.","Explain second-degree block: PR behavior matters.","Explain complete AV block and ventricular escape.","Explain bundle-branch block and ventricular activation.","Explain premature beats: atrial, junctional and ventricular.","Explain paroxysmal tachycardia and re-entry.","Explain atrial fibrillation versus atrial flutter.","Explain ventricular fibrillation, pre-excitation and arrest concepts.","Explain sinoatrial exit block versus AV conduction failure."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-3","concept":"CVP-P4:3","title":"A systematic rhythm check and sinus variation","body":["Start with calibration, rate and regularity; identify P waves, their relationship to QRS, PR behavior and QRS width. Sinus tachycardia is a sinus rhythm above 100/min in an adult, while sinus bradycardia is below 60/min. A rate label alone does not identify the mechanism or establish whether it is abnormal for the situation.","Respiratory sinus arrhythmia varies sinus cycle length with breathing, commonly increasing rate during inspiration and decreasing it during expiration through autonomic modulation. Ectopic activity, conduction blocks and re-entry are different mechanisms that may also produce abnormal rhythms."],"keyPoints":["Rate, regularity, P–QRS relationship and QRS width are separate observations.","Sinus rate can vary physiologically with breathing."],"example":"An athlete with a resting sinus rate of 55/min has bradycardia by rate definition; that number alone does not diagnose conduction block.","pitfalls":["Do not label every irregular rhythm atrial fibrillation before checking for organized P waves."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":3},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":8}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-0","concept":"CVP-P4:0","title":"First-degree AV block: every impulse conducts late","body":["First-degree AV block means the PR interval is prolonged beyond 0.20 s in adults, with every atrial impulse still followed by a ventricular complex. It is a conduction delay, not dropped conduction. Measure from P onset to QRS onset and verify that the pattern persists across beats.","The ECG finding alone does not establish a cause. Increased vagal influence, conduction-system disease and other influences can prolong conduction. A long PR must not be confused with a broad QRS, which describes ventricular depolarization duration."],"keyPoints":["PR >0.20 s with 1:1 P–QRS conduction.","Long PR and wide QRS measure different parts of conduction."],"example":"At 25 mm/s, a stable PR of six small squares with every P conducted is consistent with first-degree AV delay.","pitfalls":["The slide’s association with rheumatic disease is not a diagnosis from the ECG alone."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":12}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-4","concept":"CVP-P4:4","title":"Second-degree block: PR behavior matters","body":["Second-degree AV block has some atrial impulses without a following QRS. In Mobitz I (Wenckebach), the PR typically lengthens progressively before a nonconducted P wave, then the cycle repeats. In Mobitz II, conducted beats have stable PR intervals with intermittent unexpected failure of conduction.","A 2:1 pattern means two atrial depolarizations for each ventricular depolarization. It cannot reliably be assigned Mobitz I or II from the ratio alone because there are not successive conducted PR intervals to examine. Count atrial and ventricular events separately."],"keyPoints":["Mobitz I: progressive PR lengthening then a dropped QRS.","Mobitz II: stable conducted PR with intermittent dropped QRS; 2:1 alone is indeterminate."],"example":"PR intervals of 0.16, 0.20 and 0.24 s followed by a P without QRS form a Wenckebach sequence.","pitfalls":["The lecture shorthand equating fixed ratios with Mobitz II is incomplete."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":16}],"furtherReading":[{"title":"ACCF/AHA/HRS definitions of AV block","url":"https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2012.11.007"}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-1","concept":"CVP-P4:1","title":"Complete AV block and ventricular escape","body":["In third-degree AV block, no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles through the normal AV pathway. Atrial P waves and ventricular QRS complexes run independently, so apparent PR intervals vary. A subsidiary junctional or ventricular pacemaker maintains a slower escape rhythm; QRS width depends partly on its origin.","A pause can occur before an escape focus takes over because faster prior activation had suppressed the latent pacemaker. The key diagnostic reasoning is AV dissociation with failure of conduction, not merely a slow rate."],"keyPoints":["Complete block: independent atrial and ventricular rhythms.","Escape rhythm maintains some activation but does not restore AV conduction."],"example":"Atrial rate 90/min and ventricular rate 35/min with P waves marching through QRS/T independently supports complete AV block.","pitfalls":["An occasional P appearing just before QRS may be coincidental; inspect the whole strip."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":16}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-5","concept":"CVP-P4:5","title":"Bundle-branch block and ventricular activation","body":["A bundle-branch block delays activation of one ventricle. Excitation reaches part of the ventricular muscle through slower cell-to-cell spread instead of the usual rapid pathway, widening QRS. Complete adult bundle-branch block typically has QRS ≥0.12 s; morphology across V1 and lateral leads helps distinguish right from left patterns.","The lecture uses WiLLiaM for LBBB (W-like V1, M-like V6) and MaRRoW for RBBB (M-like V1, W-like V6). These are memory aids, not a substitute for examining the full trace. Alternating QRS amplitude is an observation with several possible mechanisms; it does not establish a single Purkinje disorder."],"keyPoints":["AV delay affects P–QRS timing; bundle delay affects ventricular QRS spread.","A mnemonic supports, but does not replace, morphology and duration."],"example":"A wide QRS with delayed right-sided terminal activation can suggest RBBB even when the PR interval is normal.","pitfalls":["Electrical alternans is not synonymous with one specific intraventricular block."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":20},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":21}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-6","concept":"CVP-P4:6","title":"Premature beats: atrial, junctional and ventricular","body":["A premature beat occurs earlier than the next expected sinus beat. A premature atrial contraction usually has an early altered P followed by a narrow QRS if ventricular conduction is normal. A junctional premature beat may have an absent, buried or retrograde P. A PVC usually has a broad unusual QRS without a preceding sinus P, followed by repolarization discordant with its main QRS.","A PAC commonly resets the sinus node and produces a noncompensatory pause; a typical PVC may leave sinus timing intact and produce a full compensatory pause. These are useful patterns rather than absolute rules. An early beat can fill poorly and create a pulse deficit."],"keyPoints":["PAC: early atrial activation; PVC: early abnormal ventricular activation.","PAC pause usually noncompensatory; typical PVC pause may be fully compensatory."],"example":"An electrical premature beat may be visible on ECG but produce a weak peripheral pulse because filling time was short.","pitfalls":["The Part-II slide calls the PAC pause compensatory; the usual distinction is corrected here."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":3},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":8}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-7","concept":"CVP-P4:7","title":"Paroxysmal tachycardia and re-entry","body":["Paroxysmal tachycardia begins and ends abruptly. Supraventricular circuits can produce narrow complexes when the usual ventricular pathways remain intact; P waves may be altered, retrograde or hidden. A ventricular tachycardia arises below the atria and typically produces a sequence of broad ventricular complexes.","Re-entry means a wave returns to tissue that has recovered excitability and stimulates it again. It requires an appropriate circuit, often unidirectional block and sufficiently slow travel relative to recovery. A longer path, slower conduction or shorter refractory period can allow the returning wave to find excitable tissue."],"keyPoints":["Re-entry reuses an excitable circuit rather than requiring a new sinus impulse each cycle.","Longer travel time relative to refractoriness favors successful return."],"example":"If a returning wave arrives while tissue is still refractory it stops; if it arrives after recovery it may continue circulating.","pitfalls":["The source includes historical treatments; this lesson teaches mechanisms rather than instructions for self-treatment."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":16}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-2","concept":"CVP-P4:2","title":"Atrial fibrillation versus atrial flutter","body":["Atrial fibrillation contains disorganized atrial activity without consistent discrete P waves. Variable transmission through the AV node commonly produces an irregularly irregular ventricular response. Loss of organized atrial contraction can reduce filling, especially when atrial contribution matters more.","Atrial flutter is more organized macro-re-entry, often producing repetitive flutter waves and a regular atrial rate. The AV node may conduct in a fixed ratio such as 2:1, giving a regular ventricular response, or variably, giving an irregular one. An irregular ventricular rhythm is therefore common in AF but not exclusive to it."],"keyPoints":["AF: no consistent organized P, commonly irregularly irregular RR.","Flutter: organized repetitive atrial waves; AV conduction ratio affects ventricular rate."],"example":"Atrial flutter at 300/min with 2:1 conduction gives a ventricular rate of about 150/min.","pitfalls":["The Part-II text contains a ratio typo; 2:1 means two atrial activations per ventricular activation."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":20}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-8","concept":"CVP-P4:8","title":"Ventricular fibrillation, pre-excitation and arrest concepts","body":["Ventricular fibrillation consists of disorganized ventricular electrical activity without coordinated ejection. Unlike a repeating monomorphic tachycardia, it has no stable organized QRS pattern. Multiple circulating wavelets and heterogeneous recovery help sustain it. Cardiac arrest means absence of effective circulation; it is not synonymous with myocardial infarction, which is ischemic injury to heart muscle.","The deck also introduces an accessory atrioventricular pathway in Wolff–Parkinson–White pre-excitation. Ventricular activation can start before the usual AV delay has finished, producing a short PR and slurred initial QRS (delta wave). An accessory pathway can also participate in re-entry."],"keyPoints":["VF: disorganized ventricular activation and ineffective coordinated pumping.","WPW pre-excitation: accessory pathway, short PR and delta wave."],"example":"A fast but repeating broad-complex rhythm is conceptually different from a chaotic trace lacking organized complexes.","pitfalls":["A heart attack may trigger arrest, but the terms describe different events."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":20},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4b","page":23}]},{"id":"CVP-P4:week1-9","concept":"CVP-P4:9","title":"Sinoatrial exit block versus AV conduction failure","body":["Sinoatrial exit block prevents a sinus-node impulse from activating atrial muscle. A surface ECG does not directly show the tiny SA-node action potential; it shows the downstream atrial P wave. An expected P–QRS sequence may therefore be missing when the impulse fails to exit. In a simple second-degree exit-block pattern, the pause may be a multiple of the preceding sinus cycle.","In AV block, atrial activation can still occur, so a P wave may appear without its expected QRS. Sinus arrest also produces missing atrial activation, but its pause need not preserve the same cycle multiple. A short strip may not settle every distinction: identify what is absent before naming the mechanism."],"keyPoints":["SA exit failure can remove the expected P and its conducted QRS.","AV conduction failure can leave a visible nonconducted P."],"example":"A visible on-time P with no following QRS is more consistent with an AV conduction problem than with that same impulse failing to exit the SA node.","pitfalls":["An absent P alone cannot reveal every detail of sinus-node impulse generation."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-p4a","page":10}]}],"summary":["Rate, regularity, P–QRS relationship and QRS width are separate observations.","PR >0.20 s with 1:1 P–QRS conduction.","Mobitz I: progressive PR lengthening then a dropped QRS.","Complete block: independent atrial and ventricular rhythms.","AV delay affects P–QRS timing; bundle delay affects ventricular QRS spread.","PAC: early atrial activation; PVC: early abnormal ventricular activation.","Re-entry reuses an excitable circuit rather than requiring a new sinus impulse each cycle.","AF: no consistent organized P, commonly irregularly irregular RR.","VF: disorganized ventricular activation and ineffective coordinated pumping.","SA exit failure can remove the expected P and its conducted QRS."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed.","The source cover says 2025/CVP121 despite the supplied filename. This is not used to alter course dates.","Study clarification: 2:1 AV block cannot be reliably classified as Mobitz I or II from the ratio alone.","Study clarification: PACs usually reset the sinus node and have a noncompensatory pause.","Historical treatment examples are retained in the source. The study guide focuses on mechanisms and recognition.","Source ratio typo: use standard AV conduction ratios such as 2:1 or 3:1, not 2:3."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-P4-w1-blank-0","prompt":"Progressive PR prolongation before a dropped QRS is Mobitz type ___.","answers":["I","1","one"],"explanation":"Mobitz I is the Wenckebach pattern."},{"id":"CVP-P4-w1-blank-1","prompt":"The slurred initial QRS of pre-excitation is called a ___ wave.","answers":["delta"],"explanation":"An accessory AV route can initiate ventricular depolarization early."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-P4-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"A systematic rhythm check and sinus variation","definition":"Rate, regularity, P–QRS relationship and QRS width are separate observations."},{"term":"First-degree AV block: every impulse conducts late","definition":"PR >0.20 s with 1:1 P–QRS conduction."},{"term":"Second-degree block: PR behavior matters","definition":"Mobitz I: progressive PR lengthening then a dropped QRS."},{"term":"Complete AV block and ventricular escape","definition":"Complete block: independent atrial and ventricular rhythms."},{"term":"Bundle-branch block and ventricular activation","definition":"AV delay affects P–QRS timing; bundle delay affects ventricular QRS spread."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}],"scopeExclusions":[{"text":"Treatment and patient-management questions are outside this lecture’s stated scope.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p4","page":2}}]},"CVP-A1":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain cardiac origin, timing and molecular patterning.","Explain from paired endocardial tubes to one flow pathway.","Explain looping places atria behind the ventricular region.","Explain sinus venosus remodeling and smooth atrial walls.","Explain atrial septation and the fetal one-way flap.","Explain ventricular septum, cushions and AV partitioning.","Explain spiral outflow septation and great arteries.","Explain tetralogy of Fallot: connect the four findings.","Explain intraembryonic coelom: making room for a beating heart.","Explain looping, dextrocardia and anterior injury reasoning.","Explain atrial wall origins and the three venous inflows."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-0","concept":"CVP-A1:0","title":"Cardiac origin, timing and molecular patterning","body":["The cardiovascular system begins developing in the third embryonic week. Splanchnic mesoderm supplies major cardiac progenitors; neural crest cells contribute importantly to outflow-tract development. The primitive heart begins beating at approximately day 22, when an effective transport system becomes necessary for a growing embryo.","Development depends on coordinated gene programs rather than one “heart gene.” The deck highlights NKX2-5, GATA4, MEF2C, HAND1/2 and TBX5. TBX5-related Holt–Oram syndrome links upper-limb abnormalities with cardiac defects; the examples connect gene regulation to organ patterning, not deterministic prediction from one finding."],"keyPoints":["Main early cardiac origin: splanchnic mesoderm.","Neural crest contributes to conotruncal/outflow development."],"example":"A question connecting an upper-limb defect with an atrial septal defect can test the TBX5 association discussed in the lecture.","pitfalls":["Do not confuse the first heartbeat with completion of all four chambers."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":8}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-1","concept":"CVP-A1:1","title":"From paired endocardial tubes to one flow pathway","body":["Paired endocardial tubes approach and fuse as the embryo folds. The single tube has a venous inflow and an arterial outflow. Trace blood from sinus venosus through primitive atrium, primitive ventricle, bulbus cordis and truncus arteriosus. This inflow-to-outflow sequence is the reverse of listing the tube from cranial to caudal.","Primitive ventricle contributes the trabeculated left ventricle; proximal bulbus contributes the trabeculated right ventricle, while conal regions contribute smooth outflow portions. The truncal region is partitioned into the proximal great arterial outlets. These are developmental contributions rather than five adult chambers."],"keyPoints":["Flow: sinus venosus → atrium → ventricle → bulbus → truncus.","Primitive ventricular segment chiefly contributes the trabeculated LV."],"example":"If the question asks “next along the direction of flow after the primitive atrium,” choose primitive ventricle, not sinus venosus.","pitfalls":["Anatomical head-to-tail order and blood-flow order point in opposite directions."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":20}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-2","concept":"CVP-A1:2","title":"Looping places atria behind the ventricular region","body":["The growing heart tube cannot remain straight. Normal rightward looping shifts the bulboventricular portion ventrally and to the right, while atrial/inflow components move dorsocranially. The result brings future ventricular regions anterior to the atrial region and establishes relationships for subsequent septation.","Looping is a geometrical rearrangement, not the same process as dividing the lumen into separate chambers. Abnormal looping can alter cardiac position; a right-sided apex is termed dextrocardia. Pericardial reflections and loss of the dorsal attachment also help create the transverse pericardial passage behind the arterial pole."],"keyPoints":["Normal looping is rightward and establishes chamber relationships.","Looping and septation are distinct developmental steps."],"example":"A diagram showing atria posterior to the future ventricles is showing the consequence of looping, even before all septa have formed.","pitfalls":["A rightward loop does not mean that the normal adult apex points right."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":23},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":24},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":25},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":26},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":26}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A1-focus-2","kind":"exam","label":"Rightward loop versus dextrocardia","evidence":"We ask many questions on that, why would a person have dextrocardia?","teaching":"Know the lecture’s normal-versus-opposite looping comparison, without inferring all-organ situs.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":26}}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-3","concept":"CVP-A1:3","title":"Sinus venosus remodeling and smooth atrial walls","body":["Venous return initially reaches a paired sinus venosus. As venous return shifts rightward, the right sinus horn enlarges and is incorporated into the smooth posterior right atrium, the sinus venarum. The left horn becomes the coronary sinus and related oblique-vein remnant. The rough pectinate atrial regions retain a primitive-atrial contribution.","This explains why the right atrium has a smooth venous part and a rough muscular part separated internally by the crista terminalis. The supplied slide 31 says that the right horn disappears; that statement is corrected here, while the original slide remains available unchanged."],"keyPoints":["Right sinus horn → smooth sinus venarum of RA.","Left sinus horn → coronary sinus."],"example":"Identify a smooth right atrial region receiving venae cavae, then connect it to incorporated right sinus-horn tissue.","pitfalls":["The right sinus horn is incorporated; it does not simply disappear."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":27},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":28},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":29},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":30},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":31}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-4","concept":"CVP-A1:4","title":"Atrial septation and the fetal one-way flap","body":["Septum primum grows toward the endocardial cushions, narrowing the ostium primum. Before this opening closes, perforations create the ostium secundum in the upper primum. Septum secundum then develops to the right, leaving the foramen ovale. Septum primum acts as its flap valve, permitting fetal right-to-left flow while limiting reverse flow.","At birth, lung expansion lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and increases pulmonary venous return to the left atrium. Left atrial pressure rises relative to right atrial pressure, pressing the primum flap against the secundum. Functional closure precedes possible anatomical fusion; a patent foramen ovale is not identical to missing septal tissue in an atrial septal defect."],"keyPoints":["Primum forms the flap; secundum leaves the foramen ovale.","Higher LA pressure after birth promotes functional closure."],"example":"Fetal RA-to-LA flow bypasses the lungs; after birth the pressure reversal tends to shut this flap.","pitfalls":["Ostium secundum and septum secundum are not the same structure."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":32},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":33},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":34},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":35},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":36},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":37},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":38},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":39},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":40}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-5","concept":"CVP-A1:5","title":"Ventricular septum, cushions and AV partitioning","body":["The muscular interventricular septum grows from the ventricular floor toward the endocardial cushions, initially leaving an interventricular foramen. Closure of the remaining opening requires coordinated contributions from cushion and outflow septal tissues, forming the small membranous region. Failure of these junctions is an important basis for membranous VSD.","AV cushions divide the common AV canal into right and left channels and contribute to the central septal/valvular apparatus. Do not attribute the entire ventricular septum to one embryonic source: its muscular and membranous components have different developmental relationships."],"keyPoints":["Muscular septum grows upward; the residual opening is closed by coordinated tissues.","Endocardial cushions help partition the common AV canal."],"example":"A defect near the membranous septum can be close to the AV conduction bundle, linking embryology with internal anatomy.","pitfalls":["The cushions contribute to several neighboring structures rather than being a separate adult chamber."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":41},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":42},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":43},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":44}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-6","concept":"CVP-A1:6","title":"Spiral outflow septation and great arteries","body":["Conotruncal ridges grow and fuse in a spiral arrangement, separating aortic and pulmonary outflow channels. Neural crest contributions are important to this process. Normal alignment connects the left ventricle with the aorta and the right ventricle with the pulmonary trunk.","The spiral relationship explains the crossing arrangement of the great arteries. Disordered septation or alignment can create conotruncal anomalies. Distinguish failure to separate the outflow from failure of appropriate connection/alignment; different defects need not share identical anatomy."],"keyPoints":["Aorticopulmonary septation separates the arterial outlets.","Neural crest contributes to conotruncal development."],"example":"Connect the outflow diagram to the mature sequence LV→aorta and RV→pulmonary trunk before memorizing an anomaly name.","pitfalls":["The aorticopulmonary septum is not the adult atrial flap valve."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":45},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":46},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":47},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":48}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:week1-7","concept":"CVP-A1:7","title":"Tetralogy of Fallot: connect the four findings","body":["The deck’s Fallot diagrams combine right ventricular outflow obstruction, a ventricular septal defect, an overriding aorta and right ventricular hypertrophy. Abnormal conotruncal alignment creates the outflow obstruction and septal relationship; RV hypertrophy develops in response to the increased pressure load rather than being an independent early tube segment.","An overriding aorta receives blood from above the ventricular septal defect. The degree of obstruction influences shunting and physiology. Use the four-part pattern to recognize the lesion rather than treating one isolated VSD as sufficient for the diagnosis."],"keyPoints":["Pulmonary/RV outflow stenosis + VSD + overriding aorta + RV hypertrophy.","RV hypertrophy is a consequence of pressure loading."],"example":"If an answer set includes an ASD instead of the VSD, it does not list the classic four components.","pitfalls":["The introductory clinical-topic list previews other anomalies; it does not provide full teaching slides for each of them in this file."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":49},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":50},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":51}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:transcript-8","concept":"CVP-A1:8","title":"Intraembryonic coelom: making room for a beating heart","body":["The lateral plate mesoderm separates into somatic and splanchnic layers around the intraembryonic coelom. The heart develops in association with splanchnic mesoderm, while the cranial coelomic region contributes to the pericardial cavity. Folding brings paired cardiac primordia toward the midline.","Keep the tissue origin and surrounding space separate: the coelom is a cavity, not the myocardium itself. The lecturer explicitly says the intraembryonic coelom can be asked about in thoracic or abdominal developmental contexts."],"keyPoints":["Intraembryonic coelom is the space between somatic and splanchnic lateral-plate layers.","Cardiogenic splanchnic tissue and the pericardial cavity are related but different."],"example":"If a diagram asks for the space next to a cardiac primordium, identify the coelomic cavity rather than naming the tube wall.","pitfalls":["Neural crest contributes to outflow development; it is not the principal origin of all pumping cardiomyocytes."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":6},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":8},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":6}],"highYieldAddition":true,"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A1-focus-8","kind":"exam","label":"Coelom in developmental questions","evidence":"Intraembryonic coelom, we ask in the exam.","teaching":"Distinguish the cavity from its adjacent mesodermal tissues.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":6}}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:transcript-9","concept":"CVP-A1:9","title":"Looping, dextrocardia and anterior injury reasoning","body":["Normal dextral looping displaces the bulboventricular region anteriorly and establishes a left-directed apex. The right ventricle becomes the most anterior chamber. In the lecture’s developmental explanation, abnormal opposite-direction looping can produce a right-directed apex, termed dextrocardia.","Use the developmental rearrangement to reason through a clinical stem rather than memorizing an isolated disease name. A hypothetical anterior penetrating injury is more likely to encounter the anterior RV than the posterior LA. Dextrocardia describes cardiac position; it does not by itself tell you the position of every abdominal organ."],"keyPoints":["Rightward looping normally leads to a left-directed apex.","RV is anterior; LA forms much of the posterior base."],"example":"“Normal loop is rightward” and “normal apex is leftward” are compatible statements about different aspects of the geometry.","pitfalls":["Dextrocardia is not automatically synonymous with complete situs inversus."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":14},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":15},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":16},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":26},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":14}],"highYieldAddition":true,"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A1-focus-9","kind":"exam","label":"Looping and anterior-chamber clinical stems","evidence":"If asked in the exam if there is a stab wound ... the ventricle because it is more anteriorly placed.","teaching":"Reason from looping to the anterior RV; page26 also explicitly mentions dextrocardia questions.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":14}}]},{"id":"CVP-A1:transcript-10","concept":"CVP-A1:10","title":"Atrial wall origins and the three venous inflows","body":["The early sinus venosus receives paired common cardinal, vitelline and umbilical venous channels. Common cardinal veins are formed by union of anterior and posterior cardinal drainage. These names describe early embryonic inflow, not six adult venae cavae.","Distinguish smooth and trabeculated atrial territories. Incorporated right sinus horn forms smooth RA; primitive atrial tissue contributes pectinate/appendage regions of both atria. Incorporation of pulmonary venous tissue contributes much of the smooth LA. The transcript’s shorthand that the entire LA comes from pulmonary veins should therefore be applied to its smooth component, not every atrial tissue."],"keyPoints":["Paired cardinal, vitelline and umbilical veins reach the early sinus venosus.","Smooth LA and its pectinate appendage have different developmental contributions."],"example":"A stem about smooth LA asks for pulmonary venous incorporation; a pectinate auricle asks for primitive atrial contribution.","pitfalls":["Do not extend the lecturer’s smooth-wall shorthand to the entire left atrium."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":12},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":13},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":17},{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":23},{"source":"cvp-slides-a1","page":19}],"highYieldAddition":true}],"summary":["Main early cardiac origin: splanchnic mesoderm.","Flow: sinus venosus → atrium → ventricle → bulbus → truncus.","Normal looping is rightward and establishes chamber relationships.","Right sinus horn → smooth sinus venarum of RA.","Primum forms the flap; secundum leaves the foramen ovale.","Muscular septum grows upward; the residual opening is closed by coordinated tissues.","Aorticopulmonary septation separates the arterial outlets.","Pulmonary/RV outflow stenosis + VSD + overriding aorta + RV hypertrophy.","Intraembryonic coelom is the space between somatic and splanchnic lateral-plate layers.","Rightward looping normally leads to a left-directed apex.","Paired cardinal, vitelline and umbilical veins reach the early sinus venosus."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed.","Study correction: the right sinus horn forms the smooth right atrium; it does not disappear.","The hand-drawn proportions are schematic. Do not memorize a one-third membranous septum: the membranous part is small relative to the muscular part.","This deck previews more clinical anomalies in its introduction than it develops. Teaching follows the detailed supplied sections through septation and tetralogy; later course lessons retain their separate scope."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-A1-w1-blank-0","prompt":"The left sinus horn becomes the coronary ___.","answers":["sinus"],"explanation":"The right horn instead forms smooth RA."},{"id":"CVP-A1-w1-blank-1","prompt":"The fetal foramen-ovale flap is septum ___.","answers":["primum"],"explanation":"Primum apposes secundum when left atrial pressure rises."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-A1-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Cardiac origin, timing and molecular patterning","definition":"Main early cardiac origin: splanchnic mesoderm."},{"term":"From paired endocardial tubes to one flow pathway","definition":"Flow: sinus venosus → atrium → ventricle → bulbus → truncus."},{"term":"Looping places atria behind the ventricular region","definition":"Normal looping is rightward and establishes chamber relationships."},{"term":"Sinus venosus remodeling and smooth atrial walls","definition":"Right sinus horn → smooth sinus venarum of RA."},{"term":"Atrial septation and the fetal one-way flap","definition":"Primum forms the flap; secundum leaves the foramen ovale."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}],"scopeExclusions":[{"text":"The lecturer explicitly says memorizing the list of developmental genes is not expected. Existing gene-association questions remain available as enrichment.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a1","page":10}}]},"CVP-A2":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain thoracic cavity, wall and apertures.","Explain surface lines: locate before you label.","Explain sternum and the sternal-angle landmark.","Explain typical ribs, joints and the costal groove.","Explain atypical ribs and cervical-rib compression.","Explain thoracic vertebrae and articulation exceptions."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-A2:week1-2","concept":"CVP-A2:2","title":"Thoracic cavity, wall and apertures","body":["The thorax includes the protective cage and the cavity it encloses. The cavity has a central mediastinum between two pleural compartments. The superior aperture communicates with the neck; it is bounded by T1 posteriorly, first ribs laterally and the superior manubrial border anteriorly. The inferior aperture is closed by the diaphragm and bounded by T12, lower ribs/costal margins and the xiphisternal region.","Anatomists often call the superior aperture the inlet and inferior aperture the outlet. Clinicians use “thoracic outlet syndrome” for compression near the superior aperture on the route to the upper limb. Name the actual boundaries instead of relying on the word outlet alone."],"keyPoints":["Mediastinum centrally, pleural compartments laterally.","Superior aperture: T1, first ribs and manubrium; inferior aperture: diaphragm."],"example":"A cervical rib near the lower neck concerns the clinical thoracic outlet, not a rib piercing the diaphragm.","pitfalls":["Clinical and anatomical uses of “outlet” do not necessarily refer to the same aperture."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":3},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":12},{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A2-focus-2","kind":"exam","label":"Thoracic boundaries and apertures","evidence":"Always pay attention to objectives ... must know materials, and we definitely will test you on those objectives.","teaching":"The stated objectives include wall/surface anatomy, bones/joints and the named clinical cases.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}}]},{"id":"CVP-A2:week1-3","concept":"CVP-A2:3","title":"Surface lines: locate before you label","body":["The midsternal line follows the center of the sternum; parasternal lines follow its lateral borders, and midclavicular lines descend from clavicular midpoints. The anterior and posterior axillary lines follow the corresponding axillary folds, while the midaxillary line lies between them. Posteriorly, the scapular line passes through the inferior scapular angle and the midvertebral line follows spinous-process tips.","A clinical location combines side, vertical line and rib/intercostal level. “Fifth left intercostal space at the midclavicular line” is much more precise than “left chest.” An intercostal space is named for the rib above it."],"keyPoints":["Vertical line plus intercostal level gives a reproducible surface location.","Midclavicular is not the same as parasternal."],"example":"The apex location uses the fifth left intercostal space near the midclavicular line, linking surface anatomy with cardiac examination.","pitfalls":["The inferior scapular angle changes with arm position; know which line the question defines."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":8},{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A2-focus-3","kind":"exam","label":"Assessed lecture objectives","evidence":"We definitely will test you on those objectives.","teaching":"Apply the listed objective to this topic; it is an assessment cue, not a promised exact question.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}}]},{"id":"CVP-A2:week1-0","concept":"CVP-A2:0","title":"Sternum and the sternal-angle landmark","body":["The sternum comprises manubrium, body and xiphoid process. The sternal angle is the manubriosternal junction and identifies the second costal cartilage. It lies near the T4–T5 intervertebral disc plane, a key landmark separating superior from inferior mediastinum and associated with the tracheal bifurcation and aortic-arch transition.","The first rib is difficult to count directly, so the palpable angle is the starting point for counting ribs and spaces. Median sternotomy provides central access to the heart and great vessels. The marrow and subcutaneous position explain the sternum’s anatomical relevance to marrow sampling, without this being a procedural guide."],"keyPoints":["Sternal angle → second costal cartilage → T4/T5 plane.","Manubrium, body and xiphoid are distinct sternal parts."],"example":"Find the second cartilage at the angle, then count downward to locate the fifth intercostal space.","pitfalls":["The sternal angle is not the xiphisternal joint."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":16},{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A2-focus-0","kind":"exam","label":"Assessed lecture objectives","evidence":"We definitely will test you on those objectives.","teaching":"Apply the listed objective to this topic; it is an assessment cue, not a promised exact question.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}}]},{"id":"CVP-A2:week1-1","concept":"CVP-A2:1","title":"Typical ribs, joints and the costal groove","body":["True ribs 1–7 attach to the sternum through their own cartilage. Ribs 8–10 connect indirectly through superior cartilage, while 11–12 float without an anterior sternal connection. Typical ribs 3–9 have a head, neck, tubercle, angle and shaft. A typical head articulates with the corresponding vertebral body and the one above, with the intervening disc; the tubercle meets the corresponding transverse process.","The costal groove runs on the internal inferior shaft and shelters intercostal vessels and nerve. A fractured rib can injure nearby pleura, lung or lower thoracic/upper abdominal structures. Relate fracture anatomy to what lies deep to the bone."],"keyPoints":["Typical head joins two neighboring bodies; tubercle joins the same-number transverse process.","Costal groove is inferior and internal."],"example":"Rib 6 typically meets T5 and T6 bodies at its head and the T6 transverse process at its tubercle.","pitfalls":["Do not use the typical two-facet rule for all atypical ribs."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":24},{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A2-focus-1","kind":"exam","label":"Assessed lecture objectives","evidence":"We definitely will test you on those objectives.","teaching":"Apply the listed objective to this topic; it is an assessment cue, not a promised exact question.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}}]},{"id":"CVP-A2:week1-4","concept":"CVP-A2:4","title":"Atypical ribs and cervical-rib compression","body":["Rib 1 is short, broad and flattened, with a scalene tubercle separating vascular grooves: the subclavian vein lies anterior to anterior scalene and the artery posterior to it. Ribs 10–12 commonly have a single head facet; 11–12 lack a typical neck and tubercle. Rib 2 has a characteristic rough area for serratus anterior.","A cervical rib arises from the C7 region and may compress the lower brachial plexus or subclavian artery. Lower-trunk C8–T1 involvement helps explain medial forearm/hand symptoms and intrinsic hand-muscle weakness in the lecture’s anatomical cases."],"keyPoints":["First-rib vein anterior, artery posterior to anterior scalene.","Cervical rib can affect C8–T1/lower trunk and subclavian artery."],"example":"A cervical rib with medial-hand symptoms points toward lower-plexus compression rather than a lesion of the phrenic nerve.","pitfalls":["Floating ribs 11–12 have no tubercular joint with a transverse process."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":20},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":21},{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A2-focus-4","kind":"exam","label":"Assessed lecture objectives","evidence":"We definitely will test you on those objectives.","teaching":"Apply the listed objective to this topic; it is an assessment cue, not a promised exact question.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}}]},{"id":"CVP-A2:week1-5","concept":"CVP-A2:5","title":"Thoracic vertebrae and articulation exceptions","body":["Typical thoracic vertebrae have costal demifacets on their bodies and a facet on the transverse process. The body facets receive rib heads while the transverse facet receives a rib tubercle. T1 has a complete superior facet for rib 1 and an inferior demifacet for rib 2; lower thoracic levels depart from the typical pattern.","T11 and T12 lack transverse costal facets because their ribs lack tubercles. Learn the joint by matching the rib feature to the vertebral feature, then apply the exceptions. A head does not normally articulate with a spinous process."],"keyPoints":["Rib head ↔ vertebral body; rib tubercle ↔ transverse process.","T11/T12 lack the typical transverse costal articulation."],"example":"A vertebra with a complete first-rib facet and an inferior demifacet for the second rib is T1.","pitfalls":["Transverse costal facets and vertebral body demifacets receive different rib parts."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":23},{"source":"cvp-slides-a2","page":24},{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A2-focus-5","kind":"exam","label":"Assessed lecture objectives","evidence":"We definitely will test you on those objectives.","teaching":"Apply the listed objective to this topic; it is an assessment cue, not a promised exact question.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a2","page":2}}]}],"summary":["Mediastinum centrally, pleural compartments laterally.","Vertical line plus intercostal level gives a reproducible surface location.","Sternal angle → second costal cartilage → T4/T5 plane.","Typical head joins two neighboring bodies; tubercle joins the same-number transverse process.","First-rib vein anterior, artery posterior to anterior scalene.","Rib head ↔ vertebral body; rib tubercle ↔ transverse process."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-A2-w1-blank-0","prompt":"The sternal angle marks the ___ costal cartilage.","answers":["second","2nd","2"],"explanation":"This landmark anchors rib counting."},{"id":"CVP-A2-w1-blank-1","prompt":"The typical rib tubercle articulates with a vertebral ___ process.","answers":["transverse"],"explanation":"The rib head instead articulates with vertebral bodies."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-A2-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Thoracic cavity, wall and apertures","definition":"Mediastinum centrally, pleural compartments laterally."},{"term":"Surface lines: locate before you label","definition":"Vertical line plus intercostal level gives a reproducible surface location."},{"term":"Sternum and the sternal-angle landmark","definition":"Sternal angle → second costal cartilage → T4/T5 plane."},{"term":"Typical ribs, joints and the costal groove","definition":"Typical head joins two neighboring bodies; tubercle joins the same-number transverse process."},{"term":"Atypical ribs and cervical-rib compression","definition":"First-rib vein anterior, artery posterior to anterior scalene."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}]},"CVP-A3":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain read a heart-wall section from lumen outward.","Explain recognize cardiomyocytes on light microscopy.","Explain intercalated discs, dyads and physiological syncytium.","Explain purkinje fibers: pale, large and subendocardial.","Explain valve histology and electrical insulation.","Explain injury, repair and which layer is affected."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-A3:week1-1","concept":"CVP-A3:1","title":"Read a heart-wall section from lumen outward","body":["The endocardium lines the blood-facing lumen with endothelium, subendothelial connective tissue and a deeper subendocardial layer. The myocardium is the thick working cardiac muscle. The epicardium is visceral serous pericardium: surface mesothelium over connective tissue, often containing fat, coronary vessels and nerves.","Both endothelium and mesothelium are simple squamous epithelia, but face different spaces. A blood-filled lumen and subendocardial Purkinje cells indicate the inner side; a serous surface with coronary vessels in fat indicates the outer side."],"keyPoints":["Inside → endocardium → myocardium → epicardium.","Endothelium faces blood; mesothelium faces the serous cavity."],"example":"Fat containing a coronary vessel adjacent to mesothelium suggests epicardium, not the blood-facing endocardium.","pitfalls":["The entire pericardial sac is not the epicardium; epicardium is the visceral serous layer."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":3},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":8},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":29}]},{"id":"CVP-A3:week1-0","concept":"CVP-A3:0","title":"Recognize cardiomyocytes on light microscopy","body":["Working cardiomyocytes are short branching striated cells, usually with one central nucleus and occasionally two. Intercalated discs form dark transverse or step-like boundaries between cells. Endomysial connective tissue contains capillaries between fibers. Skeletal muscle is also striated, but its long cylindrical fibers have numerous peripheral nuclei.","In cross section, a cut may miss a nucleus; lack of a visible nucleus in one profile does not prove that the cell is anucleate. Use several features together: branching, central nuclei, striations and discs, while recognizing that section orientation changes their visibility."],"keyPoints":["Cardiac: branching, central nuclei, intercalated discs.","Skeletal: long parallel fibers and multiple peripheral nuclei."],"example":"A striated tissue with branching fibers and dark end-to-end junctions is cardiac even if only a few nuclei are visible.","pitfalls":["Striation alone does not distinguish cardiac from skeletal muscle."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":11},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":14},{"source":"cvp-t-a3","page":6}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A3-focus-0","kind":"exam","label":"Cardiac fiber and physiological syncytium","evidence":"The cardiomyocytes in a chain connected through intercalated disc ... We are doing it this way for exam.","teaching":"Separate cells coupled by gap junctions differ from a fused skeletal morphological syncytium.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a3","page":6}}]},{"id":"CVP-A3:week1-3","concept":"CVP-A3:3","title":"Intercalated discs, dyads and physiological syncytium","body":["Intercalated discs contain interdigitating membranes, fascia adherens, desmosomes and gap junctions. Adhering junctions transmit force and prevent separation; connexin-containing gap junctions permit current flow. The cells act as a physiological syncytium while retaining separate cell membranes, unlike a fused morphological syncytium.","Cardiac T-tubules lie near Z lines. A T-tubule associated with one SR terminal cisterna forms a dyad. Numerous mitochondria between myofibrils support continuous ATP demand. These ultrastructural features connect electrical delivery, calcium signaling and sustained mechanical work."],"keyPoints":["Disc: mechanical junctions plus electrical gap junctions.","Cardiac dyad = one T-tubule plus one SR cisterna."],"example":"A junction defect can impair mechanical attachment or electrical spread depending on which disc component is affected.","pitfalls":["Connexins are gap-junction proteins, not the same as collagen or troponin."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":17}]},{"id":"CVP-A3:week1-2","concept":"CVP-A3:2","title":"Purkinje fibers: pale, large and subendocardial","body":["Purkinje fibers are specialized cardiac muscle cells in the ventricular subendocardial conduction network. They are relatively large and pale because they contain abundant glycogen and fewer myofibrils, often toward the periphery. They conduct rapidly and can act as slower latent pacemakers when normal drive is lost.","They are not peripheral nerves despite belonging to the intrinsic conduction system. Working myocytes are more eosinophilic with abundant force-generating myofibrils. The location beneath endocardium and contrast with nearby working muscle are valuable histological identifiers."],"keyPoints":["Purkinje: pale glycogen-rich modified myocytes in subendocardium.","Working cells prioritize contraction; Purkinje cells prioritize rapid conduction."],"example":"A pale cell group beneath endothelium beside darker myocardium is a better Purkinje clue than pale staining alone.","pitfalls":["The word “fiber” does not make a Purkinje cell a neuron."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":23},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":25},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":27}]},{"id":"CVP-A3:week1-4","concept":"CVP-A3:4","title":"Valve histology and electrical insulation","body":["Valve leaflets have a fibroelastic connective-tissue core covered by endothelium. AV chordae are collagen-rich cords that attach to papillary muscles and resist leaflet prolapse during systole. Semilunar valves lack chordae. The fibrous skeleton anchors valve rings and muscle and electrically separates ordinary atrial from ventricular myocardium.","The AV bundle provides the normal conducting bridge across that insulation. Thus fibrous tissue can be mechanically useful while being electrically insulating. Endothelial injury can encourage thrombotic deposition; fibrosis or calcification can impair valve mobility."],"keyPoints":["Fibrous core with endothelial covering describes a leaflet.","AV bundle crosses the insulating fibrous skeleton."],"example":"A fibrous ring can support an AV valve yet prevent direct atrial-to-ventricular myocardial impulse spread.","pitfalls":["Papillary muscles prevent prolapse; they do not actively pull the valve open."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":26}]},{"id":"CVP-A3:week1-5","concept":"CVP-A3:5","title":"Injury, repair and which layer is affected","body":["Myocardial injury can release cardiac troponin components into blood. Substantial loss of adult working myocardium is repaired mainly by fibrous scar, which does not replace the original contractile function. The effect depends on location and extent; a scar is not a new patch of normal pumping muscle.","Use suffixes anatomically: pericarditis affects pericardium, myocarditis myocardium and endocarditis the endocardial/valvular surface. Inflammation is not synonymous with infection in every case. A pericardial collection can impair filling, while a myocardial lesion can impair force and a valve lesion can impair forward-flow control."],"keyPoints":["Scar replaces lost tissue structurally without restoring normal contractile cells.","Layer-specific disease names describe location, not a complete cause."],"example":"Contrast a stiff external sac restricting filling with damaged myocardium generating less force: similar low output can arise by different mechanisms.","pitfalls":["Blood troponin elevation indicates myocardial injury; the marker alone does not specify every cause."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":24},{"source":"cvp-slides-a3","page":28}]}],"summary":["Inside → endocardium → myocardium → epicardium.","Cardiac: branching, central nuclei, intercalated discs.","Disc: mechanical junctions plus electrical gap junctions.","Purkinje: pale glycogen-rich modified myocytes in subendocardium.","Fibrous core with endothelial covering describes a leaflet.","Scar replaces lost tissue structurally without restoring normal contractile cells."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-A3-w1-blank-0","prompt":"Gap-junction channel proteins are called ___.","answers":["connexins"],"explanation":"They provide electrical communication between cells."},{"id":"CVP-A3-w1-blank-1","prompt":"Pale ventricular conducting cells are located in the ___.","answers":["subendocardium","subendocardial layer"],"explanation":"Purkinje cells lie beneath the ventricular endocardium."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-A3-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Read a heart-wall section from lumen outward","definition":"Inside → endocardium → myocardium → epicardium."},{"term":"Recognize cardiomyocytes on light microscopy","definition":"Cardiac: branching, central nuclei, intercalated discs."},{"term":"Intercalated discs, dyads and physiological syncytium","definition":"Disc: mechanical junctions plus electrical gap junctions."},{"term":"Purkinje fibers: pale, large and subendocardial","definition":"Purkinje: pale glycogen-rich modified myocytes in subendocardium."},{"term":"Valve histology and electrical insulation","definition":"Fibrous core with endothelial covering describes a leaflet."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}]},"CVP-A4":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain pericardial layers and the lubricated cavity.","Explain transverse and oblique sinuses are different spaces.","Explain phrenic nerves, blood supply and referred pain.","Explain apex, base, surfaces and borders.","Explain sulci, auricles and vessel routes.","Explain effusion, tamponade and pericardial friction.","Explain cardiac silhouette: radiographic contour versus chamber border."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-A4:week1-0","concept":"CVP-A4:0","title":"Pericardial layers and the lubricated cavity","body":["The heart lies in the middle mediastinum inside a fibroserous pericardial sac. Tough fibrous pericardium limits excessive displacement/distension and attaches inferiorly to the diaphragm’s central tendon. Serous pericardium has a parietal layer lining the fibrous sac and a visceral layer adherent to the heart: the epicardium.","The pericardial cavity is the potential space between the two serous layers, containing a thin lubricating film. The fist-in-a-balloon analogy helps: the fist is covered by the invaginated visceral surface, while the outer balloon wall is parietal. The fist is not inside the potential space itself."],"keyPoints":["Fibrous → parietal serous → cavity → visceral serous/epicardium → myocardium.","The cavity lies between serous surfaces."],"example":"Fluid in the pericardial cavity separates parietal and visceral serous layers; it is not normally inside myocardium.","pitfalls":["Visceral and parietal are layers of serous pericardium, not two layers of myocardium."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":11},{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":5}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A4-focus-0","kind":"exam","label":"Definition and layers of pericardium","evidence":"If someone ask you what is pericardium, which we might do in the exam situation.","teaching":"Outer fibrous plus inner parietal/visceral serous layers; locate the cavity.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":5}}]},{"id":"CVP-A4:week1-2","concept":"CVP-A4:2","title":"Transverse and oblique sinuses are different spaces","body":["Serous reflections around arterial and venous roots produce pericardial recesses. The transverse sinus passes behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the SVC/upper atrial region. It separates the arterial outflow sleeve from the venous inflow reflections; its location explains the surgical concept of passing behind the arterial roots.","The oblique sinus is a blind recess behind the left atrium, bounded by venous reflections around pulmonary veins and IVC. It is not a through-passage equivalent to the transverse sinus. Posterior pericardium separates this recess from the esophagus, explaining close posterior relations."],"keyPoints":["Transverse: behind aorta/pulmonary trunk, anterior to SVC.","Oblique: blind recess posterior to LA."],"example":"A probe behind both great arterial roots occupies the transverse sinus; a recess behind the left atrium is oblique.","pitfalls":["“Posterior to the heart” is too vague: identify which chamber or vessel is adjacent."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":8},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":9},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":11},{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":13}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A4-focus-2","kind":"exam","label":"Transverse sinus: structures in front and behind","evidence":"One of our very favorite questions ... I may ask you what is in front of my finger.","teaching":"Anterior: ascending aorta/pulmonary trunk; posterior: SVC/upper atrial region. The lecturer calls transverse sinus must-know.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":13}}]},{"id":"CVP-A4:week1-1","concept":"CVP-A4:1","title":"Phrenic nerves, blood supply and referred pain","body":["Fibrous pericardium and parietal serous pericardium receive somatic sensory fibers through phrenic nerves (C3–C5). Pain can be referred toward the shoulder region through shared spinal segments. Visceral serous pericardium has autonomic supply and is relatively insensitive to ordinary somatic pain. The source’s phrase “parietal layer of visceral pericardium” is a wording error; the intended layer is parietal serous.","Pericardiacophrenic vessels accompany the phrenic nerves between fibrous pericardium and mediastinal pleura. Pericardiacophrenic arteries arise from internal thoracic arteries; the visceral layer is supplied through coronary vessels. Phrenic nerves pass anterior to lung roots, while vagus nerves pass posterior."],"keyPoints":["Phrenic somatic afferents → fibrous/parietal pain and shoulder referral.","Pericardiacophrenic vessels travel with phrenic nerves."],"example":"An anatomy question about shoulder-referred pericardial pain tests C3–C5/phrenic pathways rather than a coronary-artery course.","pitfalls":["Phrenic and vagus relations to a lung root are opposite."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":8},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":13},{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":7}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A4-focus-1","kind":"exam","label":"Phrenic nerve and layer-specific sensation","evidence":"You will find 2 to 3 questions, MCQs in the finals based on phrenic, minimum 1 station in OSPE exam.","teaching":"This records the lecturer’s emphasis, not a guarantee. Contrast somatic fibrous/parietal with visceral autonomic supply; page15 mentions prior SAQs.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":7}},{"id":"CVP-A4-focus-saq","kind":"exam","label":"Layer-specific innervation in prior SAQs","evidence":"This question ... for last 2 years in SAQ ... everybody is writing phrenic nerve.","teaching":"Do not write phrenic for every layer: distinguish fibrous/parietal somatic sensation from visceral autonomic innervation.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":15}}]},{"id":"CVP-A4:week1-3","concept":"CVP-A4:3","title":"Apex, base, surfaces and borders","body":["The apex is mainly LV and projects near the left fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line. The base faces posteriorly and is mainly LA receiving pulmonary veins. The sternocostal surface is mostly RV; the diaphragmatic surface is mainly LV with a smaller RV contribution. Right pulmonary surface is mainly RA and left pulmonary surface mainly LV.","The right border is chiefly RA; inferior border chiefly RV; left border chiefly LV with left auricle superiorly. These are three-dimensional relations: a structure forming a border in one projection is not necessarily the chamber that forms the entire surface behind it."],"keyPoints":["Apex LV; base LA; anterior surface RV.","Inferior surface mainly LV, but inferior border mainly RV."],"example":"A question changing “inferior surface” to “inferior border” changes the best chamber answer.","pitfalls":["The cardiac base is posterior, not the point resting on the diaphragm."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":3},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":4},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":20}]},{"id":"CVP-A4:week1-4","concept":"CVP-A4:4","title":"Sulci, auricles and vessel routes","body":["The coronary or AV sulcus encircles the atrioventricular junction. Right coronary vessels occupy its right region, while circumflex-related vessels and the coronary sinus occupy portions of the left/posterior groove. The anterior interventricular sulcus contains the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) and great cardiac vein; the posterior interventricular sulcus contains the posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein.","These external grooves mark chamber/septal relationships rather than empty internal channels. Auricles are atrial appendages. The great vessels connect RA to venae cavae, LA to pulmonary veins, RV to pulmonary trunk and LV to aorta; arteries and veins are named by direction of flow, not oxygen content."],"keyPoints":["Anterior IV groove: LAD + great cardiac vein.","Posterior IV groove: posterior IV artery + middle cardiac vein."],"example":"A vessel pair descending the anterior ventricular groove links an arterial supply route with the great cardiac vein.","pitfalls":["Pulmonary arteries carry blood away from the heart despite lower oxygen content."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":17},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":18},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":19},{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":17}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A4-focus-4","kind":"exam","label":"LAD and anterior interventricular groove","evidence":"LAD ... one of our favorite ... thing to be asked and assessed.","teaching":"Pair LAD with the great cardiac vein in the anterior interventricular sulcus.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":17}}]},{"id":"CVP-A4:week1-5","concept":"CVP-A4:5","title":"Effusion, tamponade and pericardial friction","body":["An effusion is excess fluid in the pericardial cavity. If pericardial pressure impairs chamber filling and reduces output, the physiological state is tamponade. Rate of accumulation and sac compliance matter: a rapidly rising pressure may be important even before the largest absolute volume is reached.","Inflamed serous surfaces may rub and create a friction sound. Thickened or scarred pericardium can also constrain expansion by a different process. Pericardiocentesis refers to clinician-performed drainage of the cavity; the anatomical purpose is to relieve external pressure, not to remove blood from inside a ventricular chamber."],"keyPoints":["Effusion describes fluid; tamponade describes impaired filling from pressure.","Accumulation rate and compliance influence the pressure effect."],"example":"Two effusions of equal volume may produce different filling impairment if one accumulates rapidly in a less compliant sac.","pitfalls":["Not every effusion automatically means tamponade."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-a4","page":23}]},{"id":"CVP-A4:transcript-6","concept":"CVP-A4:6","title":"Cardiac silhouette: radiographic contour versus chamber border","body":["An anatomical border and the outline seen on a chest radiograph are related but not interchangeable. Anatomically the right heart border is chiefly RA. On the standard frontal silhouette, the right mediastinal/cardiac contour includes SVC superiorly and RA below, with a short inferior caval contribution sometimes visible.","On the left, identify the aortic knuckle, pulmonary trunk region, left atrial appendage region and LV/apex in superior-to-inferior order. The main LA lies posteriorly and does not simply form the entire left contour. The lecturer explicitly describes a labeled radiograph as an assessment format."],"keyPoints":["Right contour: SVC and RA, sometimes a short inferior caval segment.","Left contour includes aortic knuckle, pulmonary trunk region and LV."],"example":"A superior left contour label may identify the aortic arch rather than a chamber; a lower left contour is predominantly LV.","pitfalls":["Use the actual projection and marked level; not every normal radiograph clearly shows every listed contribution."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":21},{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":21}],"highYieldAddition":true,"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A4-focus-6","kind":"exam","label":"Identify the marked chest-X-ray contour","evidence":"We will give you this X-ray and we will ask you name the structure.","teaching":"Distinguish the radiographic contour and marked level from a generic chamber-border label.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a4","page":21}}]}],"summary":["Fibrous → parietal serous → cavity → visceral serous/epicardium → myocardium.","Transverse: behind aorta/pulmonary trunk, anterior to SVC.","Phrenic somatic afferents → fibrous/parietal pain and shoulder referral.","Apex LV; base LA; anterior surface RV.","Anterior IV groove: LAD + great cardiac vein.","Effusion describes fluid; tamponade describes impaired filling from pressure.","Right contour: SVC and RA, sometimes a short inferior caval segment."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed.","Source wording correction: the sensitive layer is parietal serous pericardium, not a parietal layer of visceral pericardium."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-A4-w1-blank-0","prompt":"The posterior cardiac base is mainly the left ___.","answers":["atrium"],"explanation":"LA receives pulmonary veins posteriorly."},{"id":"CVP-A4-w1-blank-1","prompt":"The somatic nerve carrying parietal-pericardial pain is the ___ nerve.","answers":["phrenic"],"explanation":"C3–C5 afferents explain referred shoulder pain."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-A4-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Pericardial layers and the lubricated cavity","definition":"Fibrous → parietal serous → cavity → visceral serous/epicardium → myocardium."},{"term":"Transverse and oblique sinuses are different spaces","definition":"Transverse: behind aorta/pulmonary trunk, anterior to SVC."},{"term":"Phrenic nerves, blood supply and referred pain","definition":"Phrenic somatic afferents → fibrous/parietal pain and shoulder referral."},{"term":"Apex, base, surfaces and borders","definition":"Apex LV; base LA; anterior surface RV."},{"term":"Sulci, auricles and vessel routes","definition":"Anterior IV groove: LAD + great cardiac vein."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}]},"CVP-A5":{"overview":"Follow the supplied lecture from its core structures or mechanisms into interpretation and application. Study one topic, then answer its questions immediately below. Your original CVP schedule and review passes stay independent.","objectives":["Explain right atrium: smooth venous inflow and rough appendage.","Explain right ventricle: inflow, outflow and moderator band.","Explain left atrium, left ventricle and septum.","Explain aV valves and semilunar valves: pressure opens them.","Explain fetal remnants and neighboring nerves.","Explain fibrous skeleton and heart sounds.","Explain auscultation sites differ from valve positions.","Explain conduction landmarks and autonomic modulation."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-0","concept":"CVP-A5:0","title":"Right atrium: smooth venous inflow and rough appendage","body":["The RA receives SVC, IVC and coronary sinus blood. Crista terminalis separates the smooth sinus venarum from the rough pectinate-muscle region and auricle. The fossa ovalis is the adult septal landmark of fetal interatrial communication. Small venous valves may mark the IVC and coronary sinus openings.","Trace inflow through the tricuspid valve to RV. Relate the smooth and rough regions to different developmental contributions rather than assuming all atrial walls look alike. The SA node lies near the upper crista/SVC junction, while the AV node is near the coronary sinus opening and septal tricuspid region."],"keyPoints":["RA receives SVC, IVC and coronary sinus.","Crista terminalis separates smooth and pectinate regions."],"example":"A right atrial ridge dividing smooth venous tissue from rough muscle is crista terminalis, not a ventricular moderator band.","pitfalls":["The fossa ovalis is a septal remnant, not the opening of a pulmonary vein."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":5},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":6},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":9}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-3","concept":"CVP-A5:3","title":"Right ventricle: inflow, outflow and moderator band","body":["The RV receives through the tricuspid valve and ejects through the pulmonary valve. Its inflow wall contains trabeculae carneae; the smooth outflow region is the infundibulum or conus arteriosus. Papillary muscles attach to AV valve cusps through chordae and resist prolapse during contraction.","The septomarginal trabecula, or moderator band, connects septal region to the anterior papillary muscle and carries part of the right bundle branch. This links a visible internal anatomical feature with fast activation of the anterior papillary apparatus."],"keyPoints":["RV smooth outflow = infundibulum.","Moderator band carries right bundle-branch fibers to anterior papillary muscle."],"example":"A band crossing the RV cavity is a conduction-related trabecula, while a slender cord attaching to a cusp is a chorda.","pitfalls":["Trabeculae carneae are ventricular; pectinate muscles are atrial."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":10},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":11}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-1","concept":"CVP-A5:1","title":"Left atrium, left ventricle and septum","body":["The LA receives pulmonary veins and forms most of the posterior base. Its main chamber is comparatively smooth, with pectinate muscle largely in its auricle. Blood crosses the two-cusp mitral valve into LV. The LV wall is much thicker than RV because it generates the higher pressures of systemic circulation.","LV has two main papillary muscles, each supplying chordae to both mitral leaflets. The smooth aortic vestibule leads to the aortic valve. The ventricular septum has a large muscular part and a small membranous part; the latter lies close to the AV bundle, so anatomy links septal defects and conduction relationships."],"keyPoints":["LA receives pulmonary veins; LV ejects into aorta.","LV aortic vestibule is smooth; membranous septum is near AV conduction tissue."],"example":"A papillary muscle does not belong to only one mitral leaflet: its chordae can support both.","pitfalls":["The thicker LV wall reflects pressure work, not simply a larger chamber volume in every state."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":12},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":13},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":14},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":15},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":16},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":17}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-2","concept":"CVP-A5:2","title":"AV valves and semilunar valves: pressure opens them","body":["The tricuspid valve guards RA→RV and the mitral valve LA→LV. Their cusps open when atrial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure and close as the gradient reverses. Chordae and papillary muscles restrain AV cusps during systole; they neither pull them open nor create the pressure that closes them.","Aortic and pulmonary valves each have three semilunar cusps and no chordae. They open when ventricular pressure exceeds arterial pressure and close when arterial pressure predominates. Coronary ostia lie in the right and left aortic sinuses, not in the pulmonary valve sinuses."],"keyPoints":["Valves move primarily with pressure gradients.","Only AV valves have chordae and papillary support."],"example":"If LV pressure rises above LA pressure, mitral closure is favored; aortic opening still waits until LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure.","pitfalls":["Papillary contraction prevents prolapse into atria; it does not open AV valves."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":19},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":20},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":21},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":22},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":23},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":24},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":25}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-4","concept":"CVP-A5:4","title":"Fetal remnants and neighboring nerves","body":["The foramen ovale leaves the fossa ovalis after closure; the ductus arteriosus becomes ligamentum arteriosum. The umbilical vein becomes ligamentum teres hepatis, ductus venosus ligamentum venosum, and distal umbilical arteries medial umbilical ligaments. These remnants retain clues to fetal flow even when they no longer carry blood.","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops beneath the aortic arch near ligamentum arteriosum before ascending toward the larynx. This relationship explains how an aortic-region process can affect voice. The right recurrent laryngeal nerve has a different loop around the right subclavian artery."],"keyPoints":["Ductus arteriosus → ligamentum arteriosum.","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops below the aortic arch."],"example":"A ligament between the pulmonary-artery region and aortic arch is a fetal arterial connection remnant, not a chord inside a ventricle.","pitfalls":["Ligamentum arteriosum and ligamentum venosum are different remnants in different regions."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":7},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":8}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-5","concept":"CVP-A5:5","title":"Fibrous skeleton and heart sounds","body":["Dense connective tissue around the valves forms the fibrous skeleton, anchoring cusps and myocardium while insulating ordinary atrial from ventricular muscle. The AV bundle is the normal bridge through this insulation. Mechanical support and electrical separation are two functions of the same framework.","S1 is associated with closure/tensing of the AV valve apparatus near the start of ventricular systole. S2 is associated with semilunar closure near the end of ejection. Sounds arise from vibrations of blood and cardiac structures during these events, not simply from two rigid doors clicking together."],"keyPoints":["S1: AV closure; S2: aortic/pulmonary closure.","Fibrous rings anchor tissue and electrically insulate atria from ventricles."],"example":"Between S1 and S2 lies most of ventricular systole; after S2 the ventricles relax and later fill.","pitfalls":["Normal valve opening is not the principal source of S1 or S2."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":27},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":28},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":29},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":30},{"source":"cvp-t-a5","page":37}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A5-focus-5","kind":"exam","label":"S1 and S2: closure, not opening","evidence":"I always bring this as an exam question ... It’s always due to closure!","teaching":"S1 relates to AV closure; S2 to semilunar closure.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a5","page":37}}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-6","concept":"CVP-A5:6","title":"Auscultation sites differ from valve positions","body":["Anatomical valves lie relatively close together behind the sternum, but their sounds are heard at useful chest locations along transmitted flow. The aortic area is second right intercostal space at the sternal border, pulmonary second left, tricuspid lower left sternal border around fourth/fifth spaces, and mitral near the apex in the fifth left space at the midclavicular line.","These are conventional listening areas, not claims that the valve itself sits directly beneath that point. Know the order and side, then connect the mitral area to the LV apex and aortic area to right upper sternal transmission."],"keyPoints":["Aortic right 2nd; pulmonary left 2nd; tricuspid lower left sternal; mitral apex.","Listening area is not exact anatomical valve projection."],"example":"A mitral sound is assessed near the apex even though the valve itself is more central and posterior.","pitfalls":["Do not swap the right aortic and left pulmonary second-space areas."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":31},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":32},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":33},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":34},{"source":"cvp-t-a5","page":39}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A5-focus-6","kind":"lecturer","label":"Memorize the listening areas","evidence":"Auscultation areas ... the rest you’re going to have to memorize it.","teaching":"Listening sites follow transmitted sound rather than exact valve projections.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a5","page":39}}]},{"id":"CVP-A5:week1-7","concept":"CVP-A5:7","title":"Conduction landmarks and autonomic modulation","body":["The SA node lies near the SVC–RA junction. The AV node lies in the inferior interatrial/septal region near the coronary sinus opening and septal tricuspid leaflet. His bundle crosses the fibrous skeleton and descends into the septal conduction network; right bundle fibers use the moderator band, while Purkinje terminals lie subendocardially.","Sympathetic input increases rate, AV conduction and contractile force. Vagal parasympathetic input mainly slows SA firing and AV conduction, with its strongest direct inotropic effects in atria rather than a uniformly strong direct effect on all ventricular muscle. The heart’s intrinsic system initiates rhythm; the autonomic system adjusts it."],"keyPoints":["SA node near SVC–RA; AV node near coronary-sinus/septal-tricuspid region.","Autonomic nerves modulate an intrinsically generated rhythm."],"example":"A vagal change can lengthen the time to the next sinus impulse and slow AV transmission without acting like a new motor impulse for every ventricular cell.","pitfalls":["The intrinsic conduction system is specialized muscle tissue, not an ordinary peripheral nerve chain."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":36},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":37},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":38},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":39},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":40},{"source":"cvp-slides-a5","page":41},{"source":"cvp-t-a5","page":45}],"examFocus":[{"id":"CVP-A5-focus-7","kind":"lecturer","label":"Node locations and the AV bundle route","evidence":"What I want you guys to remember is the location of these nodes ... bundle of His ... fibrous skeleton and ... membranous ... septum.","teaching":"Connect conduction landmarks with the anatomical significance of membranous VSD.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-a5","page":45}}]}],"summary":["RA receives SVC, IVC and coronary sinus.","RV smooth outflow = infundibulum.","LA receives pulmonary veins; LV ejects into aorta.","Valves move primarily with pressure gradients.","Ductus arteriosus → ligamentum arteriosum.","S1: AV closure; S2: aortic/pulmonary closure.","Aortic right 2nd; pulmonary left 2nd; tricuspid lower left sternal; mitral apex.","SA node near SVC–RA; AV node near coronary-sinus/septal-tricuspid region."],"sourceNotes":["Authored teaching and application questions are linked to supplied slide/page references. They are not verbatim previous-exam questions.","PDF previews can omit some embedded vector figures. The presentation download retains them; embedded narration, where present, is playable in the source reader and has not been transcribed."],"fillBlanks":[{"id":"CVP-A5-w1-blank-0","prompt":"The smooth RV outflow is the ___.","answers":["infundibulum","conus arteriosus"],"explanation":"The LV smooth outflow is the aortic vestibule."},{"id":"CVP-A5-w1-blank-1","prompt":"S2 is associated with closure of the ___ valves.","answers":["semilunar","aortic and pulmonary","pulmonary and aortic"],"explanation":"Aortic/pulmonary closure marks the end of ejection."}],"matching":[{"id":"CVP-A5-w1-match","title":"Connect the lecture’s key relationships","pairs":[{"term":"Right atrium: smooth venous inflow and rough appendage","definition":"RA receives SVC, IVC and coronary sinus."},{"term":"Right ventricle: inflow, outflow and moderator band","definition":"RV smooth outflow = infundibulum."},{"term":"Left atrium, left ventricle and septum","definition":"LA receives pulmonary veins; LV ejects into aorta."},{"term":"AV valves and semilunar valves: pressure opens them","definition":"Valves move primarily with pressure gradients."},{"term":"Fetal remnants and neighboring nerves","definition":"Ductus arteriosus → ligamentum arteriosum."}],"explanation":"Use the mechanism or anatomical relationship to explain each match, rather than memorizing the position of an answer."}]},"CVP-P5":{"overview":"Study the uploaded transcription one topic at a time, then apply it immediately in its questions.","objectives":["Explain an electrical beat is not automatically an effective pulse.","Explain coordinated excitation, calcium activation and recovery.","Explain filling, opposing load and contractile state in rhythm problems.","Explain localize an early beat: atrium, junction or ventricle.","Explain re-entry: compare travel time with recovery time.","Explain af, flutter and multifocal atrial tachycardia.","Explain accessory pathways and arrest versus infarction."],"sections":[{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-0","concept":"CVP-P5:0","title":"An electrical beat is not automatically an effective pulse","body":["An ECG records electrical activation, whereas a peripheral pulse depends on effective ejection. A premature beat shortens the preceding filling interval, reducing end-diastolic volume and potentially stroke volume. It may therefore produce a weak or undetectable peripheral pulse despite a visible QRS. In ventricular fibrillation, fragmented activation causes uncoordinated mechanical activity rather than an effective synchronous ejection."],"keyPoints":["Pulse deficit separates electrical events from effective mechanical output.","Short premature filling can reduce preload and stroke volume."],"example":"A premature QRS seen on ECG with no corresponding palpable pulse illustrates an electromechanical mismatch, not necessarily absence of depolarization.","pitfalls":["A QRS does not by itself prove normal cardiac output."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":3},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":6},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":8},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":14},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":15}],"highYieldAddition":true},{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-1","concept":"CVP-P5:1","title":"Coordinated excitation, calcium activation and recovery","body":["Normal excitation spreads rapidly enough to activate ventricular muscle coherently, calcium links activation to force, and repolarization/refractoriness prepares tissue for another cycle. A long absolute/effective refractory period limits repeated re-excitation during a twitch. Recovery is spatially heterogeneous during arrhythmia: some tissue is refractory while other tissue is already excitable."],"keyPoints":["Coordinated activation and recovery matter alongside the presence of individual action potentials.","A returning wave must encounter recovered excitable tissue to propagate."],"example":"A ring of cells can all generate action potentials yet fail to eject effectively if their timing becomes fragmented.","pitfalls":["The lecturer’s informal voltage/power analogies are not physical device settings to memorize."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":14},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":15},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":17},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":18}],"highYieldAddition":true},{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-2","concept":"CVP-P5:2","title":"Filling, opposing load and contractile state in rhythm problems","body":["Use the earlier force concepts to interpret a rhythm problem. Preload is initial stretch/filling; afterload opposes ejection; contractility describes force generation at a given starting length and loading condition. An early beat can reduce filling time and preload without proving an acute change in intrinsic contractility. Conversely, electrical disorganization may severely impair effective output even when individual cells can still contract."],"keyPoints":["A short diastolic interval first threatens filling/preload.","Do not call every fall in stroke volume reduced intrinsic contractility."],"example":"If the only stated change is less filling before a premature beat, begin with preload reasoning before inventing a new valvular obstruction.","pitfalls":["This is an integrated high-yield application of earlier physiology, not an extra official lecture event."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":3},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":8},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":14},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":15}],"highYieldAddition":true},{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-3","concept":"CVP-P5:3","title":"Localize an early beat: atrium, junction or ventricle","body":["Classify a premature beat using timing, P-wave relationship, QRS width and repolarization. An early altered P followed by normal ventricular conduction supports atrial ectopy. A junctional impulse may activate atria retrogradely, burying or shifting P around QRS. A ventricular focus typically produces a broad unusual complex because activation spreads outside the normal rapid sequence; its T often points opposite the main QRS."],"keyPoints":["Timing plus P–QRS relation is more informative than rate alone.","PACs usually reset sinus timing; typical PVCs can have a full compensatory pause."],"example":"Compare an early narrow complex with an abnormal preceding P against an early broad complex with no preceding sinus P.","pitfalls":["The source calls the PAC pause compensatory; the usual noncompensatory PAC distinction is retained in the corrected teaching."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":4},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":5},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":6},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":7},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":8},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":9}],"highYieldAddition":true},{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-4","concept":"CVP-P5:4","title":"Re-entry: compare travel time with recovery time","body":["A wave normally extinguishes when it reaches tissue that cannot yet be re-excited. An available loop with unidirectional block, sufficiently slow conduction and recovered tissue can sustain re-entry. Longer pathway length or shorter refractory duration can let the wave return after recovery. The key comparison is circuit travel time versus tissue recovery time, not the diagram’s artistic size."],"keyPoints":["Longer circuit travel relative to refractoriness can permit another cycle.","A repetitive ventricular tachycardia and chaotic VF are different organized patterns."],"example":"If a wave returns after 240 ms but the tissue recovers after 200 ms, re-excitation is possible; if it returns after 150 ms, that tissue is still refractory.","pitfalls":["Laser/heat ablation is explicitly described as outside this lecture’s assessment scope; no procedural treatment instructions are added."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":10},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":13},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":14},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":16},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":17},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":18},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":19},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":20}],"highYieldAddition":true},{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-5","concept":"CVP-P5:5","title":"AF, flutter and multifocal atrial tachycardia","body":["AF lacks consistent discrete P waves and usually gives an irregularly irregular ventricular response. Flutter has repetitive organized atrial activity, often sawtooth-like; a fixed conduction ratio can create a regular ventricular rhythm. The comparison table also includes multifocal atrial tachycardia: unlike AF, it has discrete P waves with at least three different morphologies. Do not equate every irregular rhythm with AF."],"keyPoints":["AF: disorganized atrial baseline; flutter: organized repetitive waves.","MAT: irregular rhythm with at least three discrete P morphologies."],"example":"Atrial rate 300/min with 3:1 conduction yields ventricular rate about 100/min.","pitfalls":["The transcript reproduces simplified treatment statements in a comparison table; this learning topic focuses on recognition."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":22},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":23},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":24}],"highYieldAddition":true},{"id":"CVP-P5:transcript-6","concept":"CVP-P5:6","title":"Accessory pathways and arrest versus infarction","body":["An accessory AV pathway can activate ventricular myocardium before the usual AV nodal delay has finished, giving a short PR and slurred initial ventricular activation (delta wave). An early slur is not simply a renamed Q wave. The abnormal pathway may also supply part of a re-entry circuit.","The final comparison distinguishes arrest, the loss of effective circulation, from myocardial infarction, ischemic myocardial injury. Either may trigger the other in some circumstances, but the terms are not interchangeable."],"keyPoints":["Pre-excitation links a bypass pathway to short PR/delta wave.","Arrest is a circulatory state; infarction is tissue injury."],"example":"A slurred QRS onset plus short PR suggests pre-excitation even though the rest of the ventricular complex is still visible.","pitfalls":["A visible rhythm alone does not establish effective circulation."],"references":[{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":24},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":25},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":26},{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":27}],"highYieldAddition":true}],"summary":["Pulse deficit separates electrical events from effective mechanical output.","Coordinated activation and recovery matter alongside the presence of individual action potentials.","A short diastolic interval first threatens filling/preload.","Timing plus P–QRS relation is more informative than rate alone.","Longer circuit travel relative to refractoriness can permit another cycle.","AF: disorganized atrial baseline; flutter: organized repetitive waves.","Pre-excitation links a bypass pathway to short PR/delta wave."],"sourceNotes":["The uploaded file is labeled P5 / ECG III and contains the Part-II arrhythmia transcription. Your preset course item remains Interactive Session I with its original dates.","General physiology and recognition explanations are study aids; transcript treatment anecdotes are not converted into self-treatment instructions."],"scopeExclusions":[{"text":"The laser/heat ablation aside is explicitly outside exam/question/assessment scope.","reference":{"source":"cvp-t-p5","page":18}}]}},"meta":{"sections":136,"guideCount":13}};
